window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"The works file and how to read it","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#legajo","x":"01 The works file and how to read it A job is not done “from memory” or “the way it has always been done”: it is done according to a set of documents, the works file, which is both the instruction for whoever builds and the commitment to whoever pays. How the design is calculated is explained in The industrial electrical project and the general method in Installation project; what matters here is what happens when those documents reach the site. Document What it contains What it is used for on site Design drawings Single-line diagram, raceway and equipment layout, switchboard diagrams, installation details and sections. Knowing what is installed and where. This is what gets set out. Technical specifications Materials and accepted makes, standards to meet, installation method, required tests and acceptance criteria. Knowing how to install and with what quality. This is what gets inspected. Calculation report Demand, cross-sections, voltage drop, short circuit, protection and its coordination, with the assumptions used. Understanding why a cross-section or setting is what it is, before changing it. Bill of quantities and budget Quantity of each material by item, and prices. Ordering and receiving materials; certifying progress. Schedules Load, cable (origin, destination, size, length), terminal and label schedules. Pulling, connecting and identifying without guessing. Catalogues and data sheets Dimensions, weights, curves and limits of each device, and the manufacturer’s instal"},{"t":"Setting out and work sequence","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#replanteo","x":"02 Setting out and work sequence Setting out is taking the drawing to the place: marking on the floor, walls and structure the centreline of each tray, the position of each switchboard, the penetrations and the drops to each machine. It is not copying the drawing at 1:1; it is the last check of the design against reality. While setting out you discover interferences —a compressed-air pipe where the tray was going, a column that is not on the drawing, a machine that was moved— and clearance heights, and you check that there is room to work and operate in front of the switchboards. Every deviation is noted and agreed with the inspector before mounting, because afterwards it has to be dismantled. The order of work is not arbitrary: whatever ends up buried or embedded is done first, because later it is costly to reach; supports and trays before cables, and switchboards before pulling, so each cable arrives at a terminal that is already in place. Stage What is done Checked before moving on 1 Setting out and stocking. Mark the job and receive materials. Materials against specification; interferences resolved. 2 Earthing and buried raceways. Electrode resistance; ducts clean and with a pull wire. 3 Supports and trays (or conduits), with joints and earth bonding jumpers. Alignment, fixing, continuity between sections, burr-free edges. 4 Mounting switchboards and the motor control centre. Levelling, fixing, cable entry with the specified IP rating. 5 Cable pulling in schedule order. B"},{"t":"Trays, pulling, switchboards and identification","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#montaje","x":"03 Trays, pulling, switchboards and identification Raceways The cable tray (perforated or ladder type, in galvanized steel, aluminium or fibreglass-reinforced plastic in corrosive environments) is the solution for many cables over long, accessible runs: it is wired quickly, everything is visible and it is easy to extend. Conduit (rigid metal or PVC) is preferred where there is risk of impact, moisture or dust, or where it must be embedded; trunking, in switchboards and short runs. In every case the supports are placed at the spacing given by the manufacturer for the load, for example every 1.5 m: N: number of supports · L: run length · s: support spacing · ⌈ ⌉: round up Metal trays are bonded to the earthing network and continuity between sections is ensured with copper joints and jumpers; a tray that floats between badly joined sections is not a protective conductor, it is a hazard. Power and control or signal cables are kept apart, in separate trays or with a divider, because power conductors induce noise in signal ones; where they must cross, they cross at 90°, and analogue and communication signal cables are shielded (see Variable speed drives ). Pulling conductors Pulling begins before touching the cable: the tray must be complete, burr-free, and the route checked against the schedule. Then: Rule Why The reel turns and the cable is paid out; it is never pulled off “the coil” in a spiral. A cable twisted during pulling keeps internal stresses and can damage the insulation"},{"t":"Underground lines, distribution and transformer stations","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#subterraneas","x":"04 Underground lines, distribution and transformer stations What an underground cable is like, which standard governs (AEA 95101 for underground lines, AEA 95401 for transformer stations) and why a line may or may not be buried is in Substations and distribution lines; how to locate a fault in a buried cable, in Faults and safety in distribution networks. Here what matters is the order of the work, which is the same in low and medium voltage, and which is watched more closely the higher the voltage: Step What is done 1 Checking for interferences. Drawings of the other services (water, gas, communications, other lines) are requested and hand-dug trial pits are made where crossings will occur. A medium-voltage cable pierced by a shovel or a backhoe is a serious accident. 2 Opening the trench with the width and depth set by the design, shored if the soil requires it, fenced and signposted for pedestrians and vehicles. 3 Levelled bottom and sand bed of fine, clean sand without stones, which supports the cable evenly and protects it from rubbing against the soil. 4 Pulling the cable from the reel, over rollers and without dragging it, respecting the manufacturer’s maximum tension and minimum bending radius. Each cable and phase is identified before being covered. 5 Sand over the cables and mechanical protection on top (slabs or bricks), which warns whoever digs later and spreads the blow of a shovel. 6 Warning tape some distance above the protection, and backfill compacted in laye"},{"t":"Manufacturer catalogues, motor selection and motor control centre","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#motores","x":"05 Manufacturer catalogues, motor selection and motor control centre The catalogue is the source of the data the design needs and the job has to verify. It is worth distinguishing the product’s data sheet (guaranteed values for that model), the family’s general catalogue (to compare and choose), and the installation manual (how to mount, tighten, connect and maintain it). From the data sheet the same things are always read: rated voltage and current, breaking or making capacity, degree of protection (IP), working temperature and derating factor for temperature or altitude, dimensions and weight (for the support and space), standards met and order code. What the sheet says is compared with what the specification asks, line by line. Reading a motor data sheet The motor nameplate is explained in AC motors; the catalogue adds the data used to choose and to verify starting. A typical sheet (values of an example three-phase motor): Catalogue item Example value What it is for Rated power (at the shaft) 11 kW It is what it delivers, not what it draws. Compared with what the load requires. Poles · speed 4 · 1,465 rpm Sets the load speed and, with the power, the torque. Voltage · connection 380 V · Δ (380/660 V Δ/Y) Which supply it connects to and how; conditions star-delta starting. Efficiency · IE class 91.4 % · IE3 How much energy is lost. Sets the cost of running the motor for years. Power factor (cos φ) 0.84 Enters the current and the compensation ( AC power ). Rated current I n 2"},{"t":"Installed and contracted power, lighting and rational use of energy","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#potencia","x":"06 Installed and contracted power, lighting and rational use of energy Installed power is the sum of the power of everything connected. Not all of it is drawn at once: maximum demand is what the installation really asks for at its busiest moment, and it is calculated by applying to each group of loads its demand factor (how it is determined, in The industrial electrical project ). Contracted power is what is agreed with the utility for the supply, and it defines how much fixed charge is paid and what equipment the service requires: if too little is contracted, real demand exceeds it and the tariff may penalize it; if too much, you pay for power that is never used. For an own transformer station, the transformer rating is chosen from the demand in kVA, with a reserve for growth. Example · From installed to contracted power and to the transformer A workshop has four motors in the MCC (5.5 + 7.5 + 11 + 15 = 39 kW, nameplate power), 6 kW of lighting, 8 kW of sockets and services, and 10 kW of air conditioning: 63 kW installed. With demand factors of 0.65 for the motors, 0.9 for lighting, 0.4 for sockets and 0.8 for air conditioning: Group Installed Demand factor Demand MCC motors 39 kW 0.65 25.35 kW Lighting 6 kW 0.90 5.40 kW Sockets and services 8 kW 0.40 3.20 kW Air conditioning 10 kW 0.80 8.00 kW Total 63 kW 41.95 kW Demand is about 42 kW: that is the order of the power to contract, not the 63 kW installed. In kVA, with an average power factor of 0.85: A standard 63 kVA transf"},{"t":"Acceptance tests, commissioning and as-built drawings","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#recepcion","x":"07 Acceptance tests, commissioning and as-built drawings A job is not finished when the last cable is connected, but when measurements have shown that it works and is safe, and when whoever will maintain it receives the documentation of what was actually built. Acceptance tests verify the whole installation; those done on each machine (no-load, load, temperature rise) are in Motor and transformer testing, and the method and instruments for earthing and insulation, in Earthing, insulation and generator testing. Test What is verified and with what Reference criterion Visual and mechanical inspection Installation against drawing: cross-sections, labels, tightening, supports, IP ratings, burr-free edges. No open non-conformities. Continuity of protective conductor and trays Low-resistance ohmmeter between each exposed part and the originating switchboard. Continuity at every point; values recorded. Insulation resistance 500 V megohmmeter between conductors and earth, loads disconnected. Reference: at least 1 MΩ on circuits up to 500 V (IEC 60364-6, the basis of part 6 of AEA 90364). Earthing Earth tester on the electrode and the network. Less than or equal to the design value. Phase sequence and direction of rotation Phase sequence meter at the board; motor rotation uncoupled from the load. Phase order equal to the whole system; correct rotation. Protection Thermal relay and motor breaker settings at nameplate current; RCD trip tested with an instrument. Values from the calculati"},{"t":"On site","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#obra","x":"08 On site Exercise 1 · Read a works file and find its errors Materials: a short file (a raceway drawing with single-line diagram, its cable schedule and a two-page specification), prepared by the teacher with three deliberate inconsistencies (a different cross-section between drawing and schedule, a label that does not exist in the diagram, a make without “or equivalent”). Steps: 1) note the current revision of each drawing; 2) build the list of materials and quantities; 3) cross-check drawing, schedule and specification; 4) write a query note for each doubt. Acceptance criterion: the three inconsistencies are detected and each note states which document contradicts which and what is being asked. Exercise 2 · Set out and mount a tray section Materials: 8 m of perforated tray with supports, joints, copper jumpers, level, tape measure, drill and a low-resistance ohmmeter. Steps: 1) set out the centreline with chalk or string; 2) mount the supports at the spacing given by the catalogue; 3) join the sections with their jumpers; 4) measure continuity between the two ends. Measured: support spacing, levelness and resistance between ends. Acceptance criterion: spacing no larger than the catalogue’s, tray without visible slope or twist, burr-free edges and continuity measured and recorded at every joint. Exercise 3 · Pull and connect a motor circuit Materials: a training switchboard with motor breaker and contactor, 10 m of 4 × 1.5 mm² cable, a small three-phase motor, ferrules, lab"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Installing from a drawing of an earlier revision Rework and lost materials; usually discovered when it is already installed. Settling a contradiction between drawings “by eye” If it turns out wrong, the responsibility lies with whoever decided without asking. Measuring on the printed drawing instead of using the dimensions Cables too short or too long: the paper may be reduced or distorted. Bending the cable tighter than the manufacturer’s radius Internal insulation damage that shows up as a fault months later. Pulling without leaving slack or labelling It cannot be re-terminated and the cable is lost among identical ones. Stacking cables in several layers in the tray Poor heat dissipation: the current rating drops and the cable overheats. Covering a trench without recording the route The next person to dig breaks it; the fault cannot be located. Starting under load a motor chosen only by power Starting torque is not enough (for example with star-delta) and the motor does not accelerate. Contracting installed power instead of demand You pay for power that is never used, and the transformer ends up oversized. Leaving as-built drawings for the end They are made from memory, with errors; the job is handed over with drawings that do not match reality."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Execution of the electrical works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ejecucion-obra#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Which documents make up the works file and what is looked up in each? Drawings (what and where), specifications (how and with what quality), calculation report (why), bill of quantities (how much), schedules (cables, terminals, labels), catalogues and standards (limits and installation rules). If they contradict each other, ask in writing; do not decide by your own judgement. Why are earthing and buried raceways done first, and switchboards before pulling? Because buried or embedded work is costly to reach afterwards, and because each cable has to arrive at a terminal that is already in place. The order avoids rework. A cable with an outer diameter of 18 mm has a minimum bending radius of 12 times its diameter. How much is it? 12 · 18 mm = 216 mm. It is the cable’s minimum radius; the tray bend is chosen with an equal or larger radius. Four cables of 12, 14, 16 and 20 mm go in a single layer in a tray. What minimum usable width is needed with a 30 % reserve? The sum is 62 mm and with the reserve, 1.3 · 62 = 80.6 mm. The next commercial width is 100 mm. How is the rated current of a 7.5 kW, 380 V motor with efficiency 0.90 and cos φ 0.85 calculated? I = 7,500 / (√3 · 380 · 0.90 · 0.85) = 7,500 / 503.5 = 14.9 A. Why can a motor be right for steady running and still fail to start the load? Because in steady running only power matters, but when starting the motor torque must exceed the load torque at every speed. With star-delta the torque drops to a third: fin"},{"t":"Faraday’s law","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#faraday","x":"01 Faraday’s law In 1831 Michael Faraday ran the decisive experiment: he brought a magnet close to a coil connected to a galvanometer. While the magnet was moving, the needle deflected. With the magnet at rest — even inside the coil — the needle read zero. The key word is CHANGE A magnetic field does not generate voltage. A magnetic field that changes does. You can achieve this by moving the magnet, moving the coil, changing the current that produces the field, or rotating the loop inside a fixed field. That last one is what an alternator does. Induced electromotive force (EMF), in volts. N = number of turns, ΔΦ = change in flux in webers, Δt = time in seconds. The minus sign is Lenz’s law. Example A coil of 500 turns sees its flux change from 0 to 4 mWb in 20 ms: If the same change took place in 1 ms, the voltage would be 2000 V. That dependence on the speed of the change is what explains the destructive spikes when inductive circuits are opened."},{"t":"Lenz’s law","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#lenz","x":"02 Lenz’s law It explains the minus sign: the induced current always flows in the direction that opposes the cause that produces it. It is not arbitrary: it is conservation of energy If the induced current helped the motion instead of opposing it, the magnet would accelerate on its own and the coil would generate energy that nobody supplies: it would be a perpetual motion machine. Lenz’s law guarantees that the electrical energy generated is supplied by whoever moves the magnet. That is exactly why a generator is harder to turn when a load is connected to it. The nicest demonstration: the magnet in the copper tube A neodymium magnet is dropped inside a copper or aluminum tube ( non -magnetic materials). Instead of falling freely, it descends very slowly, as if floating. The changing field induces circular currents in the tube (eddy currents) that, by Lenz’s law, create a field that opposes the motion. This is the principle of the magnetic brake on trucks and trains, and of the damping of the galvanometer seen in analog instruments. EMF from a moving conductor A conductor of length L moving at speed v perpendicular to a field B. This is the principle of the generator: if instead of a conductor there is a loop that rotates, the EMF turns out sinusoidal, which is why the grid is AC."},{"t":"Self-induction","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#autoinduccion","x":"03 Self-induction A coil also induces voltage in itself: if the current flowing through it changes, its own flux changes, and that generates an EMF that — by Lenz’s law — opposes the change in current. L is the inductance, in henries (H). It depends on the square of the number of turns: doubling the turns quadruples the inductance. Capacitor Inductor Opposes changes in voltage Opposes changes in current Stores energy in the electric field Stores energy in the magnetic field W = ½·C·V² W = ½·L·I² In DC it is an open circuit In DC it is a short circuit (only its ohmic resistance) Its voltage cannot change instantly Its current cannot change instantly τ = R·C τ = L/R The breaking extra-current (inductive kickback) The most important practical consequence of the whole topic. When you open a circuit containing an inductor, the current tries to drop to zero in a very short time. Since, as Δt tends to zero the induced voltage shoots up to hundreds or thousands of volts, no matter that the circuit runs at 12 V. That overvoltage: Destroys the transistor or the IC that did the switching. Burns the contacts of a relay or a switch, through the electric arc. It is put to use on purpose in the ignition coil of an internal combustion engine, where 12 V becomes 20,000 V for the spark plug. It is controlled with a freewheeling diode in antiparallel with the inductor (in DC) or with an RC snubber network (in AC). It is not an optional accessory. The RL circuit The current grows exponentially, "},{"t":"Mutual induction: the transformer","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#transformador","x":"04 Mutual induction: the transformer If two coils share the same core, a change in current in one induces voltage in the other. That is mutual induction, and its application is the transformer. Transformation ratio. Voltages are proportional to the turns and currents are inversely proportional. In an ideal transformer: power is conserved. Worked example A 220 V to 12 V transformer with 1100 turns on the primary, supplying a 2 A load. Ratio: Secondary turns: turns Primary current: Power:; ✓ Design consequence: the secondary carries 18 times more current, so its wire must be much thicker than the primary’s. It is the quickest way to tell which winding is which in an unknown transformer: the one with thin wire and many turns is the high-voltage one. How that coil behaves with alternating current — and why its reactance grows with frequency — can be seen live in the vector study of AC. A transformer does not work with DC Without a change in flux there is no induction: with DC, the secondary delivers zero volts and the primary behaves like a plain low-resistance winding, and burns out. The only exception is switching power supplies, which chop the DC into pulses of tens of kHz — and precisely because the frequency is high, the transformer can be tiny."},{"t":"Hysteresis and iron losses","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#histeresis","x":"05 Hysteresis and iron losses When a ferromagnetic material is magnetized and demagnetized repeatedly, the B-H curve does not retrace its path: it describes a closed loop called the hysteresis loop. Soft materials Narrow loop: low remanence and low coercive field. They magnetize and demagnetize easily, so they lose little energy per cycle. Soft iron, silicon-iron sheet, soft ferrite, permalloy. Used in transformer, motor and relay cores. Hard materials Wide loop: high remanence and high coercive field. Once magnetized, they are hard to demagnetize. Alnico, hard ferrite, neodymium-iron-boron, samarium-cobalt. Used to make permanent magnets. The two iron losses Loss Cause How it is reduced Hysteresis loss Energy spent re-orienting the domains in each cycle. Proportional to the loop area and to the frequency. Using magnetically soft material (silicon-iron, ferrite). Eddy-current loss The core is a conductor: the changing flux induces circular currents in its own mass, which heat it. Laminating the core into thin sheets insulated from one another, perpendicular to those currents. At high frequency ferrite is used, which is an insulator. These two, added to the copper losses ( in the windings), are what keep a real transformer from being 100% efficient. A small transformer is around 80%; a distribution transformer, more than 98%. Why the sheets are laminated and insulated This is the classic exam question. If the core were a solid piece, eddy currents would flow freely through its"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Faraday and Lenz with a galvanometer A coil of several hundred turns connected to a center-zero galvanometer. Bring a magnet close: the needle deflects to one side. Move it away: it deflects to the other. Leave it still inside: zero. Repeat moving it faster and check that the deflection increases. Flip the magnet and verify that all the directions reverse. Lab 2 · The magnet in the tube Simultaneously drop a neodymium magnet and a screw of equal weight into two identical copper or aluminum tubes. The screw falls immediately; the magnet takes several seconds. Repeat with a PVC tube: both fall the same. It is the most convincing demonstration of Lenz’s law and of eddy currents. Lab 3 · Seeing the breaking extra-current The coil of a 12 V relay powered through a switch, with the oscilloscope connected in parallel (×10 probe, DC coupling, fast timebase and trigger in SINGLE mode on a falling edge). When you open the switch, a negative spike of several tens or hundreds of volts appears. Then fit the freewheeling diode and repeat: the spike is limited to −0.7 V. This is the lab that justifies once and for all the diode in every circuit with a relay. Lab 4 · Transformation ratio On a 220/12 V transformer with a center tap: measure with the voltmeter the voltages of each winding at no load and verify the ratio. Then connect a load (a 12 V lamp) and measure the two currents: check that they are in inverse ratio to the voltages and that the powers are similar. The"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom or confusion Clarification “A magnet at rest inside the coil generates voltage” No. A change in flux is needed. With the magnet at rest, ΔΦ = 0 and e = 0. The transistor burns out when a relay is switched off Breaking extra-current. The freewheeling diode is missing. The transformer hums and heats up with no load Excessive primary voltage (saturated core), loose laminations, or it was connected to a voltage lower than the design value at the wrong frequency. A transformer was connected to DC and burned out Without a change in flux there is no induced EMF to oppose the current: the primary is left with only its ohmic resistance, which is very low. Faraday’s law and Lenz’s law get mixed up Faraday gives the magnitude of the induced voltage; Lenz gives its direction (always opposing the cause). The solid core gets extremely hot Eddy currents. The core must be made of thin sheets insulated from one another, or of ferrite at high frequency. L is calculated and gives an absurd value when the turns are doubled L depends on N², not on N: doubling the turns quadruples the inductance."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electrodynamics and induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What condition is essential for an EMF to be induced? That the magnetic flux linked by the loop varies in time. It can vary because the magnet moves, the coil moves, the loop rotates, or the current that creates the field changes. A coil of 200 turns sees its flux change from 3 mWb to 1 mWb in 10 ms. What EMF is induced?. The magnitude is 40 V; the sign indicates that the induced current will try to sustain the flux that is disappearing. State Lenz’s law and say what principle it guarantees. The induced current flows in the direction such that it opposes the cause that produces it. It guarantees the conservation of energy: if it were the other way around, the system would accelerate on its own and generate energy from nothing. Why does a magnet fall slowly inside a copper tube if copper is not magnetic? Because the changing field induces eddy currents in the wall of the tube, and those currents create a field that, by Lenz’s law, opposes the motion. The material does not need to be magnetic: being a good conductor is enough. Why is it dangerous to open a circuit containing an unprotected inductor? Because: when the current is cut in a very short time, the induced voltage shoots up to hundreds or thousands of volts. That punctures semiconductors and produces arcing at the contacts. It is avoided with a freewheeling diode in DC or an RC snubber in AC. A transformer with,, supplied at 220 V with 3 A in the secondary. What are V₂ and I₁? Ratio... Power check: a"},{"t":"Electric charge","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#carga","x":"01 Electric charge Charge is a property of matter, like mass. It comes in two signs and obeys three principles: There are two kinds. Positive (proton) and negative (electron). Like charges repel, unlike charges attract. Charge is conserved. It is neither created nor destroyed: it is only transferred from one body to another. When something becomes negatively charged, something else is left positive. Charge is quantized. Every value of charge is an integer multiple of the elementary charge. There are no half charges. One coulomb is an enormous quantity: it equals electrons. That is why the charges in electrostatics are measured in microcoulombs or nanocoulombs. Ways to charge a body By friction Two different materials are rubbed together and one pulls electrons from the other. This is the mechanism behind the ruler that attracts bits of paper and the spark when you get out of the car. By contact A charged body touches another and transfers charge to it. Both end up with charge of the same sign. By induction A charged body is brought near without touching and redistributes the charges of the other. If the other body is connected to ground at that moment and then the charged body is removed, it is left charged with the opposite sign."},{"t":"Coulomb's law","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#coulomb","x":"02 Coulomb's law The force between two point charges is proportional to the product of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance. in vacuum. The force is attractive if the charges have different signs and repulsive if they have the same sign. Comparison with gravity Coulomb's law has the same form as the law of universal gravitation, but it is incomparably stronger: between two electrons, the electric repulsion is about 10 42 times greater than the gravitational attraction. If matter were not almost perfectly neutral, electric forces would dominate everything. Worked example Two charges of +2 µC and −3 µC separated by 5 cm. Calculate the force. An attractive force (opposite signs) of 21.6 N: equivalent to the weight of more than 2 kg, between two charges of a few microcoulombs. And if the distance were doubled, the force would fall to one quarter (5.4 N), not to one half: that is what “inversely proportional to the square” means."},{"t":"Electric field","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#campo","x":"03 Electric field Instead of thinking of forces “at a distance”, it is better to say that a charge modifies the space around it. That modification is the electric field, and another charge placed there feels a force. Above, the definition: force per unit of test charge. Below, the field produced by a point charge."},{"t":"Potential and potential difference","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#potencial","x":"04 Potential and potential difference Moving a charge within an electric field costs (or delivers) work. The potential at a point is that work per unit of charge. What is measured in practice is not the potential of a point but the potential difference between two points, which is what we call voltage. Speaking of the “potential of a point” implies choosing an arbitrary reference: in a circuit, the ground; in an installation, the earth. Equipotential surfaces These are the places where the potential is the same. Moving a charge along an equipotential costs no work. Equipotentials are always perpendicular to the field lines. A conductor in equilibrium is entirely an equipotential: that is why the metal case of a piece of equipment, if properly connected, has the same potential at all its points. Example · Field between two plates Two parallel plates 2 mm apart with 12 V between them:. With the same voltage but a 0.1 mm separation (the typical dielectric thickness of a ceramic capacitor), the field rises to 120 kV/m. That is why a low-voltage capacitor breaks down if more than its rated voltage is applied: it is not the voltage itself, it is the field inside the dielectric."},{"t":"Capacitance and the capacitor","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#capacitancia","x":"05 Capacitance and the capacitor A capacitor is two conductors facing each other, separated by an insulator. When voltage is applied, one plate accumulates positive charge and the other the same amount of negative charge. The capacitance measures how much charge it stores per volt applied. (permittivity of free space), ε r = relative permittivity of the dielectric, A = facing area, d = separation. The farad is an enormous unit: common capacitors range from picofarads to millifarads. The formula gives the three ways to increase capacitance: More area. That is why electrolytics are two long strips rolled up. Less separation. Limited by the voltage it has to withstand. A better dielectric (higher ε r ). Dielectrics Material ε r Dielectric strength (kV/mm) Use Vacuum / air 1.0 3 Variable tuning capacitors Paper 3.5 16 Motor-start capacitors Polyester (Mylar) 3.2 300 General use in electronics Polypropylene 2.2 400 Audio, high frequency, low losses Mica 5.4 100 High stability, radio frequency Ceramic NP0/C0G 30 — Oscillators: very stable with temperature Ceramic X7R / Z5U 1000 to 10000 — Decoupling: high capacitance, poor stability Aluminum oxide (electrolytic) 8 700 Filtering. The oxide layer is nanometers thick: hence the enormous capacitance Dielectric strength and working voltage Every insulator breaks down if the field exceeds a certain value. That limit is the dielectric strength, and from it comes the working voltage printed on the capacitor. Exceeding it destroys it, somet"},{"t":"Capacitors in series and in parallel","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#asociacion","x":"06 Capacitors in series and in parallel They behave the opposite way from resistors, and that reversal is the cause of half the errors in the exercises. In parallel · they ADD It is like increasing the area of the plates. All of them have the same voltage and the working voltage of the assembly is that of the lowest. In series · reciprocals It is like increasing the separation. All of them have the same charge and the voltages add: that is how you get to withstand more voltage. Example · Two 470 µ F / 200 V capacitors in series and the allowable voltage becomes 400 V. This is what is done in the power supplies of large audio equipment. Careful: in practice you have to add equalizing resistors (100 kΩ) in parallel with each capacitor, because the leakage currents differ and, without them, the voltage is shared poorly and one of the two ends up withstanding almost all of it."},{"t":"Energy and the RC circuit","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#rc","x":"07 Energy and the RC circuit Stored energy, in joules. It depends on the square of the voltage: doubling the voltage quadruples the energy. A capacitor does not charge instantaneously: if it is connected to a source through a resistor, the charge follows an exponential curve governed by the time constant. With R in ohms and C in farads, τ comes out in seconds. A useful shortcut:. This behavior is the basis of a lot of circuits seen later: the power supply filter, timers with the 555, debounce circuits and low-pass filters. ⏱️ LM555 simulator Shows live the charging and discharging of the capacitor through the resistors, with the animated charge level and the timing calculation on screen. ›"},{"t":"Electrostatics in the shop: ESD","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#esd","x":"08 Electrostatics in the shop: ESD This is not an anecdotal subject: electrostatic discharge (ESD) is one of the main causes of component destruction in the electronics industry. Situation Typical voltage generated Walking on a carpet (dry air) up to 35,000 V Walking on a vinyl floor up to 12,000 V Getting up from a plastic chair up to 18,000 V Opening an ordinary plastic bag up to 20,000 V Minimum a person can feel ≈ 3,000 V Enough to damage a CMOS device ≈ 100 V The conclusion that matters You can destroy an integrated circuit with a discharge that the person does not even notice. And often the damage is latent: the component keeps working, but fails weeks later. That is why you work with an antistatic wrist strap grounded through 1 MΩ (the resistor protects the person), a dissipative mat, and by keeping CMOS ICs in conductive foam or a metallized bag. It is especially critical with the 40xx series and with microcontrollers."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#laboratorio","x":"09 In the lab Lab 1 · Charging and induction With a leaf electroscope: (a) rub a plastic rod with wool and bring it near without touching — the leaves separate by induction and come back together when it is moved away; (b) touch the electroscope with the rod — it charges by contact and the leaves stay apart; (c) touch the electroscope with a finger to discharge it to ground. Note the sign of the charge in each case. Lab 2 · Measuring capacitance and checking the combinations With a multimeter that has a capacitance function, measure three different polyester capacitors and compare them with their printed code (checking whether they are within tolerance). Then connect them in parallel (measure: it should give the sum) and in series (measure: it should give less than the smallest). It is the exercise that fixes the reversal with respect to resistors. Lab 3 · Time constant, (, slow on purpose so you can see it with the multimeter). Power it with 9 V and note the capacitor voltage every 2 seconds up to 50 s. Plot it and verify that at 10 s the voltage is 63 % of 9 V, that is 5.67 V. Repeat for the discharge by shorting the source and check that the curve is the mirror image. Lab 4 · Stored energy Charge a 4700 µF electrolytic to 12 V and calculate the energy:. Discharge it through a 12 V lamp and watch the flash. Never discharge an electrolytic by shorting it with a screwdriver: the peak current is tens of amperes and it can throw out molten metal. Safety with capacitors A large "},{"t":"Common errors","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#errores","x":"10 Common errors Symptom Usual cause The electrolytic swelled or exploded Reversed polarity, or working voltage exceeded. Remember that the peak of an AC voltage is 1.41 times its RMS value. The series capacitor calculation gives more than the individual ones The resistor formula was applied. For capacitors it is the other way around. The measured capacitance is much lower than the printed one Old, dried-out electrolytic. It is the most common failure in equipment more than ten years old. A “new” CMOS IC does not work ESD damage during handling. It may have been invisible. The RC circuit takes much longer than calculated Units: R in kΩ with C in µF gives milliseconds, not seconds. A tantalum capacitor caught fire It was mounted backwards. Tantalum capacitors mark the positive terminal with the band, the opposite of aluminum electrolytics, which mark the negative."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electrostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica#autoevaluacion","x":"11 Self-assessment If the distance between two charges is doubled, what happens to the force? It is reduced to one quarter. Coulomb's law is inversely proportional to the square of the distance. What is the difference between electric field and electric potential? The field is force per unit of charge (a vector quantity, in N/C or V/m); the potential is energy per unit of charge (a scalar quantity, in volts). In a uniform field they are related by. A 100 µF capacitor charged to 12 V: how much charge does it store and how much energy?.. How do you increase the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor? By increasing the facing area, reducing the separation or using a dielectric with a higher relative permittivity. The trade-off of reducing the separation is that the working voltage goes down. Three 30 µF capacitors: what is the total capacitance in series and in parallel? In parallel:. In series:. With n equal capacitors in series, the total is the value divided by n. What does the code “223” on a ceramic capacitor mean? 22 followed by 3 zeros, in picofarads:. and. How long does it take to charge practically fully?. It is considered charged at 5τ, that is 2.35 seconds. Why does the antistatic wrist strap include a 1 MΩ resistor? To protect the person. The resistor lets the static charge drain away (which carries very little energy) but limits the current to a safe value if by accident a point at mains voltage is touched. A wrist strap connected directly to ground would be dang"},{"t":"Energy, power and efficiency","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#energia","x":"01 Energy, power and efficiency Energy is the capacity to do work or produce heat. It appears in several forms —chemical, thermal, mechanical, electrical, radiant, nuclear— and changes from one to another, but it is neither created nor destroyed: it is transformed. Electricity is almost never the origin of energy: it is the carrier that takes it from a primary source (a fuel, reservoir water, wind, the Sun) to an end use (light, motion, heat, information). Quantity Symbol Unit (SI) Other common units What it expresses Energy E joule (J) kWh (1 kWh = 3.6 MJ), kcal (1 kWh ≈ 860 kcal), toe (≈ 11.6 MWh) What is stored, spent or transformed. Power P watt (W = J/s) kW, MW, HP (≈ 746 W) How fast energy is transformed. Efficiency η dimensionless (given in %) — What fraction of the input is put to use. What is “lost” does not disappear Efficiency is always below 1 because in every transformation part of the energy ends up as dispersed heat that can no longer be used: friction, Joule heating, exhaust gases. It was not destroyed; it was degraded to a useless form. When several transformations are chained, the efficiencies are multiplied, not added: kW is not kWh The kW measures power (how much energy per second); the kWh measures energy. A 2 kW heater on for half an hour uses 1 kWh. Writing “kWh per hour” or “kW consumed in the month” is a conceptual error. The electricity bill charges energy in kWh and, for large customers, also the contracted power in kW. Example · A motor delivering "},{"t":"Thermal energy","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#termica","x":"02 Thermal energy Heat is energy in transit between two bodies at different temperatures; temperature measures the thermal state of a body, not the amount of energy it holds. Heat flows by conduction (through a solid), convection (carried by a fluid) and radiation (waves, no medium needed). Engineering uses the kelvin (K); a difference of 1 K equals one of 1 °C. To heat a body without a change of state, the heat required is proportional to the mass and to the temperature rise: The specific heat c of water is 4.18 kJ/(kg·K), one of the highest there is; that is why water is the working and cooling fluid par excellence. When the state changes, heat is absorbed without any rise in temperature: vaporizing 1 kg of water at 100 °C takes about 2.26 MJ (latent heat), which is the energy the steam gives back when it condenses. From heat to electricity, and back Turning electricity into heat is easy and almost perfect: a resistor does it with an efficiency close to 1 (Joule heating, Electric current ). Turning heat into work is hard: no heat engine can convert all the heat it receives into work, and the theoretical maximum depends only on the temperatures of the hot and cold sources (in kelvin): A steam plant with a boiler at 540 °C (813 K) and a condenser at 30 °C (303 K) cannot exceed: In practice they reach about 40 %. The rest is delivered to the condenser, which is cooled with river or lake water, or with cooling towers. Fuels are compared by their calorific value: the thermal ene"},{"t":"Energy sources and their uses","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#fuentes","x":"03 Energy sources and their uses A source is primary if it is taken from nature as it is (oil, gas, uranium, river water, wind, solar radiation) and secondary if it results from transforming another: gasoline and electricity are secondary. Sources are also classified as non-renewable (depleted on a human time scale) and renewable (naturally replenished). Conventional sources: oil and gas They are organic remains buried for millions of years in sedimentary basins, which heat and pressure turned into hydrocarbons and which became trapped in porous rock. They are extracted through wells. Oil travels by pipeline and is separated at a refinery into gasoline, diesel, fuel oil, LPG and other products. Natural gas, mostly methane, travels through gas pipelines or, liquefied at about −160 °C (LNG), on ships. Conventional gas sits in permeable reservoirs and flows by itself; unconventional gas is held in very low-permeability rock ( shale, tight ) and requires hydraulic fracturing, as in the Vaca Muerta formation in the Neuquén basin. In Argentina, oil and gas supply the great majority of primary energy —on the order of three quarters or more—, with producing basins such as the Neuquina, Golfo San Jorge, Austral and Noroeste. In electricity generation, most comes from thermal plants burning natural gas; hydro comes next, with nuclear and non-hydro renewables making up the rest. These are orders of magnitude: the share changes every year and the exact figure is found in CAMMESA reports."},{"t":"From source to electricity: generation","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#generacion","x":"04 From source to electricity: generation Almost all the world’s electricity is produced with the same principle: electromagnetic induction ( Magnetism and electromagnetism ). An alternator spins an electromagnet inside a winding and an alternating voltage is induced in it. What changes from plant to plant is the prime mover that spins it: a turbine driven by steam, gas, water or wind, or a combustion engine. The exceptions are photovoltaic panels, which turn light directly into direct current and have no moving parts. Plant Primary energy Prime mover Approximate efficiency Steam thermal Fuel oil, gas, coal Boiler and steam turbine 35 to 40 % Gas turbine Natural gas, diesel Gas turbine 30 to 40 % Combined cycle Natural gas Gas turbine + heat-recovery boiler + steam turbine 50 to 60 % Nuclear Uranium Reactor, steam generator and turbine 30 to 35 % Hydro Potential energy of water Pelton, Francis or Kaplan turbine 80 to 90 % Wind Kinetic energy of the wind Rotor and generator (Betz limit: 59 %) 35 to 45 % Solar photovoltaic Solar radiation None: photovoltaic effect 15 to 22 % Generator set Diesel, gasoline or gas Internal combustion engine 30 to 40 % Generator sets, emergency generation and each turbine type are covered in Generation and generator sets. Why the system runs at 50 Hz The frequency of the voltage a synchronous alternator delivers is tied to its rotational speed and to its number of pole pairs p: Pole pairs p Speed at 50 Hz Typical of 1 3,000 rpm Steam and gas turbo"},{"t":"Transmission and transformation: why high voltage","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#transporte","x":"05 Transmission and transformation: why high voltage The big plants are where the resource is —hydro in Comahue, in the littoral and in the sierras; thermal near the gas pipelines— and the loads are in the cities, hundreds of kilometres away. The power has to be transmitted, and every conductor has resistance: part of the energy turns into heat along the way. For a three-phase line, the transmitted power and the Joule loss are: Solving for the current: for the same power P, raising the voltage V lowers the current I in the same proportion, and the losses, which depend on I 2, drop with the square of the voltage. Raising the voltage 4 times cuts losses 16 times. That is the reason for high-voltage transmission. Example · A 3 Ω-per-phase line carrying 60 MW A three-phase line has 3 Ω of resistance per conductor and carries 60 MW at power factor 1 (deliberately simplified to see the effect of voltage). It is calculated with a line voltage of 132 kV: Repeated at the other two voltages: Line voltage Current per conductor Line losses Fraction of the 60 MW 33 kV 1,050 A 9.9 MW 16.5 % 132 kV 262 A 0.62 MW 1.03 % 500 kV 69.3 A 0.043 MW 0.072 % Going from 33 to 132 kV cuts losses 16 times, just as the square of the voltage ratio predicts. At 33 kV the 1,050 A current would need enormous conductors, and even so a sixth of what is transmitted would be lost. What makes changing the voltage possible In alternating current, the transformer raises or lowers the voltage with an efficiency abo"},{"t":"From the interconnected system to the socket: distribution grids","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#redes","x":"06 From the interconnected system to the socket: distribution grids The SADI (Argentine Interconnection System) joins the country’s plants and loads into a single grid running at 50 Hz. Connecting everything has two advantages: any plant can supply any region, and mutual backup means a local failure does not leave everyone without service. CAMMESA (the company that administers the wholesale electricity market) coordinates plant dispatch and administers the wholesale market. The activity is governed by Law 24.065 and overseen by bodies such as the ENRE nationally and, in Córdoba, the ERSeP for provincial distribution (EPEC and the cooperatives). Level Typical voltage Role Generation on the order of 10 to 20 kV Alternator voltage; depends on each machine. Trunk transmission 500 kV Large blocks of power over long distances between regions (SADI). Regional transmission 220 kV and 132 kV Carry energy to provinces and large cities; feed substations. Medium-voltage distribution 33 kV and 13.2 kV Run through towns and neighbourhoods; feed industries and transformer stations. Low-voltage distribution 380 V between phases (220 V phase-neutral) Deliver to the end user; three-phase system with neutral, at 50 Hz. Voltages are grouped, according to regulations, into low, medium, high and extra-high voltage; the exact limits of each category are set by the standards applicable in each case. The points where the level changes are the substations (ET) and, at the last step, the distribution t"},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#laboratorio","x":"07 In the laboratory Practice 1 · Measure the efficiency of an electric kettle Materials: electric kettle (or heating jug) of known power, plug-in energy meter (wattmeter), thermometer, measuring cylinder or scale, stopwatch. Steps: 1) Put in 1 litre of water (1 kg) and measure the initial temperature. 2) Plug the energy meter and the kettle in, and reset the kWh counter. 3) Heat to about 80 °C and disconnect. 4) Note the final temperature, the energy recorded and the time. 5) Calculate the useful heat Q = m · c ·ΔT and the efficiency as Q divided by the measured energy. Repeat and average. What is measured: temperatures, energy in kWh (or Wh), time, power. Acceptance criterion: the efficiency comes out between roughly 80 and 95 %, never above 100 %, and two consecutive tests differ by less than 5 %. If it comes out above 100 %, there is a measurement or unit error. Practice on the 220 V mains Practices 1 and 3 are done with mains voltage. Use the laboratory’s meter and safety leads, without opening any equipment or touching live metal parts, with the board protected by a residual-current device and a circuit breaker, and always with the teacher present. Hot water also burns: do not lift the kettle while it is connected. Practice 2 · Energy audit of a classroom Materials: worksheet, the nameplate data of the appliances, a plug-in wattmeter if available. Steps: 1) List every appliance in the classroom (luminaires, computers, projector, fan, heater) with its nameplate power. 2)"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Confusing kW with kWh Power and energy get mixed up; consumption and cost calculations give meaningless results. Adding the efficiencies of a chain instead of multiplying them The total efficiency is overestimated: 0.90 and 0.40 give 0.36, not 1.30 or 0.65. Using °C in the Carnot formula Absurd results (even negative ones). Carnot temperatures are always in kelvin. Believing that “consuming energy” means destroying it You lose sight of where the unused part goes: it is heat, and that heat has to be removed. Thinking that if the voltage rises the current rises For the same power it is the opposite: I = P /(√3· V ·cos φ). Raising the voltage lowers the current. Forgetting the 3 for the three phases in the losses of a three-phase line Total losses are underestimated threefold: they are 3· R · I 2, not R · I 2. Mixing line voltage with phase voltage With 380 V between phases there are 220 V between phase and neutral (380/√3); using one for the other multiplies or divides the results. Calling every renewable source “clean” or every fossil source “dirty” without looking at the full cycle Sources are compared with incomplete arguments; impact, efficiency, cost and availability all have to be considered. Using a calorific value without checking the units (kcal/m³ or kWh/m³) An error of 860 times: 1 kWh equals about 860 kcal."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electrical energy: where it comes from and how it arrives","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/energia-electrica#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the difference between power and energy? A 2 kW heater runs for 3 hours: how many kWh does it use? Power is energy per unit of time (kW); energy is the total consumed (kWh). It is calculated E = P · t: A motor delivers 4 kW at the shaft with an efficiency of 0.80. What power does it draw from the grid and how much does it lose? It draws 5 kW and loses 1 kW as heat. How much energy is needed to heat 50 kg of water from 20 °C to 60 °C? Apply Q = m · c ·ΔT with c = 4.18 kJ/(kg·K) and ΔT = 40 K, and convert to kWh by dividing by 3,600: With an efficiency below 1, the energy actually paid for is higher. Why does a steam plant not exceed 40 % efficiency? Because of the second law of thermodynamics: a heat engine cannot convert all the heat into work, and the maximum (Carnot efficiency) depends on the temperatures of the hot and cold sources. With 540 °C and 30 °C the limit is 63 %; real losses bring it down to 40 %. The rest is delivered to the condenser. Why is power transmitted at high voltage? If it goes from 132 to 500 kV, how do the current and the losses change? For the same power, a higher voltage means a lower current, and losses depend on the square of the current. The current is divided by 3.79 and the losses by about 14: A 4-pole alternator (p = 2) is connected to a 50 Hz grid. At what speed does it turn? It turns at synchronous speed, 1,500 rpm: What is the SADI, who coordinates dispatch, and why is the growth of solar and wind a challenge? Th"},{"t":"Where a radio link is used","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#que-es","x":"01 Where a radio link is used Replacing cable Joining two buildings, a plant with its office, a town with the fiber node. It is installed in days and does not depend on right-of-way permits. Backhaul Carrying the traffic of a cell tower to the core network, or connecting radio and TV repeaters. Rural areas It is the realistic way to bring internet to a farm or a remote settlement: a chain of links between hills, with low-cost equipment in license-exempt bands. Band Typical range Characteristics 2.4 GHz (unlicensed) 10 to 30 km Very congested in cities. Large antennas for the same gain. 5 GHz (unlicensed) 10 to 50 km The usual choice for low-cost links: less crowded and good small antennas. 7 to 15 GHz (licensed) 10 to 60 km Professional links with interference protection. 18 to 38 GHz 1 to 10 km A lot of capacity, short hops: rain becomes the dominant factor. 60 to 80 GHz < 2 km Gigabit capacity. Oxygen in the air absorbs strongly at 60 GHz."},{"t":"Line of sight and the Fresnel zone","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#fresnel","x":"02 Line of sight and the Fresnel zone Seeing the other end with your eyes is not enough. The wave does not travel along a line but through a volume shaped like an ellipsoid: the first Fresnel zone. If an obstacle enters it, attenuation appears even though there is a direct view. r in meters, d₁ and d₂ the distances from each end to the obstacle in km, D the total distance in km and f the frequency in GHz. Worked example · Does it clear that stand of trees? A 10 km link at 5.8 GHz, with a 12 m tall stand of trees right in the middle of the span. The antennas are 20 m above the terrain. First it is solved as if the Earth were flat; then its curvature is added. Radius of the first zone at the midpoint:. Required clearance, 60 %: 6.8 m below the straight line. The straight line between the two antennas passes 20 m above the ground at the middle of the span. The treetops are at 12 m: 8 m remain clear.: over flat terrain the link passes, although with little margin. The curvature of the Earth is still missing. With the factor of the standard atmosphere, in the middle of the span the terrain rises. The 8 m of clearance drop to 6.5 m, below the 6.8 m: strictly speaking it does not pass. Both antennas must be raised at least 0.3 m, and quite a bit more if the trees keep growing. That last point is real and always forgotten: trees grow and buildings appear. A link is calculated with the terrain of the coming years."},{"t":"The link budget","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#presupuesto","x":"03 The link budget It is an addition and subtraction of decibels, from end to end. If the result exceeds the receiver sensitivity with enough margin, the link works. Worked example · A 15 km link at 5.8 GHz Item Value Cumulative Transmitter power +20 dBm 20 Cable and connector loss (Tx) −1 dB 19 Antenna gain (Tx) +24 dBi 43 Free-space loss (15 km, 5.8 GHz) −131.2 dB −88.2 Antenna gain (Rx) +24 dBi −64.2 Cable and connector loss (Rx) −1 dB −65.2 dBm Receiver sensitivity −83 dBm — Margin 17.8 dB. A margin of almost 18 dB is a healthy link: it withstands rain, misalignment and equipment aging. How much margin is needed Less than 10 dB: fragile link. It works on a nice day and drops with rain or wind. 15 to 20 dB: reasonable for a service link. More than 25 dB: critical links, or high bands where rain hits hard. And the fade margin due to multipath must also be subtracted, which on long spans over reflective terrain —water, salt flats, flat fields— can be 20 dB."},{"t":"Rain, availability and fading","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#clima","x":"04 Rain, availability and fading Rain attenuation It depends enormously on the frequency. Below 6 GHz it is almost negligible; at 11 GHz it already matters; at 23 GHz or more, it is the dominant factor in the design: a heavy storm can add tens of dB. That is why high-band links are short hops, and why in areas of intense rainfall it is preferable to move down a band even if it costs bandwidth. Availability It is expressed as a percentage of the year: 99.9 % → up to 8.8 hours per year out of service. 99.99 % → 53 minutes per year. 99.999 % → 5 minutes per year. Every extra nine costs margin, and margin costs bigger antennas or taller towers. Ground reflection On long spans over smooth surfaces, part of the signal is reflected and reaches the receiver out of phase. If it arrives in antiphase, it cancels. It is fought with space diversity —two antennas separated vertically by a few meters— or by choosing heights that put the reflection outside the critical zone. It is the reason why two identical links can behave very differently."},{"t":"From calculation to installation","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#montaje","x":"05 From calculation to installation The complete procedure Survey both ends: coordinates, available height, obstacles, power supply and access. Plot the terrain profile between them, with contour lines or with a profile tool, and include the curvature of the Earth. Check Fresnel at the critical points and define the tower heights. Choose the band and equipment, and calculate the link budget with margin. Install and aim: with a compass and GPS for the coarse work, then fine-tuning with the equipment's own level indicator, moving a little at a time and waiting for the reading. Document: azimuth, elevation, received level, SWR, photos of the alignment and of the sealing. Tower work It is one of the most dangerous tasks in the trade: height, electrical storms, radiation from other active antennas on the same structure. Never climb alone, use a harness with a double lanyard, check the condition of the tower and of the guy wires, and ask for the nearby transmitters to be switched off before working in front of their antennas. With wind or a storm in sight, do not climb."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Calculating a real link Choose two real points in the city or the area, obtain their coordinates and heights, plot the profile and calculate the Fresnel zone at the most critical point. Determine the minimum height of the antennas and make the link budget with specific commercial equipment and its prices. Lab 2 · Fresnel in miniature With two WiFi devices and directional antennas, set up a 100 to 200 m link inside the school and measure the received level. Then place a metal surface at different points —near one end and in the middle— and note the drop. The attenuation is much greater when the obstacle is in the middle: that is where the Fresnel zone is widest. Lab 3 · Fine aiming With the link established, misalign one antenna one degree at a time and note the drop in level. Plot level against angle: you obtain the antenna's radiation pattern and see how critical aiming is for high-gain antennas. Lab 4 · Margin and rain Record the received level of the link over several days, including a rainy day. Compare the drop with what is expected for that band. With 5 GHz equipment the drop is small, and that observation —made with your own data— explains why that band is chosen."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause There is a direct view and the link performs poorly The Fresnel zone is obstructed even though the other end can be seen. The link degrades over the years Trees grew or a building appeared along the span. It drops in the rain Insufficient margin for the chosen band. In high bands, rain dominates. It works in the morning and drops at noon Fading due to multipath or atmospheric refraction, typical over flat terrain or water. Received level far below the calculated one Coarse aiming, crossed polarization, or actual cable loss greater than assumed. The antenna was aimed at a side lobe When the antenna is moved, several maxima appear: look for the highest, not the first one that shows up. Interference in an unlicensed band Another user on the same channel. The spectrum has to be surveyed before choosing the channel."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Microwave links","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why is a line of sight not enough? Because the wave occupies a volume, the Fresnel zone. An obstacle inside it attenuates the signal even if it does not block the direct view. The practical rule is to clear 60 % of the first zone. An 8 km link at 5 GHz with an obstacle in the middle: what is the radius of the first zone?. At least 6.6 m must be cleared. Where is the Fresnel zone widest? In the middle of the span. That is why an obstacle at the center is much more of a nuisance than one near either antenna. What margin is considered healthy on a service link? On the order of 15 to 20 dB. Below 10 dB the link drops with rain, wind or any degradation. Calculate the free-space loss for 20 km at 5.8 GHz.. Why are 23 GHz links short hops? Because rain attenuates enormously at those frequencies: a storm can add tens of dB. It is compensated by shortening the span and increasing the margin. What does an availability of 99.99 % mean? That the link can be out of service for up to 53 minutes per year. Every additional “nine” requires more margin, and therefore bigger antennas or taller towers. How is fading from ground reflection fought? With space diversity: two antennas separated vertically, so that when one is in a null the other is not. Also by choosing heights that shift the reflection point. When aiming, several signal maxima appear. Which one is the correct one? The absolute maximum: the others are side lobes. Aiming at a side lobe gives a link that works but w"},{"t":"Before testing: method, instruments and safety","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#metodo","x":"01 Before testing: method, instruments and safety A test is a planned measurement: the machine is put in a known condition, a few quantities are measured with suitable instruments, and the quantities that cannot be measured directly are calculated from them. The nameplate says what the machine should do; the test says what it does. Operating principles and starting methods are in AC motors and Transformers; this page shows how to check them. No-load Rated voltage and nothing connected to the output (the motor, with no load coupled). The machine draws little power, almost all of it to magnetize the core and overcome friction. It gives the iron losses and the excitation current. Short-circuit or locked rotor Output shorted (or shaft locked) and reduced voltage, just enough for rated current to flow. It gives the copper losses and the leakage impedance. Under load The machine delivers power to a real load or to a brake. It gives the efficiency, the regulation and the temperature rise, but it uses all the energy and needs a suitable load. Why two «small» tests replace a big one At full load, a 5 kVA transformer delivers 4 kW at. In the classic tests the supply provides only 60 W at no load and 110 W on short circuit. Since iron losses depend on voltage and copper losses on current, each test isolates one group of losses with a fraction of the energy. With both, the equivalent circuit is built and everything else is calculated from it: efficiency at any load, regulation, short-cir"},{"t":"Transformer: no-load and short-circuit tests","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#transformador","x":"02 Transformer: no-load and short-circuit tests Both tests use the same instruments: a variable autotransformer, a voltmeter, an ammeter and a wattmeter. What changes is which winding is supplied and what is done with the other. No-load test Short-circuit test Supplied from The low-voltage side (more convenient). The high-voltage side (rated current is lower). The other winding Open. Shorted, through a low-resistance ammeter. Applied voltage Rated, raised gradually. Reduced: the voltage that makes rated current flow, a few percent of rated. Measured,,,, Losses that show up Iron: (copper is negligible because is small). Full-load copper: (iron is negligible because the voltage is small). Parameters obtained and: the excitation branch. and: the leakage impedance. No-load test. The current splits into a part in phase with the voltage (iron losses, ) and another lagging by 90° (magnetizing, ). Short-circuit test. It is measured on the supplied side, so the values are referred to that side. To move them to the other side, multiply or divide by, where is the turns ratio. Example · The parameters of a 5 kVA transformer Single-phase transformer, 5 kVA, 440/220 V, 50 Hz. No-load test from the low side: 220 V, 1.8 A, 60 W. Short-circuit test from the high side, at rated current: 22 V, 11.36 A, 110 W. 1 · Rated currents. A and A. 2 · No load. (φ₀ = 81.3°). A and A. Then and. Referred to the high side they are multiplied by: 3227 Ω and 495 Ω. 3 · Short circuit.;;. In percent:, and. 4 · E"},{"t":"Induction motor: no-load and locked-rotor tests","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#motor","x":"03 Induction motor: no-load and locked-rotor tests The induction motor is a transformer whose secondary rotates. That is why it is tested the same way: at no load you see the excitation branch, and with the rotor locked (slip, the equivalent of the short circuit) the leakage impedance. The difference is that the motor has mechanical losses that a transformer does not. The rotating field, slip and starting methods are in AC motors. No-load test Rated voltage, no load coupled, with the machine running for several minutes until the bearings settle. Voltage, current in the three phases and power are measured. The input power covers three things: iron, friction and windage, and stator copper. To separate iron from mechanical losses, the test is repeated lowering the voltage from to about half, is plotted against and extrapolated to: what remains does not depend on voltage and is the mechanical loss. Locked-rotor test Shaft locked and reduced voltage, raised from zero until the current is the rated one; quick reading, in a few seconds. With almost all the current goes through the series branch and the excitation branch can be neglected., and are measured; the stator resistance,, must be measured beforehand with direct current. Per-phase values of the equivalent star, with the line voltage. Without other data, the leakage reactance is split in halves:. Example · A 5.5 kW three-phase motor 4-pole, 5.5 kW, 380 V, 50 Hz motor, with A and 1440 rpm on the nameplate. Stator resistance mea"},{"t":"Measurements on AC and DC motors","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#medicion","x":"04 Measurements on AC and DC motors Besides the laboratory tests, the technician measures the motor in service and out of service. This is the list of what is checked on an alternating-current machine and what each value says. Measurement How it is done What it tells Nameplate and connection Read voltage, current, speed,, insulation class, duty and terminal box. Whether the motor matches the supply (380 V star or 220 V delta) and the load. See the nameplate. Line voltage Between the three phases, at the motor terminals and under load. Voltage drop in the feeder and voltage unbalance. A small voltage unbalance produces a current unbalance several times larger. Current in each phase Clamp meter on each conductor, in normal service. Overload (against ) and unbalance. Power and Two wattmeters or analyzer;. Real motor load and power factor. See AC power. Winding resistance Between terminals, with DC, cold and with the temperature noted. Equal phases (within a few percent), open circuits or shorted turns, and the basis for the temperature-rise test. Insulation Megohmmeter between terminals and frame, with the motor disconnected. Moisture or varnish deterioration. The trend matters, not a single value; as a rough minimum, MΩ at 40 °C (IEEE 43). Speed Tachometer on the shaft. Slip; with 1500 rpm synchronous and 1440 rpm,. Temperature Thermal camera or thermometer on frame and bearings. Overload, blocked ventilation or worn bearings. Current unbalance is calculated as the largest devi"},{"t":"Load curve and temperature-rise test","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#carga","x":"05 Load curve and temperature-rise test The load test is the only one that measures efficiency, speed and power factor directly, and the one that shows the real temperature rise. The motor is loaded with a brake: a Prony brake (band or shoe with a scale), a generator feeding an electrical load, or an electromagnetic brake. Output power comes from torque and speed. in N·m, in rpm, in W. With a 0.25 m arm and a 100 N force, the torque is 25 N·m and, at 1440 rpm, W. Load Line current Speed Efficiency Input power 25 % 5.3 A 0.48 1485 rpm 81.4 % 1.69 kW 50 % 6.9 A 0.70 1470 rpm 86.3 % 3.19 kW 75 % 9.0 A 0.80 1455 rpm 86.6 % 4.76 kW 100 % 11.5 A 0.85 1440 rpm 85.6 % 6.42 kW 125 % 14.2 A 0.87 1425 rpm 84.2 % 8.16 kW Simplified model built from the tests: constant losses of 273 W (iron plus friction), copper loss of 650 W at rated current proportional to the square of the load, reactive power growing the same way, and useful power equal to the fraction of 5.5 kW. Above 100 % the motor is overloaded; it is shown only to see the trend. Why a motor should not be oversized Efficiency is maximum when copper losses equal the constant ones: of rated load. A motor running at 25 % has and: the installation pays for reactive current and loss energy because a motor was bought «just in case». It is better to choose it to run between 60 % and 100 % of its rating. Temperature-rise test The machine is run at its rated load until thermal equilibrium —when the temperature stops rising, with a variati"},{"t":"Visual inspection and selection criteria","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#inspeccion","x":"06 Visual inspection and selection criteria Before connecting an instrument, look. Visual inspection takes minutes, costs nothing and finds defects that no electrical test detects, or that make the test dangerous (a broken frame, a low oil level). It is done with the machine disconnected and recorded on a checklist. Item What to look for What it indicates Nameplate Legible, complete and matching the installation. If missing or erased, there is no reference value to test against. Frame, feet and end shields Cracks, dents, rust, burnt paint, loose bolts. Overheating, vibration or poor mounting. Fan and fins Obstructions, dirt, damaged blades. Poor ventilation: the motor will run hotter than in the test. Shaft and bearings Turn by hand: play, roughness, noise, grease leaking out. Worn or poorly lubricated bearing. Terminal box and connections Loose or discolored terminals, wrong links; conductors with burnt insulation. Poor tightening: hot spots. Check the connection against the supply voltage. Visible winding Darkened varnish, burnt smell, conductive dust. Repeated overheating or moisture. Brushes and commutator (DC) Wear, sparking, scored or blackened surface, protruding bars. Poor commutation or brushes of the wrong grade. Transformer: tank and oil Leaks, level, oil color, tank deformation. Overheating, damaged gaskets, moisture ingress. Transformer: silica gel and bushings Silica gel color; cracks or dirt on the bushings. Saturated silica gel no longer protects the oil; a di"},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#laboratorio","x":"07 In the laboratory Practice 1 · The parameters of a single-phase transformer Materials: 100 to 500 VA transformer with two windings, variable autotransformer, voltmeter, ammeter, wattmeter (or power analyzer), multimeter for resistance, leads of adequate cross-section. Steps: 1) read the nameplate and calculate and; 2) measure the resistance of each winding with DC; 3) no-load test from the low side, raising the voltage slowly to rated, and note,,; 4) short-circuit test from the high side, closing the secondary through the ammeter and raising from zero to; note and and bring the voltage back to zero; 5) calculate,,,,,, and the efficiency at 100 % and 50 %. Accepted if: matches measured with DC to within 15 % (corrected for temperature) and is less than 10 % of the rated power. Practice 2 · No-load and locked-rotor tests of a three-phase motor Materials: 0.37 to 1.5 kW motor, variable three-phase autotransformer, two wattmeters or analyzer, clamp meter, megohmmeter, mechanical device to lock the shaft. Steps: 1) visual inspection and insulation measurement; 2) stator resistance with DC; 3) no-load at rated voltage, running about 15 minutes; 4) no-load at six decreasing voltages, from to about half; 5) with the shaft locked, raise the voltage from zero up to rated current and read in less than 10 s; 6) calculate parameters, mechanical and iron losses, starting current and torque. Accepted if: against gives a straight line, the three no-load currents differ by no more than 10 "},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Short-circuit testing the transformer at rated voltage A current tens of times rated flows (in the example, 20) and the winding burns in seconds. Always raise the voltage from zero. Using an ordinary wattmeter in the no-load test At of 0.1 the pointer barely moves and the relative error is huge. Use a low-power-factor one or an analyzer. Taking the motor's as iron loss It includes friction and windage and the stator copper; they must be subtracted. Mixing the transformer sides Values measured on the low and high sides cannot be added: they must be referred to the same side by multiplying by. Keeping the rotor locked too long Rated current in an unventilated rotor heats the winding very quickly; results change and the motor is damaged. Measuring winding resistance without noting the temperature The value is useless for comparison or for calculating the temperature rise. Measuring hot resistance several minutes after stopping the machine The winding has already cooled: the rise is underestimated. Extrapolate to the instant of shutdown. Testing with an unbalanced or distorted supply Currents and losses are no longer representative; a supply problem is mistaken for a motor defect. Opening the field of a running DC motor Runaway: speed rises until the rotor is destroyed."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Motor and transformer tests","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/ensayos-maquinas#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is obtained from each of the two transformer tests? The no-load test, at rated voltage, gives the iron losses and the parameters of the excitation branch (, ). The short-circuit test, at rated current and reduced voltage, gives the copper losses and the leakage impedance (, ). Why does the short-circuit test not destroy the transformer? Because it is done with reduced voltage, a few percent of rated (5 % in the example), just enough for rated current to flow. At rated voltage the current would be about 20 times larger. A no-load test reads 220 V, 1.5 A and 45 W. Calculate and.. (referred to the side where it was measured). A 380 V three-phase motor at no load draws 4.6 A and 340 W. What is its power factor, and why is it so low?. Almost all the no-load current is magnetizing (reactive): active power only covers losses, and there is no load to absorb more. How is the starting current estimated from the locked-rotor test? The current is measured at reduced voltage and scaled linearly with voltage: A. It is an estimate somewhat higher than the real one, because of saturation. A winding's resistance goes from 1.00 to 1.25 Ω. With °C, what is the temperature rise? K: it exceeds the limit of class A (60 K) and complies with those of classes E (75 K), B (80 K), F (105 K) and H (125 K). Why does an oversized motor perform poorly? Because at low load its power factor and its efficiency fall. The example motor, at 25 % load, has and, against 0.85 and 85.6 % at f"},{"t":"The toolchain","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#toolchain","x":"01 The toolchain Programming a microcontroller has one central difference from programming a PC: the program is written and compiled on one machine and run on another. This is called cross-compilation. Component What it does Toolchain The set of compiler + assembler + linker + utilities, specific to the target architecture ( avr-gcc, arm-none-eabi-gcc, Microchip XC8). IDE The graphical environment that integrates editor, compiler, programmer and debugger: MPLAB X, Atmel Studio, STM32CubeIDE, PlatformIO, Arduino IDE. Linker script Describes the chip's memory map and decides at which address each thing goes. Startup code An assembly routine that runs before main(): it sets up the stack, initializes variables and jumps to the program. Programmer / flasher The hardware that writes the.hex into flash: PICkit, USBasp, ST-Link, or a bootloader over USB. Debugger Lets you halt the program on the real chip and inspect variables: ICD, JTAG, SWD. The three memories of a microcontroller Flash — stores the program. Non-volatile, large (tens of KB), written when programming. RAM — stores the variables. Volatile and very scarce: 512 bytes to 8 KB in an 8-bit micro. It is the resource that runs out first. EEPROM — a few non-volatile bytes, for configuration that must survive power-off. When compiling, the environment reports how much flash and how much RAM the program uses. Always look at that number. A program that uses 95 % of the RAM will fail as soon as the stack grows a little."},{"t":"Pointers","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#punteros","x":"02 Pointers A pointer is a variable that holds a memory address. It is the concept that is hardest to grasp and the one that pays off most: without pointers there is no hardware access, no pass by reference, and no data structures. int x = 42; int *p; // p is a pointer to int p = &x; // p takes the ADDRESS of x printf ( \"%d\", *p); // prints 42 — * accesses the CONTENTS *p = 7; // now x is 7, without naming x What they are really for Modifying the caller's variables // does NOT work: receives copies void mal ( int a, int b) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; } // DOES work: receives addresses void bien ( int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; } bien (&x, &y); Returning more than one value void leer_sensor ( int *temp, int *hum) { *temp = medir_t (); *hum = medir_h (); } int t, h; leer_sensor (&t, &h); A C function returns a single value; with pointers it can “return” as many as needed. Pointer arithmetic Adding 1 to a pointer does not add 1 to the address: it adds the size of the pointed-to type. That is the reason a pointer has a type. int v[ 5 ] = { 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }; int *p = v; // the array name is ALREADY the address of the first element *p // 10 — equivalent to v[0] *(p + 2 ) // 30 — equivalent to v[2]; advanced 2 * sizeof(int) bytes p[ 2 ] // 30 — bracket notation IS pointer arithmetic // Traverse an array with pointers for ( int *q = v; q < v + 5; q++) { printf ( \"%d \", *q); } The three pointer mistakes Uninitialized pointer. It contains garbage and writing through "},{"t":"Structures, unions and typedef","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#estructuras","x":"03 Structures, unions and typedef A structure groups data of different types under a single name. It is what lets you treat “a sensor” or “a measurement” as a single thing. typedef struct { uint8_t id; int16_t temperatura; // in tenths of a degree uint16_t humedad; uint8_t valido; } Sensor; Sensor s1; s1.id = 3; s1.temperatura = 235; // 23.5 °C Sensor red[ 8 ]; // an array of structures red[ 0 ].humedad = 640; Sensor *ps = &s1; ps->id = 4; // with a pointer, use -> instead of. Union All its fields share the same place in memory. It is used to view the same piece of data in two ways. union { uint16_t palabra; uint8_t byte[ 2 ]; } conv; conv.palabra = 0xABCD; // conv.byte[0] and [1] are // 0xCD and 0xAB (or the reverse, // depending on endianness) Bit fields They let you declare fields of an exact width in bits: ideal for configuration registers. struct { uint8_t habilitado: 1; uint8_t modo: 2; uint8_t prescaler: 3; uint8_t reservado: 2; } ctrl; ctrl.modo = 2; // readable Structure padding ( alignment ) The compiler may insert empty bytes between fields to align them, so sizeof(Sensor) can be larger than the sum of its fields. This matters when the structure represents the exact format of a communication frame or of a file: in those cases you must use __attribute__((packed)) or serialize field by field."},{"t":"Hardware access and device drivers","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#hardware","x":"04 Hardware access and device drivers Here everything above comes together. In a microcontroller, peripherals are controlled by writing and reading registers located at fixed memory addresses. A pointer to a specific address is the mechanism that makes it possible. // A register is a pointer to a fixed address, marked volatile #define PORTB (*( volatile uint8_t *) 0x25 ) #define DDRB (*( volatile uint8_t *) 0x24 ) #define PINB (*( volatile uint8_t *) 0x23 ) DDRB |= ( 1 << 0 ); // PB0 as output PORTB |= ( 1 << 0 ); // PB0 high The three elements of that line are the ones you need to understand: (volatile uint8_t *) 0x25 — converts the number 0x25 into a pointer to a byte at that address. volatile — forbids the compiler from optimizing the accesses, because the value can change by hardware. *(...) — dereferences, so that PORTB behaves like an ordinary variable. What a device driver is It is the software layer that translates between “what the program wants to do” and “the registers that have to be touched”. It isolates the rest of the program from the details of the chip. // ---- led.h: the INTERFACE, the only thing the rest of the program sees ---- void led_init ( void ); void led_on ( uint8_t n); void led_off ( uint8_t n); void led_toggle ( uint8_t n); // ---- led.c: the IMPLEMENTATION, specific to this micro ---- #include \"led.h\" #include \"registros.h\" void led_init ( void ) { DDRB = 0xFF; PORTB = 0x00; } void led_on ( uint8_t n) { PORTB |= ( 1 << n); } void led_off ( uint8_"},{"t":"Bootloaders","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#bootloader","x":"05 Bootloaders A bootloader is a small program that lives in a reserved area of the flash and runs first at power-up. Its function is to be able to program the rest of the flash without needing an external programmer. Without bootloader With bootloader A programmer is needed (PICkit, USBasp, ST-Link). A USB or serial cable is enough. All the flash is available for the program. The bootloader takes between 0.5 and 4 KB. Instant startup. Initial delay while it waits to see whether a new program is sent. Needed to flash the bootloader the first time. Allows updating the firmware in the field, without opening the device. The best-known case An Arduino board comes with a bootloader flashed at the factory. When you press “Upload”, the IDE compiles with avr-gcc, generates the.hex and sends it over the serial port; the bootloader receives it and writes it into flash. That is why an Arduino board does not need a programmer, and why a “bare” micro bought loose does need one at least once, to flash the bootloader onto it."},{"t":"Real time and interrupts","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#tiempo-real","x":"06 Real time and interrupts A system is real-time when responding late is equivalent to responding wrongly. It does not mean “fast”: it means predictable. An airbag that fires 200 ms late has failed, even if the processor is extremely fast. Polling ( cyclic query ) The program asks over and over whether something happened. while ( 1 ) { if ( boton_apretado ()) accion (); otras_tareas (); } Simple and predictable, but it wastes time and the latency depends on how long the rest of the loop takes. Interrupts The hardware gives notice: it suspends the program, runs a routine and returns. ISR (INT0_vect) { bandera = 1; // short } while ( 1 ) { if (bandera) {... } } Immediate response without wasting CPU, in exchange for a program that is harder to reason about. The three rules of an interrupt routine Keep it very short. Set a flag, store a piece of data and exit. While the ISR runs, the rest of the system is stopped. No printf, no delays, no waiting. Everything slow goes in the main loop. Every variable shared with the main loop is declared volatile. Without that, the optimizer breaks the communication between the two. Latency and priorities Latency is the time between the event occurring and the ISR beginning to execute. It is made up of the time to finish the instruction in progress, the context save, and — most importantly — any period during which interrupts are disabled or another ISR is running. That is why critical sections must be as short as possible: every instruction wi"},{"t":"Concurrency","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#concurrencia","x":"07 Concurrency As soon as there are interrupts, there are two flows of execution sharing variables. And that is where errors appear that do not exist in a sequential program. The race condition volatile uint16_t contador; // 16 bits on an 8-bit micro ISR (TIMER0_OVF_vect) { contador++; } int main ( void ) { while ( 1 ) { uint16_t c = contador; // ← DANGER... } } An 8-bit micro needs two instructions to read a 16-bit variable. If the interrupt lands right in the middle — after reading the low byte and before the high one — the value obtained mixes the before and the after. With contador going from 0x00FF to 0x0100 you can read 0x01FF: a value that never existed. The solution is the critical section: read with interrupts disabled, and re-enable them immediately. uint8_t sreg = SREG; // save the previous state cli (); // disable interrupts uint16_t c = contador; // atomic read SREG = sreg; // restore (do NOT call sei() blindly) Concept What it is Critical section A stretch of code that cannot be interrupted without corrupting data. It is protected by disabling interrupts, and must be as short as possible. Atomic operation One that completes with no possibility of interruption. On an 8-bit micro, reading or writing a uint8_t is atomic; a uint16_t is not. Reentrant function One that can be called again before it finishes (from an ISR) without breaking. It uses no global or static variables. Circular buffer The standard structure for passing data from an ISR to the main loop: the I"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Lab 1 · Seeing memory for real A PC program that declares several variables of different types and prints their address with %p and their size with sizeof. Verify that the addresses of an array are separated by exactly the size of the type, and that adding 1 to an int* advances 4 bytes while adding 1 to a char* advances 1. It is the most direct way to understand pointer arithmetic. Lab 2 · Pass by value and by reference Write two versions of a function intercambiar (“swap”), one with ordinary parameters and another with pointers. Check that the first one does nothing. Then write a function that receives an array and its length and returns through pointers the maximum, the minimum and the average. Lab 3 · A simulated driver Write led.h and led.c as in the example, but with a global variable unsigned char PORTB that simulates the port, and a function that prints its contents in binary. Then write a main that makes a “Knight Rider” chaser using only the driver functions. Compile the two files separately and link them: it is the first multi-file project of the course. Lab 4 · Structures and data frames Define a structure that represents a measurement (identifier, value, timestamp, checksum ). Write functions to fill it in, compute the checksum and dump it into an array of bytes ready to transmit. Print the sizeof of the structure and compare it with the sum of its fields: if they differ, there is padding, and that is the reason to use packed."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Pointer declared but not initialized A write to a random address. On a micro, a reset or erratic behavior. Returning the address of a local variable Dangling pointer: the memory has already been reused. Confusing *p = 5 with p = 5 The first writes the value; the second changes the address pointed to. The second is almost never what you want. Using. with a pointer to a structure Compile error. With a pointer, use ->. volatile missing on a register or on an ISR variable The optimizer removes reads and the program hangs. It only shows up when compiling with optimization. Long ISR, or one with delays Other interrupts are lost and the system stops responding. Reading a shared 16-bit variable without a critical section Race condition: impossible values, very hard to reproduce. Enabling interrupts with sei() at the end of a critical section It enables them even if they were previously disabled for another reason. You must save and restore the state. Using malloc on a microcontroller Fragmentation of a tiny RAM and unpredictable failures. In embedded systems, static memory is used."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"C and C++ development environments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment What is cross-compilation and why is it necessary in embedded systems? It means compiling on one machine (the PC) so that the program runs on a different architecture (the microcontroller). It is necessary because the micro has no keyboard, screen or capacity to run a compiler. If int *p is 0x1000 and p++ is executed, what is its value now? It depends on the size of int: if it is 2 bytes, 0x1002; if it is 4 bytes, 0x1004. Pointer arithmetic advances in units of the pointed-to type, not in bytes. Write a function that swaps two integers of the caller. void intercambiar ( int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; } // call it like this: intercambiar(&x, &y); Why is a hardware register declared volatile ? Because its value can change for reasons outside the program (the hardware modifies it). Without volatile, the compiler may read it just once and reuse that copy, or eliminate writes that “serve no purpose” but actually drive a peripheral. What concrete advantage does separating code into a driver have? The rest of the program stops depending on the microcontroller. When you change chip, only the driver is rewritten; in addition the program can be tested on a PC with a simulated driver and it reads much better ( led_on(3) instead of PORTB |= 8 ). What is a bootloader and what advantage does it give? A small program resident in flash that can program the rest of the flash over a serial or USB port. It avoids needing an external programmer and allows updat"},{"t":"What it represents and what it does not","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#que-es","x":"01 What it represents and what it does not Connectivity, not geometry A schematic says what is connected to what. It does not say where each component goes, how long a wire is, or how big the circuit board is. Two schematics drawn in very different ways can describe exactly the same circuit, and the software knows it: internally both generate the same netlist. Hence the freedom to draw it in the most readable way, which is the only criterion that matters."},{"t":"Rules for making it understandable","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#reglas","x":"02 Rules for making it understandable Do Signal from left to right: input on the left, output on the right. Power rails at the top and ground at the bottom, always. One functional block per area, with a title if the schematic is large. Net labels with names that mean something: RESET, TX, VBAT. Decoupling capacitors next to the IC they supply. Margin notes for whatever the drawing cannot express: “adjust R7 to 2.5 V,” “mount on heatsink.” Don’t Crossing lines unnecessarily, or using little “hop-over” arcs at the crossings: today this is solved with labels. Joining four lines at a single point: with a junction dot, the node is ambiguous. Make two separate T junctions instead. Drawing the IC with its pins in physical order if that tangles the schematic. Leaving nets unnamed and then hunting for them by color. Using the same label spelled two different ways: VCC and Vcc can be two different nets for the software. The junction dot Two lines that cross without a dot are not connected; with a dot, they are. This is a universal convention and the source of half the reading errors on a printed schematic, especially if the resolution is low. That is why good practice is to avoid crossings and not depend on the dot. Hierarchy and buses Hierarchical sheet A large project does not fit on one sheet. It is divided into blocks —power supply, input, control, outputs— each on its own sheet, with ports that connect them. The top sheet shows the block diagram and each block opens separately. Bu"},{"t":"Symbol, footprint and model","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#bibliotecas","x":"03 Symbol, footprint and model Every component in the project has three different representations that must be kept consistent. This is where beginners’ designs fail most often. Symbol What you see in the schematic. It defines how many pins the part has, what they are called and what type they are (input, output, power, bidirectional). Footprint The pattern of copper and holes on the board: the actual physical shape of the component. A single symbol can have many footprints: the same transistor comes in TO-92, TO-220 or SOT-23. 3D model It is used to check heights and clearances against the enclosure, and is exported to mechanical CAD. Always check the footprint against the datasheet It is the most expensive error in board design, because it is not discovered until the manufactured boards arrive. Before sending the design to fabrication: Compare the footprint with the dimensional drawing in the datasheet, millimeter by millimeter. Print the board at 1:1 scale and place the real components on top. It takes a minute and catches almost every error. Confirm the pin numbering: a TO-92 has three legs and at least three different pin orders depending on the manufacturer and the part. Check the orientation of connectors and polarized components: the pin 1 mark has to be on the silkscreen."},{"t":"Electrical rules checking","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#erc","x":"04 Electrical rules checking The ERC (electrical rules check) examines the schematic for inconsistencies, using the declared type of each pin. It does not understand circuits: it understands rules. Even so, it catches most careless errors. Warning What it means What to do Unconnected pin An input was left floating Connect it, or explicitly mark it “no connect” if intentional. In CMOS, never leave it floating. Two outputs tied together Two output pins on the same net It is a short circuit when one drives high and the other low. It is only valid with open-collector or tri-state outputs. Power pin with no source There are VCC pins but nothing that drives them Add a power source symbol or mark the net as powered by a connector (PWR_FLAG in KiCad). Single-pin net A label that does not match any other Almost always a typo in the net name. Duplicate reference designators Two R7s in the same schematic Renumber automatically. Symbol with no footprint assigned The component cannot be transferred to the board Assign the correct footprint, checking it against the datasheet. What the ERC does not see It does not know whether the base resistor is too large, whether the filter capacitor is big enough or whether the transistor will saturate. The electrical check is about consistency of the drawing, not of the design. That is what calculation, simulation and the prototype are for."},{"t":"What comes out of the schematic","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#documentos","x":"05 What comes out of the schematic Document What it contains Who it is for Netlist The list of nets with all their pins The PCB software. It is the bridge between schematic and board. BOM (bill of materials) Reference designator, value, footprint, quantity, supplier part number Purchasing and assembly. It is generated automatically from the schematic. Schematic as PDF The drawing with its title block and revision Documentation, repair, presentation Design notes Criteria, calculations, values to adjust Whoever picks the project up a year from now, who will probably be yourself Revisions Every schematic carries a revision number and a date in the title block, and a line saying what changed. Without that, as soon as two versions are circulating nobody knows which one matches the board on the bench, and more time is lost finding out than in the whole design. Tool Note KiCad Free, open source and full-featured. It is the standard in education and increasingly in industry. Runs on Windows, Linux and Mac. EasyEDA In the browser, with a huge library tied to a supplier. Very handy for getting started. Eagle Historic and widely used; Autodesk discontinued it in 2026 and its functions moved to Fusion (Fusion Electronics). Proteus Its strength is simulating the circuit, even with the microcontroller running the program. Altium Professional, expensive and powerful; the standard in high-volume industry."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Draw a known circuit Take the regulated power supply with the LM317 already studied and draw it completely in the chosen software: symbols, values, reference designators, net labels and title block. Run the ERC until no warnings remain and export the PDF. Lab 2 · Find other people’s errors The teacher hands out a schematic with five planted errors —a floating pin, a misspelled label, a reversed electrolytic capacitor, two repeated reference designators and a wrong footprint—. Look for them first by eye, write them down, and only then run the ERC to compare what each one found. Lab 3 · The same circuit, two drawings Draw a simple circuit twice: once following the readability rules (signal from left to right, power at the top) and once in a rush. Generate both netlists and compare them: they are identical. This shows that readability is not for the machine, it is for people. Lab 4 · Library and BOM Create the symbol and footprint of a component that is not in the library, assign it, and generate the complete bill of materials of the project with quantities and part numbers. Get a quote from a local supplier: that is where the project stops being a drawing and acquires a price."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The board is missing connections that are in the schematic Lines that look like they touch but are not joined: they were drawn on top of each other without the software creating the junction. Two nets that should be one Labels spelled differently: VCC and Vcc, or with a trailing space. The component does not fit on the manufactured board Wrong footprint, or one not checked against the datasheet. The IC was inserted backwards The pin 1 mark is not on the silkscreen, or the footprint places it wrongly. The ERC reports nothing and the circuit does not work The ERC checks consistency, not design. Nothing replaces calculation and the prototype. The BOM does not match what was assembled A value was changed on the board and not in the schematic. The schematic is always the source: fix it there and regenerate everything. Nobody knows which version is the good one The revision and date are missing from the title block."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Schematic circuit diagrams","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What information does a schematic carry, exactly? The connectivity: which pins are joined together, with which components and with which values. It does not carry position, size or conductor length. What is a netlist and what is it for? The listing of all the nets of the circuit with the pins that belong to each one. It is what the PCB software reads to know what it must connect: the bridge between the schematic and the board. What is the difference between a symbol and a footprint? The symbol is the representation in the schematic and defines the pins; the footprint is the physical pattern of copper and holes on the board. A single symbol can have several footprints depending on the package. Why may two crossed lines be connected or not? Because the connection is indicated by the junction dot. Without a dot, the lines cross without touching. Since the dot can get lost in a low-quality printout, it is best simply to avoid crossings by using net labels. The ERC warns “power pin with no source.” What is going on? There are power pins on the net but no element that supplies it. This is normal when the power comes from an external connector: it is resolved by marking that net with the appropriate power flag. Why is it sometimes valid to tie two outputs together? Only if they are open-collector or tri-state: in the first case each output can pull the net to zero but not to one, and in the second only one is active at a time. With ordinary totem-pole outputs, tyi"},{"t":"External structure","d":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc#externa","x":"01 External structure Block Function Power supply Powers the internal logic and, in many units, also delivers the 24 V for the sensors. Its capacity limits how many modules can be mounted. CPU Runs the program, manages memory, handles communication and performs self-diagnosis. Input modules Adapt the field signals to the logic level: they filter, limit and isolate. Output modules Convert commands into usable power: relay, transistor or triac. Communication modules Serial ports, fieldbus or Ethernet, to talk to other devices and to the supervisory system. Rack and internal bus Mechanical support and data bus that connects the CPU with all the modules. Programming terminal The PC with the manufacturer’s software. It is also used to monitor online and to force signals. The 24 V supply is not unlimited The 24 V output built into the PLC usually delivers only a few hundred milliamperes. Hanging twenty inductive sensors, the relay coils and the pushbutton panel lighting from it ends up loading it to the limit: the voltage drops, the sensors start to fail intermittently and the diagnosis points everywhere except at the power supply. As soon as there are several loads, an external supply is added and the common is shared."},{"t":"Memory and addressing","d":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc#interna","x":"02 Memory and addressing Area What it stores Program memory The user program, in non-volatile memory. It is kept without power. Input image The snapshot of the state of all the inputs at the start of the cycle. It is what the program reads. Output image What the program writes, which is transferred to the terminals at the end of the cycle. Internal markers Auxiliary bits: flags, intermediate states, comparison results. They have no physical terminal. Data Words, integers and reals for calculations, setpoints and process values. Timers and counters Blocks with their preset value, their current value and their output bit. Retentive area Part of the memory that survives a power failure: accumulated production, setpoints, machine state. How each signal is named The notation varies between manufacturers but the idea is the same: a type identifier, a byte and a bit. For example %I0.3 is the fourth input of the first byte, %Q1.0 the first output of the second byte, %M10.2 an internal marker and %IW64 an analog input word. What matters is not the syntax but the discipline: every signal has a symbolic name — arranque_bomba, nivel_alto —and the program is written with those names. A program full of %I and %Q with no symbols is indecipherable for whoever comes afterwards, who is almost never the person who wrote it."},{"t":"Input modules","d":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc#entradas","x":"03 Input modules Digital inputs They typically operate at 24 V DC; 110 and 220 V AC versions also exist. Each input has a filter of a few milliseconds that removes contact bounce and noise. That filter adds to the scan time. One LED indicator per channel shows the true state: it is the first diagnostic tool. Defined thresholds: below about 5 V is a sure zero, above about 15 V is a sure one. In between, undefined. PNP and NPN A PNP sensor delivers +24 V when it detects; an NPN one delivers 0 V. The input module has to be wired accordingly: common at 0 V for PNP sensors, common at +24 V for NPN. Mixing the two types in the same group of inputs is one of the most frequent wiring mistakes, and it produces inputs that stay always on or always off. Analog input Characteristics 4 to 20 mA The industrial standard. Being a current, it is not affected by the voltage drop in the cable, and the live zero makes it possible to tell “zero of the variable” from “cable cut.” 0 to 10 V Simple and economical, but sensitive to noise and cable length. For short runs inside the panel. Thermocouple and PT100 Dedicated modules with linearization and cold-junction compensation already handled internally. Resolution Usually 12 to 16 bits over the range. The program receives an integer that has to be scaled to the engineering unit. Worked example · Scaling an analog input A pressure transmitter of 0 to 10 bar delivers 4 to 20 mA. The PLC module converts that range to an integer from 0 to 27648. With a "},{"t":"Output modules","d":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc#salidas","x":"04 Output modules Type Current Speed When it is used Relay 2 to 5 A slow (ms) The most versatile: it works for DC and AC, and with a voltage-free contact. It wears out with switching operations. Transistor 0.5 to 2 A fast (µs) DC only. For solenoid valves, signaling and PWM or pulse outputs. Triac 0.5 to 2 A medium AC only, with no contacts to wear out. Good for loads that switch very often. What has to be added externally Flyback (freewheeling) diode across every DC coil, as close to it as possible. Without it, the turn-off spike destroys the output transistor. Snubber circuit —a resistor and capacitor in series—across AC coils. A fuse per group of outputs: a short circuit in the field must not take out the entire module. Interposing relays or contactors for large loads. The PLC output commands, it does not supply power. Respect the current per group, which is always lower than the sum of the maximum individual currents."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Lab 1 · Getting to know the unit On a real PLC: identify the power supply, CPU, modules and terminal blocks; read the nameplate; determine the supply voltage, the number and type of I/O, and the current available at the 24 V output. Draw the complete terminal diagram before connecting anything. Lab 2 · PNP and NPN Wire a PNP inductive sensor and an NPN one to the inputs, with the correct common in each case. Then swap them on purpose and observe the behavior: inputs always on or always off. Document how each case is recognized from the module’s LEDs. Lab 3 · Analog scaling Connect a potentiometer or a transmitter to an analog input and read the raw value. Write the conversion to engineering units and verify it with a multimeter at several points. Add wire-break detection below 4 mA. Lab 4 · Outputs and protection Drive a DC solenoid valve from a transistor output, with and without a freewheeling diode, observing the turn-off spike with the oscilloscope. Measure the reverse voltage that appears without the diode: it often exceeds one hundred volts."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause Inputs always on or always off PNP sensor wired as NPN, or common wrongly connected. Sensors that fail intermittently The internal 24 V supply is overloaded and the voltage drops. The transistor output burns out The freewheeling diode across the coil is missing. Outputs in a group that do not respond Group fuse blown, or total current above what is allowed. Noisy analog reading 0-10 V signal over a long cable or next to power wiring. Use 4-20 mA and shielded cable. The program thinks the pressure is zero Cable cut without detection: the live zero of 4-20 mA was not used. Data is lost when power is cut The variables were not in the retentive area. Program impossible to maintain Signals without symbolic names or comments."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the process image? The copy in memory of the state of all the inputs, taken at the start of the cycle. The program reads from there, never from the physical terminal, and so it works on a consistent snapshot. What is galvanic isolation used for in the I/O? So that there is no electrical connection between the field and the logic: the noise, overvoltages and potential differences of the plant do not reach the processor. Difference between a PNP and an NPN sensor. The PNP delivers +24 V when activated and the NPN delivers 0 V. The input module must be wired with the corresponding common in each case. Why is 4-20 mA preferred over 0-10 V? Because being a current it is not affected by the voltage drop in the cable, it resists noise better, and the live zero makes it possible to tell a variable at zero from a cut cable. Scale a reading of 6912 out of 27648 for a range of 0 to 10 bar.. What type of output is appropriate for an AC load that switches very often? A triac output: it has no contacts to wear out and it handles AC. A relay would pit with so many switching operations. What is added to a DC coil driven by a transistor output? A flyback (freewheeling) diode in antiparallel, as close to the coil as possible. Without it, the turn-off spike destroys the transistor. What is the retentive area of memory? The part that keeps its contents when the power is cut: accumulated production, setpoints, machine state. Why does the input filter increase the respon"},{"t":"Where we are coming from","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#de-donde","x":"01 Where we are coming from In microprocessor and microcontroller architecture we established what is inside: a CPU with an ALU and registers, memories, buses and peripherals, all in one chip. Now the point is to use one, and that means answering three very concrete questions: Which pin does what? The pinout and the multiplexing of functions: almost every pin serves several purposes and you have to choose. Which register configures it? Everything the chip does is decided by writing bits into special function registers. Programming a peripheral is, literally, putting ones and zeros in the right place. Which memory do I use? Flash for the program, SRAM for the variables, EEPROM for what must survive a power cut. Choosing wrong is expensive. Family Architecture Instructions Speed Where you see it Mid-range PIC 16F84A, 16F628A, 16F877A Harvard, RISC 35 20 MHz → 5 MIPS Argentine industry, equipment in service, plenty of documentation in Spanish High-end PIC 18F, dsPIC Harvard, RISC 75+ 40–70 MHz When more peripherals or more computing power are needed AVR ATmega328P, ATtiny85 Harvard, RISC 131 20 MHz → 20 MIPS Arduino. One cycle per instruction and free tools ARM Cortex-M STM32, RP2040 32-bit Thumb-2 48–480 MHz The current industry standard ESP32 32-bit + radio — 240 MHz Anything that needs WiFi or Bluetooth Why we study a small, old family Because in a mid-range PIC you can see everything: the memory map fits on one page, the registers are eight bits wide, there is no operating s"},{"t":"The pinout","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#patas","x":"02 The pinout It is the first useful page of any datasheet. It shows the package with its numbered pins and, for each one, all the functions it can perform, separated by slashes. Group Typical pins What to get right Power V DD / V CC y V SS / GND A 100 nF capacitor right next to each pair, and if there are several pairs, all of them connected. Without that capacitor the micro resets by itself. Reset MCLR̄ / RESET̄ Active low: it goes with a 10 kΩ resistor to the positive supply. Leaving it floating is the number one reason a micro “won’t start.” Oscillator OSC1/OSC2, XTAL1/XTAL2 Crystal with its two 22 pF capacitors, as close as possible. If the internal oscillator is used, those pins are free to use as I/O. Programming ICSP: MCLR, PGD, PGC · ISP: MOSI, MISO, SCK, RESET It pays to bring them out to a connector even if the project is final: reprogramming without desoldering is worth its weight in gold. Analog AN0…ANn, V REF At power-up, many pins start out configured as analog. If you want them digital, you have to say so explicitly. Peripherals TX/RX, SDA/SCL, SDO/SDI/SCK, CCP/PWM, INT They are fixed to certain pins: the printed circuit board has to respect them, or you have to use pin remapping if the chip offers it. The classic trap: analog pins by default On many PICs, after reset the ANSEL register (or ADCON1 ) leaves several pins as analog inputs. A program that reads them as digital always gets zero, with nothing to indicate the error. The first lines of any serious pro"},{"t":"Special function registers","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#registros","x":"03 Special function registers The CPU has no “instructions for turning on an LED.” What it has is access to a set of named memory locations —the SFRs — where each bit governs something in the hardware. All peripheral handling comes down to reading and writing those bits. // Three ways to write the same thing (AVR): set bit 5 of port B to 1 PORTB = PORTB | 0b00100000; // explicit, hard to read PORTB |= ( 1 << PB5); // the idiomatic form PORTB |= _BV(PB5); // with the library macro // And to clear it, the inverted mask PORTB &= ~( 1 << PB5); // Test an input bit if (PIND & ( 1 << PD2)) { /* pin 2 is high */ } These few lines are 90% of the work with registers. They come straight from the bitwise operators covered in the C language. How to read a register in the datasheet Each register appears with its eight bits, the name of each one, whether it is read, write or both, and its value after reset. That last item is the one most often overlooked: if a bit starts at 1, the peripheral is already doing something before the program says anything. The other important convention: an overbar over the name, or a trailing n ( MCLRn, CSn ), means active low. Read-modify-write PORTB |= (1<<PB5) doesn’t just write: it reads the port, changes one bit and writes it back. If another pin on the same port is loaded —an LED that pulls the voltage down, a pin that is still switching— what is read is not what had been written, and that pin changes state by itself. It is a famous PIC bug. The fix is t"},{"t":"Input and output ports","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#puertos","x":"04 Input and output ports Each digital pin is handled with two or three registers, and it is worth getting the mechanism clear because it is identical in all families, even though the names change. PIC AVR Function Direction TRISx DDRx Input or output Write PORTx / LATx PORTx Output level Read PORTx PINx Actual state of the pin Pull-up resistor WPUx / OPTION PORTx with DDR set to input Prevents floating inputs The two conventions are opposite: don’t mix them up On PIC, TRIS set to 1 is an input (the mnemonic: 1 looks like an I for input, 0 like an O for output ). On AVR, DDR set to 1 is an output. It is exactly the opposite, and it is the most common confusion for anyone moving from one family to the other. A pin configured as an output by mistake, connected to a pushbutton that pulls it to ground, gets destroyed. Example · How much current a pin can handle AVR: 40 mA absolute maximum per pin, 20 mA recommended, and 200 mA in total for the whole chip. Mid-range PIC: 25 mA per pin, and 200 mA summing the whole port. A red LED with a 2 V drop supplied from 5 V through 330 Ω draws: no problem at all. Eight LEDs on at once on the same port draw 73 mA: still within limits, but at 20 mA each it would be 160 mA and the chip heats up and the voltages drift. A 12 V, 30 mA relay, a motor or a lamp never connect directly: they need a transistor ( the transistor as a switch ) with its flyback diode."},{"t":"The three memories and the configuration bits","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#memorias","x":"05 The three memories and the configuration bits Memory PIC16F877A ATmega328P What it holds Flash 8 K words of 14 bits 32 KB The program. It is loaded with the programmer or with a bootloader. SRAM 368 B 2 KB Variables and stack. It is erased when power is removed. EEPROM 256 B 1 KB Configuration, calibration, counters: whatever must survive a power cut. 368 bytes of RAM are 368 bytes A 40-character text string eats 11% of the RAM of a 16F877A. An array of 100 16-bit integers doesn’t fit. The compiler doesn’t always warn you: the program compiles, gets programmed and fails in strange ways when the stack collides with the variables. That is why on small microcontrollers you use right-sized types ( uint8_t instead of int ), avoid strings and keep constants in Flash. Configuration bits: what is decided before the program runs They are called configuration bits on PIC and fuses on AVR. They are not data memory: they are programmed along with the program and define how the chip starts up. Clock source: crystal, resonator, internal oscillator, with or without the divide-by-8. It is the fuse that most often leaves a chip “dead”: if an external crystal is selected and there is no crystal, the micro doesn’t start and can’t be reprogrammed the normal way either. Watchdog: enabled or not, and with what timeout. Brown-out: at what voltage the chip resets if the supply drops. MCLR as reset or as an input pin. Code protection: prevents reading the Flash. It is used in a finished product; i"},{"t":"The instruction set","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#instrucciones","x":"06 The instruction set A mid-range PIC has 35 instructions. You don’t need to memorize them, but you do need to understand which classes exist and how long each thing takes, because the real timing of the program comes from there. Class Examples What they do Data transfer MOVLW, MOVWF, MOVF Move data between the accumulator (W) and memory. Arithmetic and logic ADDWF, SUBWF, ANDWF, XORWF Operate, leaving the result in W or in the register. Bit-oriented BSF, BCF, BTFSS, BTFSC Set, clear and test a bit. They are what make handling peripherals so convenient. Control GOTO, CALL, RETURN, RETFIE Jumps, subroutines and return from interrupt. Special CLRWDT, SLEEP, NOP Refresh the watchdog, sleep, waste a cycle on purpose. Example · How long a delay really takes A PIC with a 4 MHz crystal. The clock is divided by 4, so one instruction cycle lasts 1 µs. Almost all instructions take one cycle: 1 µs. The ones that jump —GOTO, CALL, and the test instructions when they actually skip— take two. A delay loop of three instructions repeated 250 times takes about 750 µs; by nesting two such loops you reach the millisecond with microsecond precision. That calculation is the reason a micro can guarantee a timing, something that is impossible on a computer with an operating system. It is also why loop delays get replaced by timers as soon as the program does more than one thing ( peripherals ). Assembly and C coexist Today almost everything is written in C, and that is fine: it is faster to write "},{"t":"Special features","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#especiales","x":"07 Special features Watchdog An independent counter that resets the chip if the program doesn’t refresh it in time. It is the safety net against a hang: an infinite loop caused by noise or unexpected data doesn’t leave the equipment dead, it restarts it. It is refreshed with CLRWDT (or wdt_reset() ) in the main loop, never inside an interrupt: if the main program hangs and the interrupt keeps running, the watchdog would never act. Brown-out reset It monitors the supply and holds the chip in reset while the voltage is below a threshold. Without it, when the voltage drops slowly the micro keeps executing with corrupted data: it writes garbage to the EEPROM and activates outputs at random. In equipment that handles power, enabling it is not optional. Low-power modes SLEEP stops the CPU clock and drops consumption from milliamperes to microamperes. The chip wakes up on an interrupt, on the watchdog or on a change on a pin. It is what lets a battery-powered sensor last for years. Power-on reset and startup The chip waits for the supply to stabilize and only then executes the first instruction, which is always at address 0. That delay is configurable and should be left enabled: without it, the micro starts before the rest of the circuit."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Lab 1 · The minimal circuit Build on a breadboard the bare minimum for the micro to live: power supply with a 100 nF capacitor, MCLR with its 10 kΩ resistor, crystal with its two capacitors and a programming connector. Program something that just turns on an LED. Then remove the decoupling capacitor and observe that the micro resets or runs erratically. It is the quickest demonstration of why it is there. Lab 2 · Registers by hand Without using any library, write the direction and level configuration of a whole port and build an 8-LED binary counter. Then read the datasheet and add the configuration of the analog pins so the ones that were missing work too. Write down which register solved each problem. Lab 3 · Measuring the clock Write a loop that toggles a pin as fast as possible and measure the frequency with the oscilloscope. From that measurement and the number of instructions in the loop, calculate the instruction time and compare it with the crystal. Repeat after changing the fuses to the internal oscillator and compare the stability. Lab 4 · Watchdog and low power Enable the watchdog and deliberately cause an infinite loop: the equipment must restart. Then measure the micro’s current while running and with SLEEP, waking it with a pushbutton. The difference —from tens of milliamperes to microamperes— explains by itself the design of any battery-powered device. Lab 5 · EEPROM Store a power-up counter in EEPROM and display it at startup. Cut and restore pow"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The micro won’t start and can’t be programmed either Fuses set for an external oscillator and no crystal on the circuit board. It is recovered with a programmer that injects a clock, or with high voltage. It resets by itself, especially when a relay is activated The 100 nF capacitor is missing, or the flyback diode, or the ground is badly distributed. A digital input always reads 0 The pin was left configured as analog by default. A pushbutton reads random values Floating input: the pull-up (internal or external) or the debouncing is missing. When writing one bit of a port, another pin changes by itself Read-modify-write on PORT with a load. Use LAT. The program compiles but behaves erratically SRAM has run out: the stack overwrites the variables. Check the memory map the compiler reports. It works powered by the programmer but not from its own power supply Insufficient or poorly filtered supply, or not all the V DD /V SS pairs are connected. The EEPROM filled up with corrupted data Writes during a voltage dip. Enable brown-out. The equipment hangs once a week No watchdog. With a watchdog it restarts by itself and there is also a record that it happened."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The microcontroller family","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment What is the difference between TRIS on a PIC and DDR on an AVR? The convention is opposite: on a PIC, TRIS set to 1 configures the pin as an input; on an AVR, DDR set to 1 configures it as an output. Mixing them up can destroy a pin that ends up as an output against a pushbutton to ground. Why is it better to use LAT instead of PORT for writing? Because writing to PORT involves first reading the actual state of the pins. If a pin is loaded, what is read doesn’t match what was written and that pin changes state. LAT returns the last value written, without looking at the pin. A PIC with a 4 MHz crystal: how long does an instruction cycle last? The clock is divided by four:, that is, 1 µs per instruction. Jump instructions take 2 µs. What are fuses or configuration bits and why are they dangerous? They define how the chip starts up —clock, watchdog, brown-out, MCLR function, code protection— and are programmed along with the program. If you select a clock that the circuit board doesn’t have, the micro doesn’t start and can’t be reprogrammed the usual way. How much current can a pin deliver and what do you do if you need more? On the order of 20 to 25 mA, with a total limit per chip of about 200 mA. For more, use a transistor or a MOSFET as a switch, with its base resistor and the flyback diode if the load is inductive. What is the watchdog for, and where must it NOT be refreshed? It resets the chip if the program stops refreshing it, getting it out of a hang. "},{"t":"How the light gets trapped","d":"Fiber optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica#principio","x":"01 How the light gets trapped A fiber is a glass core surrounded by a cladding with a slightly lower refractive index. When light reaches the boundary at a sufficiently shallow angle, it is totally reflected: nothing escapes. By repeating that reflection thousands of times per meter, the light travels kilometers confined in the core, even following the bends of the cable. The numerical aperture indicates the maximum angle from which light can be injected so that it stays guided; θ c is the critical angle beyond which total reflection occurs. Type Core Behavior and use Step-index multimode 50 to 100 µm Many paths with very different lengths: the pulse broadens quickly. Short spans and low speed. Graded-index multimode 50 / 62.5 µm The index decreases outward, so the rays that travel farther move faster and arrive almost together. Campus and building networks, up to a few hundred meters. Single-mode 8 to 10 µm The core is so thin that only one mode propagates. It is the fiber for long links and for the whole internet backbone. The outer diameter is always the same All communications fibers have 125 µm of cladding; what changes is the core. That is why connectors and tools are interchangeable, and why it is so easy to make a mistake: a single-mode and a multimode fiber look identical from the outside. They are told apart by the cable color and by the markings, not by eye."},{"t":"Attenuation, windows and dispersion","d":"Fiber optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica#perdidas","x":"02 Attenuation, windows and dispersion Window Wavelength Attenuation Use 1st 850 nm ≈ 3 dB/km Multimode, short spans. Cheap emitters. 2nd 1310 nm ≈ 0.35 dB/km Single-mode, minimum dispersion. Metropolitan networks. 3rd 1550 nm ≈ 0.20 dB/km Minimum attenuation. Long distance and optical amplification. The two causes of loss and the two of dispersion Absorption: impurities in the glass, above all traces of water (OH groups), which produce attenuation peaks at certain wavelengths. Rayleigh scattering: light is deflected by microscopic irregularities in the glass. It is stronger at short wavelengths, and it sets the attenuation floor. Modal dispersion: multimode only. The different paths take different times and the pulse broadens. Chromatic dispersion: each color travels at a slightly different speed. It limits very high speeds in single-mode and is compensated with specific fibers and modules. Worked example · Optical link budget A 40 km single-mode link at 1550 nm, with 4 fusion splices and 2 connectors at each end. Transmitter of 0 dBm, receiver with a sensitivity of −28 dBm. Item Loss Fiber: 8.0 dB Splices: 0.4 dB Connectors: 2.0 dB Reserve margin for repairs and aging 3.0 dB Total 13.4 dB Received power:, far above the −28 dBm of sensitivity. The link has 14.6 dB of margin: more than enough. With those numbers one could even use a cheaper transmitter or extend the distance. Too much light can also arrive Unlike radio, in fiber there is a maximum: if the received power excee"},{"t":"Emitters, receivers and multiplexing","d":"Fiber optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica#componentes","x":"03 Emitters, receivers and multiplexing Emitters LED: cheap, wide spectrum, low power. Multimode and low speed only. Laser: narrow spectrum, high power and extremely fast switching. It is what makes long-distance single-mode and high speeds possible. Receivers PIN photodiode: simple, stable, the most widely used. Avalanche photodiode (APD): has internal gain and therefore more sensitivity, at the cost of more noise and of needing high voltage. Both are direct applications of the devices covered in special semiconductors: the same LED and the same photodiode, taken to the extreme of speed and spectral purity. Wavelength-division multiplexing Dozens of simultaneous channels can travel over the same fiber, each one in a different color, separated at the end by optical filters. It is what allows the capacity of an already installed fiber to be multiplied without touching the cable run: you swap the equipment at the ends and that is it. In dense systems, the channels are spaced less than a nanometer apart. And at 1550 nm there are optical amplifiers that amplify the light directly, without converting it to an electrical signal. Thanks to them a submarine cable crosses an ocean with simple repeaters every few tens of kilometers."},{"t":"Termination, splicing and measurement","d":"Fiber optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica#instalacion","x":"04 Termination, splicing and measurement Task Typical loss Details Fusion splice 0.02 to 0.10 dB The fibers are stripped, cleaned and cleaved with a precision cleaver, and the fusion splicer aligns them and joins them with an electric arc. It is the permanent method. Mechanical splice 0.1 to 0.5 dB Alignment with index-matching gel. Fast, for temporary repairs. Connector (SC, LC, FC, ST) 0.2 to 0.5 dB Flat, PC, UPC or APC polish. APC connectors, green, have the end face at an angle to reduce reflection. Instruments Power meter and light source: measures the total loss of the span. It is the measurement that certifies a link. OTDR: shows the profile along the fiber and locates the fault to the meter. Visual fault locator: a red laser that can be seen through the cladding; it is used to identify fibers and find nearby breaks. Inspection scope: for viewing the connector end face. The number one cause of problems is, simply, dirt. Safety Never look into the end of a fiber or of an active connector: 1310 and 1550 nm light is invisible and can damage the retina before the blink reflex kicks in. Check with a meter, never with the eye. The fiber scraps from cleaving are slivers of glass a few microns thick: they embed in the skin, cannot be seen and do not come out. Pick them up with adhesive tape and discard them in a rigid container. The isopropyl alcohol used for cleaning is flammable, and the fusion splicer generates an electric arc."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Fiber optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Lab 1 · Homemade light guide Before touching the fiber: shine a laser beam into a stream of water falling from a container. The light follows the curve of the stream by total internal reflection. It is the historic demonstration of the principle, and it makes visible what will later happen inside the glass. Lab 2 · Measuring a link With a source and a power meter, measure the loss of a patch cord, of a spool and of a link with splices. Calculate the attenuation per kilometer and compare it with the fiber specification. Repeat after cleaning the connectors: the difference is often surprising. Lab 3 · Splicing Strip, clean, cleave and fuse two fibers. Measure the splice loss and repeat until it is below 0.1 dB. Note what happens when the cleave is angled or when dirt is present: the fusion splicer reports it and the loss confirms it. Lab 4 · Bending and fault Measure the received power while bending the fiber with ever smaller radii: macrobend loss appears. Then, with an OTDR, locate a splice and a forced bend along the spool, and check the reported distance with a tape measure."},{"t":"Common errors","d":"Fiber optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica#errores","x":"06 Common errors Symptom Usual cause High loss on a new link Dirty connectors. It is the most common cause of all, and it is solved by cleaning. Unstable link that gets worse when the cable is moved Excessive bending, a crushed cable or a loose connector. The equipment does not link even though plenty of light arrives Receiver saturated: an attenuator is missing. Or different fiber types at each end. Single-mode was mixed with multimode They look the same from the outside. Read the cable markings and respect the colors. High loss in a splice Angled cleave, dirt, or worn electrodes in the fusion splicer. The OTDR shows a huge peak at the start Dead zone of the instrument itself: a launch cable is used to measure the first connector properly. Total outage with no apparent cause Cable cut by third-party construction work. The OTDR tells you how many meters away it is."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Fiber optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What keeps the light inside the core? Total internal reflection: the core has a higher refractive index than the cladding, and rays striking at an angle greater than the critical angle are reflected completely. Difference between multimode and single-mode fiber. Multimode has a large core (50 to 62.5 µm) and admits many paths, which broadens the pulse; single-mode has a core of 8 to 10 µm and admits only one, so it is suited to long distances and high speeds. Why is 1550 nm used for long distance? Because it is the window of minimum attenuation, around 0.20 dB/km, and because there are optical amplifiers that work in that band. What is the numerical aperture? The maximum angle at which light can be injected so that it stays guided. It depends on the difference in index between core and cladding. Budget for a 20 km link at 1310 nm with 2 splices and 4 connectors. of fiber; of splices; of connectors; plus 3 dB of margin: total 12.2 dB. Can “too much” light reach the receiver? Yes: above its maximum power the receiver saturates and the error rate increases. It is corrected with an optical attenuator. What is an OTDR for, and what does its trace show? To see the profile of the fiber along its length. The slope is the attenuation per kilometer, the steps are splices, the peaks are connectors or reflections, and the abrupt end indicates a break, along with its distance. What is wavelength-division multiplexing? Transmitting several channels over the same fiber, e"},{"t":"The idea: frequency as the criterion","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#idea","x":"01 The idea: frequency as the criterion An amplifier makes the signal bigger. A rectifier turns it into DC. A filter does something different: it chooses. And it can choose because capacitors and inductors present a reactance that depends on frequency, which resistors do not. Everything follows from these two lines A capacitor opposes current less the higher the frequency; an inductor opposes it more. A divider made of a resistor and one of them is already a filter, and which side the output is taken from decides whether it is low-pass or high-pass. Type What it passes Concrete example Low-pass From DC up to f c Power supply filter; anti-aliasing ahead of an A/D converter; the woofer. High-pass From f c upward Coupling capacitor between stages; the tweeter of a speaker cabinet. Band-pass Only a band around f 0 Radio tuning; 38 kHz remote-control receiver; equalizer. Band-stop (notch) Everything except a band Removing the 50 Hz hum from a biomedical or audio signal."},{"t":"The first-order RC filter","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#rc","x":"02 The first-order RC filter It is the simplest filter there is: a resistor and a capacitor. It is worth knowing by heart because it shows up everywhere, often without anyone having put it there on purpose: the output resistance of a stage and the capacitance of the cable form a low-pass filter even though nobody designed it. With and:. The same formula works for the low-pass and for the high-pass: what changes is where the output is taken from. What happens exactly at the cutoff frequency The reactance of the capacitor equals the resistance:. The output falls to 0.707 of the input, which is −3 dB. Power falls to half: that is why it is also called the half-power frequency. The phase shift is 45°. And far from the cutoff, the slope is −20 dB per decade, or what amounts to the same, −6 dB per octave: every time the frequency is multiplied by ten, the signal is divided by ten. Worked example · Attenuation at different frequencies First-order RC low-pass with: Frequency V out / V in In decibels 100 Hz 0.995 −0.04 dB 1 kHz (f c ) 0.707 −3 dB 2 kHz 0.447 −7.0 dB 10 kHz 0.0995 −20 dB 100 kHz 0.00995 −40 dB The practical conclusion: a first-order filter does not separate well. To attenuate by a factor of a hundred you have to go two decades beyond the cutoff. If you need to reject something that is close to what you want to keep, you need a higher order. Decibels turn what are multiplications in plain ratios into sums, and that is why they are always used for filters: two stages of "},{"t":"Order, slope and shape of the curve","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#orden","x":"03 Order, slope and shape of the curve The order of a filter is the number of reactive elements that determine its response. Each order adds 20 dB per decade of slope. Order Slope Attenuation one decade later How it is built 1 −20 dB/dec 10 times An RC or an RL 2 −40 dB/dec 100 times An LC, or an active Sallen-Key 3 −60 dB/dec 1000 times A second-order section plus a first-order one 4 −80 dB/dec 10,000 times Two second-order stages in cascade Putting two RC filters in cascade does not give a proper second order Each stage loads the previous one, so the resulting curve is not the expected one: the cutoff shifts and the transition becomes softer, not sharper. There are two ways to fix it: separate the stages with a voltage follower, or use an active filter directly, which is what the Sallen-Key topology does. The shape of the curve: not all second orders are the same With the same order you can favor different things, and each choice has its own name: Butterworth Passband as flat as possible. It is the default choice when there is no strong reason for anything else. in the second order. Chebyshev Falls faster in exchange for accepting ripple in the passband. It is used when something very close to the useful band has to be rejected. Bessel Prioritizes phase: it delays all frequencies equally, so it does not distort pulses. It is the one of choice for square waves and data. The Q factor in a filter With low Q the curve is soft and has no peak; with it is flat (Butterworth); with"},{"t":"Passive LC and active op amp filters","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#lc-activos","x":"04 Passive LC and active op amp filters Passive LC filters They need no power supply and handle power: they are the only possible ones at the output of a transmitter or in a speaker crossover. They work at any frequency, including hundreds of MHz. Their response depends on the load impedance: the same filter behaves differently with 8 Ω than with 4 Ω. In audio, the inductors required are large, expensive and lossy. Active filters With an op amp, resistors and capacitors: no inductors. They can have gain as well as filter, and their output is low-impedance: they can be chained without loading each other. They allow high Q without bulky components. Limited by the bandwidth of the op amp: they are no good at RF. And they need a power supply. Second-order Sallen-Key. With equal components (, ) it reduces to, and Q is set by the gain of the op amp. Worked example · Anti-aliasing filter for an A/D converter The sampling rate is 10 kSa/s, so everything above 5 kHz has to be rejected. A cutoff of 3 kHz is chosen to leave some margin. With first order, at 5 kHz the attenuation is barely −5.8 dB: a little more than half of the unwanted signal gets through. Not good enough. With a fourth-order Butterworth (two Sallen-Key stages in cascade), the attenuation at 5 kHz is about −18 dB, and at 10 kHz −42 dB. That is already acceptable. Components for with: → commercial value 5.6 kΩ, or 5.1 kΩ if you prefer to stay above the frequency. E12 series values can be looked up with the resistor calc"},{"t":"Band-pass and band-stop","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#banda","x":"05 Band-pass and band-stop They can be built in two ways: by chaining a high-pass with a low-pass (wide band) or by taking advantage of the resonance of an LC (narrow band). By resonance A parallel LC presents maximum impedance at resonance: placed in series with the signal, it passes only that band. A series LC does the opposite: it presents minimum impedance, and placed to ground it drags that band down to the floor. That is the band-stop. 50 Hz notch The most requested case: removing the mains hum from a small signal. An active twin-T network is used, which gives deep, narrow rejection. You have to be careful with the width: if the notch is too wide, it also takes away useful information from the signal, and in an electrocardiogram that distorts the trace. Worked example · AM radio tuning A station at 1 MHz with a tank circuit of:: that is why the variable capacitor of an AM radio goes up to about 400 pF, just enough to cover the whole band. With a Q of 100, the bandwidth is: exactly one AM channel fits and the adjacent one is rejected. That is exactly the trade-off of a tuner: with Q too high the highs of the modulation are lost; with low Q, the neighboring station leaks in."},{"t":"Where filters show up, even if nobody calls them that","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#donde","x":"06 Where filters show up, even if nobody calls them that Circuit What filter it really is Filter capacitor of a power supply Low-pass: passes the DC and blocks the 100 Hz ripple. Coupling capacitor between stages High-pass: blocks the bias DC and lets the signal through. 100 nF capacitor next to each IC Low-pass toward the supply: sends fast noise to ground. Tone control of an amplifier Adjustable low-pass and high-pass. Anti-aliasing before an A/D converter Low-pass, and it is mandatory: what gets in wrong can no longer be taken out. Remote-control receiver Band-pass centered at 38 kHz that separates the signal from ambient light. Antenna and tuner High-Q band-pass, see impedance measurements. Snubber of a triac Filter that limits dV/dt ( special semiconductors ). Averaging readings in a program A digital low-pass: the moving average is exactly that."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Lab 1 · Curve of an RC, point by point and. With the generator at constant amplitude, measure the output at 100, 200, 500 Hz, 1, 1.6, 3, 5, 10, 30 and 100 kHz. Plot the ratio in dB on semilogarithmic paper. Check that at 1592 Hz the output is 0.707 and that between 10 and 100 kHz the curve drops 20 dB. Also measure the phase shift at the cutoff: it should be 45°. Lab 2 · The cascade that is not a second order Measure the curve of an RC, then that of two identical RCs connected one after the other, and finally that of the two separated by an op amp follower. Compare the three curves: the shift of the cutoff in the middle case is the loading effect, and it is plainly visible on the graph. Lab 3 · Sallen-Key active filter Build a second-order low-pass with a TL081, and. Plot the curve and check the slope of 40 dB per decade. Then vary the gain with a trimmer and watch how a peak appears before the cutoff as Q goes up: it is the same phenomenon that makes a circuit oscillate if you go too far. Lab 4 · Filtering out the hum Add to a 1 kHz signal a little 50 Hz taken from a step-down transformer. Listen to the result and look at it on the oscilloscope. Then pass the mix through a high-pass with and compare: the hum disappears and the signal stays intact. It is the real solution to a very common audio problem. Lab 5 · Passive filter with a square wave Inject a 1 kHz square wave into the RC of Lab 1 and observe the output: it gets rounded. Lower the cutoff frequency and"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The measured cutoff does not match the calculated one Capacitor tolerance, or the impedance of the next stage loading the filter. Two filters in cascade do not give the expected slope Loading effect between stages: you need to separate them with a follower or use an active filter. A peak appears just before the cutoff Q too high because of excess gain in the Sallen-Key. Adjust the feedback ratio. The active filter does not work at high frequency The bandwidth of the op amp has been exceeded. There you have to go to passive LC filters. The low-pass filter still lets fast noise through The capacitor is above its self-resonance and behaves like an inductor. Add a small ceramic one in parallel. The speaker crossover sounds hollow at the crossover point Phase cancellation between the two ways. In second order the tweeter polarity has to be reversed. The signal is distorted even though the amplitudes are right Phase distortion: each harmonic arrives with a different delay. If that matters, the filter must be a Bessel. The 50 Hz notch takes away part of the signal Q too low: the rejection is wide. It has to be narrowed, even though it becomes more sensitive to component drift."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Filters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What happens exactly at the cutoff frequency of an RC filter?, the output falls to 0.707 of the input (−3 dB), the power falls to half and the phase shift is 45°. and: what is the cutoff frequency?. A third-order filter, one decade past the cutoff: how much does it attenuate? 60 dB, which is 1000 times in voltage. Each order contributes 20 dB per decade. Why do two RC filters in cascade not give a correct second order? Because the second stage loads the first: the impedance of one changes the response of the other. The cutoff shifts and the transition becomes softer. They are separated with a voltage follower, or an active topology such as Sallen-Key is used. A band-pass filter centered at 455 kHz with: what bandwidth does it have?. It is the typical value of the intermediate-frequency filters of an AM radio. When is a Bessel preferable to a Butterworth? When the waveform matters and not just the amplitude: square waves, pulses, data. A Bessel delays all frequencies equally, so it does not distort the pulse, at the cost of a slower roll-off. Why does an anti-aliasing filter have to be analog? Because it has to act before sampling. Afterward, the high frequency has already been folded into the useful band as a false signal indistinguishable from a real one: no digital filter can separate it. What kind of filter is, at bottom, the coupling capacitor between two stages? A first-order high-pass, formed by that capacitor and the input impedance of the next stage"},{"t":"Linear versus switching","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#lineal-conmutada","x":"01 Linear versus switching The linear supply was already covered with the LM317 regulator: mains transformer, rectifier, filter and a regulator that dissipates the difference between input and output. Example · What a linear supply wastes A regulator delivering 5 V and 2 A from a 12 V input: Useful output:. The regulator has across it, with 2 A: it dissipates. Efficiency, and a heat sink for 14 W is needed. The same output from a switching supply at 90 % draws 11 W from the input and dissipates 1 W. The difference is not an improvement: it is another order of magnitude. Linear Switching Efficiency 30 to 60 % 80 to 95 % Size and weight Large: 50 Hz transformer Small: ferrite core at tens of kHz ( transformers ) Electrical noise Very low High: it switches thousands of times per second Response to load changes Very fast Depends on the control loop Complexity Low High: feedback loop, protections, filters Where linear supplies are still used High-accuracy analog stages, reference-grade audio, power for noise-sensitive sensors"},{"t":"The basic topologies","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#topologias","x":"02 The basic topologies They all do the same thing: they switch at high frequency and use an inductor to store energy and deliver it at the right moment. What changes is the arrangement. Topology Output Isolated Used in Buck No Stepping voltage down efficiently: on-board regulators, chargers Boost No Stepping voltage up: LED backlights, power factor correction Buck-boost Inverts the polarity No When the input can be above or below the output. This is the classic version (the inverting buck-boost); there are variants that do not invert the polarity, such as the SEPIC Flyback Set by the turns ratio Yes Phone chargers, supplies up to ~100 W. The most widespread Forward / half bridge / full bridge Set by the turns ratio Yes PC power supplies, welders, industrial chargers of hundreds of watts Example · Designing a buck converter 24 V input, 12 V output at 3 A, switching at 100 kHz. Duty cycle:. Inductor current: its average value equals the output current, 3 A, with a typical ripple of 30 %: 0.9 A peak to peak. Inductance:. The commercial 68 µH value is chosen, checking that it can handle 3.5 A without saturating. Diode: it conducts 50 % of the time; a Schottky is chosen for its low drop. Output capacitor: chosen for the allowed ripple and, above all, for its ESR ( impedance measurements ): the ESR multiplied by the ripple current is most of the voltage ripple. Core saturation If the current through the inductor exceeds the value at which its core saturates, the inductance disappe"},{"t":"Controlled rectifiers","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#rectificadores","x":"03 Controlled rectifiers When power is taken directly from the mains and has to be regulated, thyristors are used instead of diodes. The firing angle sets the DC output voltage. Full single-phase bridge and three-phase bridge. V L is the line-to-line RMS voltage. Example · Single-phase and three-phase, with numbers Single-phase bridge on 220 V with:. With:. Three-phase bridge on 380 V with:. With:. The three-phase bridge also delivers a much cleaner DC output: six pulses per cycle instead of two, so the ripple is smaller and at a higher frequency, and the filter ends up smaller. When α goes past 90°: the rectifier becomes an inverter At angles greater than 90°, the cosine becomes negative and so does the average voltage: if the load can deliver energy —a DC motor braking, for example— the circuit returns it to the mains. This mode is called inversion and it is the basis of regenerative braking in traction. The energy of a braking train goes back to the line instead of turning into heat."},{"t":"Inverters","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#inversores","x":"04 Inverters They do the reverse: DC to AC. Four switches in a bridge chop the DC voltage and, by modulating the pulse width, build a sine wave. Output type What it looks like Where it is used Square wave Two levels. Many harmonics. Very simple equipment. Harms motors and switching supplies. Modified square wave Three levels, with a step at zero. Low-cost UPS units. Acceptable for resistive loads. Sinusoidal PWM Pulses of variable width plus a filter. Quality UPS units, solar inverters, variable-speed drives. Two parameters that define a PWM inverter Modulation index: the amplitude of the reference sine wave relative to that of the carrier. It controls the output voltage. Carrier frequency: typically 2 to 16 kHz. The higher it is, the easier to filter and the quieter the motor, but the greater the switching losses. The reference frequency is the output frequency: that is what makes it possible to vary the speed of a motor. Dead time is not optional In each leg of the bridge there are two switches in series between the positive and the negative rail. If for a moment both conduct, the result is a short circuit of the DC source that destroys them immediately. That is why the drive introduces a dead time —microseconds long— between turning one off and turning the other on. Every bridge driver IC includes it, and in a custom design it is the first thing to check on the oscilloscope."},{"t":"Firing and drive circuits","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#disparo","x":"05 Firing and drive circuits For thyristors Synchronization with the mains, isolation by pulse transformer or optocoupler, and enough energy in the pulse. With inductive loads, a pulse train ( automatic control systems ). For MOSFETs and IGBTs They need a driver: the gate is capacitive and has to be charged and discharged with amperes of peak current for the switching to be fast. The high-side switches of the bridge are floating, so their drive is solved with a bootstrap circuit or an isolated supply. A badly driven gate ruins the efficiency If the driver is weak, the transition drags on: during that time the device has voltage and current at the same time, and the switching losses skyrocket. An IGBT that should switch in 200 ns and takes 2 µs dissipates ten times more. Many supplies that “heat up for no reason” have this problem, not a failed component."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Measured efficiency Power the same load (12 V, 1 A) with a 7812 linear regulator from 20 V and with a switching buck module. Measure input voltage and current in both cases and calculate the efficiency. Touch both: the linear one burns, the switching one is lukewarm. Note the power dissipated in each. Lab 2 · Seeing inside the buck converter On a buck module, use the oscilloscope to measure the voltage at the switching node —you see the square wave—, the inductor current with a current probe or a shunt resistor, and the output ripple with AC coupling. Check that D matches V o /V i and vary the load to see how the loop adjusts D. Lab 3 · Controlled rectifier With an isolation transformer and reduced voltage, build a controlled single-phase bridge and measure the average voltage for α = 0°, 45°, 90° and 135°. Compare with the formula. Repeat with an inductive load and observe the difference in the current waveform. Lab 4 · Dead time On a low-voltage H-bridge, use two channels to measure the drive signals of the upper and lower switch of one leg and check the dead time. Reduce it gradually and watch the peak of the supply current: it is the demonstration of why dead time exists, done without destroying anything."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The switching supply whistles It is running in burst mode because of a light load, or the core vibrates from magnetostriction. Often normal with no load. Dies as soon as it is asked for current Inductor saturating, or undersized switch. Lots of output ripple Capacitor with high ESR, or the capacitor is far from the switching point. Interferes with the radio and with other equipment Input filter missing or badly placed, and large ground loops on the board ( PCB ). Gets very hot for no apparent reason Weak driver: slow transitions and high switching losses. The bridge is destroyed at start-up Insufficient or zero dead time: the two switches of a leg conduct at the same time. The controlled rectifier voltage does not match the formula The load is inductive and there is conduction during part of the negative half-cycle, or the freewheeling diode is missing. The inverter drives the motor with noise and vibration Carrier too low, or square-wave output instead of sinusoidal PWM."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Power supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment A linear regulator delivers 5 V and 1.5 A from 15 V. How much does it dissipate and what is its efficiency? It dissipates and delivers 7.5 W: efficiency. Why can a switching supply be so small? Because it works at tens or hundreds of kHz instead of 50 Hz: the transformer core and the filters needed are much smaller, since the energy per cycle is far lower. In a buck converter with 24 V input and, what is the output?. What happens if a converter's inductor saturates? It stops behaving as an inductor: the current grows almost without limit in each cycle and destroys the switch. That is why the inductor is chosen by its saturation current. Three-phase bridge on 380 V with: what is the DC voltage?. What does it mean for a controlled rectifier to operate with α greater than 90°? That its average voltage becomes negative and the circuit returns energy to the mains: it works as an inverter. This is what makes regenerative braking possible, if the load can deliver energy. How is a sine wave built with PWM? By comparing a reference sine wave with a triangular carrier of much higher frequency. The output switches at the carrier frequency with variable width, and its average value follows the sine wave. A filter lets only that component through. What is dead time for in a bridge? To ensure that the two switches of the same leg never conduct at the same time. Without it, the DC source is short-circuited through the two switches, which are destroyed immediately. Why doe"},{"t":"The synchronous alternator in service","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#alternador","x":"01 The synchronous alternator in service All serious generation, from a dam to a construction-site set, relies on the same principle: a rotating electromagnet (the rotor, fed with direct excitation current) induces an alternating voltage in the fixed windings of the stator ( Magnetism and electromagnetism ). It is synchronous because the frequency is tied to the speed: f = p · n / 60, with p pole pairs. The relation and the speed table for 50 Hz are in Electrical energy; here it is enough to remember that a common four-pole set turns at 1,500 rpm. Rotor type What it looks like Where it is used Salient pole Many poles standing out from the shaft, large diameter and short length. Turns slowly. Hydro generators and 1,500 rpm generating sets. Round (cylindrical) rotor Solid slotted cylinder, two or four poles, long and slender. Withstands the centrifugal force at 3,000 rpm. Steam and gas turbo-generators. In modern sets the excitation is provided without brushes or slip rings: an exciter (a small alternator whose armature rotates with the rotor) feeds alternating current to a rotating diode bridge, which supplies the main field. Regulating the exciter current regulates the whole generator. Many machines add a permanent-magnet generator to power the regulator, so the alternator keeps its excitation even when the output voltage collapses in a short circuit. Two variables, two regulators In a set there are two things to control, and they hardly affect each other. The voltage is set "},{"t":"Turbines: types and characteristics","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#turbinas","x":"02 Turbines: types and characteristics The turbine is the prime mover of large plants: it converts the energy of a moving fluid —steam, hot gas or water— into the rotation of a shaft. It is more compact and smoother than a reciprocating engine, but it needs a pressurized fluid to be available; at small ratings the reciprocating engine dominates. The plants of each kind and their efficiencies are in Electrical energy; here the point is what each turbine looks like and what characterizes it. Turbine Fluid and principle Characteristics Regulated by Steam Steam from a boiler or a reactor expands in nozzles and blades. It can be impulse (the steam is accelerated in fixed nozzles and the rotor only receives the impulse) or reaction (the expansion continues in the moving blades). Large ones combine both, in several casings. Runs at 3,000 rpm (two poles) or 1,500 rpm (four). A condensing turbine uses the maximum pressure drop; a back-pressure turbine delivers steam at medium pressure for a process (cogeneration). Valves that regulate the admitted steam. Gas Brayton cycle: a compressor raises the air pressure, a combustion chamber heats it by burning fuel, and the gases expand in the turbine. More than half of the turbine work is spent driving the compressor. Starts fast (minutes), from tens of kW (microturbines) to hundreds of MW. The very hot exhaust feeds the combined cycle. Fuel flow. Pelton (hydraulic) Impulse: one or more high-speed water jets hit buckets on the rim of the wheel"},{"t":"Internal combustion engines, fuels and lubricants","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#motores","x":"03 Internal combustion engines, fuels and lubricants Below a few MW, the usual drive for a generator is a reciprocating internal combustion engine: pistons that turn the pressure of burned fuel into crankshaft rotation. In practice there are two families: Diesel engine (compression ignition): it draws in and compresses only air, with a compression ratio of about 16:1 to 22:1; at the end of the compression the air is so hot that the injected diesel ignites on its own. It is the engine of medium-power and emergency sets: it consumes little, withstands a lot of work and its fuel is less flammable. Otto engine (spark ignition): it compresses an air-fuel mixture (petrol, natural gas or LPG) with a compression ratio of 8:1 to 12:1 and a spark plug ignites it. It is the engine of small portable sets (1 to 10 kVA) and of gas-fuelled sets. Both are usually four-stroke: intake, compression, expansion (the only one that delivers work) and exhaust, in two crankshaft turns. The engine must hold the alternator speed (1,500 rpm) even when the load changes: that is the governor’s job. The shaft power is the product of torque and angular speed: P in W, M (torque) in N·m, n in rpm. At 1,500 rpm, 40 kW corresponds to a torque of 255 N·m. The efficiency of the engine is obtained from the specific consumption b (grams of fuel burned per kWh delivered at the shaft) and the heating value of the fuel: b: specific consumption in kg/kWh; LHV: lower heating value of the fuel in MJ/kg (3.6 MJ = 1 kWh). "},{"t":"The medium and small generating set","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#grupo","x":"04 The medium and small generating set A generating set (motor-generator) is an engine and an alternator coupled on a common bedplate, with their auxiliaries and a control panel. “Small” is the portable or site set, a few kVA, almost always single-phase and petrol-fuelled; “medium” is the three-phase diesel set, fixed or trailer-mounted, from some tens to a few hundred kVA, which backs up a building, a plant or an antenna site. Part What it is for What is checked in maintenance Bedplate and mounts Rigid frame joining engine and alternator; anti-vibration mounts that isolate the floor. Coupling alignment, condition of the mounts, fasteners. Engine Delivers the torque to the shaft. Levels, belts, leaks, filters, running hours. Alternator and voltage regulator Converts the rotation into three-phase voltage. Insulation, terminals, cleanliness of ventilation, AVR condition. Cooling Carries off the heat of engine and alternator. Coolant level and concentration, hoses, radiator free of dirt. Fuel Base tank, day tank, filters and drain. Level, water at the bottom, filters, tank venting. Exhaust Takes the gases to a safe place. Tightness, thermal insulation, silencer. Starting Batteries, charger and starter. Battery voltage, electrolyte density, charger, terminals. Control panel Starts and stops; measures and protects (oil pressure, temperature, overspeed, failure to start). Alarms, emergency stop, sensor calibration. Coolant heater Keeps the engine warm so it starts quickly and takes"},{"t":"Paralleling and synchronizing","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#paralelo","x":"05 Paralleling and synchronizing Two or more sets can feed the same busbar. It is done for redundancy (if one fails, the other keeps running), to be able to maintain a set without cutting the service, to grow by adding a machine, and so that only the necessary sets run, close to their best efficiency. The price is complexity: the breaker of each set must be closed at a precise instant and then the load must be shared. The four conditions to close A generator can be connected to a live busbar only when the voltage (RMS), the frequency, the phase sequence and the phase angle all match those of the busbar. The sequence is checked once, at commissioning, with a phase-sequence meter; the other three are adjusted every time, and watched with a voltmeter, a frequency meter and a synchroscope, whose pointer turns at the speed of the frequency difference (toward “fast” if the incoming set is faster) and points to 12 o’clock when the waves are in phase. There are two ways to do it. Manual synchronizing uses the synchroscope, or three lamps: in the “dark lamp” method the set is closed when all three go out together; if they are cross-connected the light “rotates” and its direction of rotation says whether the set is faster or slower. Automatic synchronizing is done by a controller that adjusts the speed and the voltage and closes the breaker when the conditions are met and hold for a few moments (a synchronism-check relay). Closing out of synchronism breaks machines If the breaker is cl"},{"t":"Sizing, testing and protecting a set","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#dimensionado","x":"06 Sizing, testing and protecting a set Choosing a set is solving a problem with several possible answers; a small one sags when motors start, an enormous one always runs at low load and gets sick. The method has six steps: Survey the loads the set must feed (the essential ones, or the whole installation): power, power factor, efficiency and type of starting. Add kW and kvar; the total apparent power is not the sum of the apparent powers, but the composition of the active and reactive ones. Add a reserve margin (for growth and to avoid running at full load), of about 20 %. Check the starting of the largest motor: the transient voltage dip must be acceptable. Correct for altitude and temperature and choose the duty category (standby or prime) and the next commercial size. Check the fuel and the runtime the service requires. Example · A backup set for a workshop A workshop, supplied at 400/230 V, needs backup for: lighting and sockets, 9 kW at power factor 0.95; a compressor with a 7.5 kW motor (shaft output, efficiency 0.88, power factor 0.84); and a pump with an 11 kW motor (efficiency 0.89, power factor 0.85). The set is a diesel at 1,500 rpm, with x ′ d = 0.20 and an alternator efficiency of 0.905. Choose its rating. Load Electrical P (kW) Power factor S (kVA) Q (kvar) Lighting and sockets 9.00 0.95 9.47 2.96 Compressor: 7.5 / 0.88 8.52 0.84 10.14 5.50 Pump: 11 / 0.89 12.36 0.85 14.54 7.66 The active and reactive powers are added; the apparent power is composed: A 20 % rese"},{"t":"In the workshop","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#taller","x":"07 In the workshop Practice 1 · Survey a generating set Materials: a workshop set (stopped, with the batteries disconnected), form, flashlight, multimeter, insulation tester. Steps: copy the nameplates of the engine and the alternator (power, voltage, current, power factor, rpm, duty category); identify each system in the table of section 04; check oil, coolant and fuel levels; measure battery voltage and density; measure the alternator insulation between phases and to frame (method in generator testing ). Acceptance criterion: complete form, no fluid leaks, insulation above the minimum of the IEEE 43 rule ( U n in kV plus 1 MΩ: 1.4 MΩ for 400 V) and charged batteries. Practice 2 · Load test with a resistor bank Materials: set, three-phase resistive load bank, network analyzer or clamp with voltmeter and frequency meter, fuel measuring cylinder or flow meter, infrared thermometer. Steps: start the set and let it run unloaded for a couple of minutes; load it in steps of 25, 50, 75 and 100 %; at each step wait for stabilization and record voltage, frequency, currents, kW, oil pressure, coolant temperature and consumption; compute the g/kWh of each step; reduce the load and let it cool. Acceptance criterion: voltage within ±5 % of 400 V and frequency between 49 and 51 Hz at all steps (the contractual value is that of the manufacturer’s specification); no alarms; consumption close to the catalogue value. Practice 3 · Transfer switchboard sequence Materials: training ATS with a lo"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Sizing by kW only (or by kVA only) A set with an alternator to spare and an engine that falls short, or the reverse: the voltage sags or the machine overheats. Forgetting motor starting The voltage drops too much, contactors drop out and the motor does not start: the load is left without service just when it is needed. Using the emergency rating for daily service The engine works above what the manufacturer guarantees and ages quickly. Testing the emergency set at no load Soot and unburned diesel build up; when needed, it will not take the load. It is tested under load. Connecting the set to the installation with a plug or a jumper Backfeed to the utility grid: risk of death for whoever works on the line. Closing the breaker of a paralleled set without watching the synchroscope Short-circuit current and a mechanical blow to the coupling: broken shafts and windings. Leaving the tank nearly empty and undrained No fuel at the moment of the outage, and water and sediment that clog the filters. Changing the oil only by running hours On an emergency set the hours are few, but the oil ages anyway: it is also changed by time. Installing the set in an unventilated room Overheating, loss of power and, in the worst case, carbon monoxide in the building."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Power generation and generating sets","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/generacion-motogeneradores#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What does the AVR regulate and what does the governor of a generating set regulate? The AVR regulates the voltage, acting on the alternator’s excitation current. The governor regulates the frequency, acting on the fuel to keep the engine speed constant. A three-phase set of 100 kVA, 400 V and power factor 0.8. How many kW does it deliver and what is its rated current? The active power is: and the rated current: The engine must also be able to deliver those 80 kW at the shaft (plus the alternator losses). Why does a four-pole set turn at 1,500 rpm? Because in a synchronous alternator the frequency is tied to the speed. For 50 Hz and p = 2 pole pairs: How do a Pelton, a Francis and a Kaplan turbine differ? In the kind of drop they use. The Pelton is an impulse turbine, for high heads and small flows; the Francis, a reaction turbine, for medium heads and flows; the Kaplan, a reaction turbine with axial flow and adjustable blades, for low heads and large flows. List the steps of an automatic transfer switchboard sequence upon a grid failure. The controller detects the loss of voltage and waits for a delay; it gives the start order and opens the grid contactor; when the set is stable it closes the set contactor (never both at once). When the grid returns, it waits for a delay, retransfers and lets the set cool unloaded before stopping it. Which four conditions must be met to synchronize a set with a busbar? Equal voltage, equal frequency, equal phase sequence an"},{"t":"Building the plan: inventory, criticality and frequencies","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#plan","x":"01 Building the plan: inventory, criticality and frequencies The three kinds of maintenance, thermography and the basic plan for an industrial switchboard are explained in Maintenance of electrical components. This page covers what comes next: how the work of a whole plant, building or workshop is organised so that the plan actually gets carried out. The mission of maintenance To keep the function of each piece of equipment —not the equipment itself— with safety, with the availability that production needs and at the lowest total cost. The department has six functions: conserve, inspect, repair, improve, plan and record. The last two are what turn a group of technicians into a system. Implementation stages. A preventive plan is not put together in one afternoon; it is built in order and reviewed every year: Stage What is done What comes out of it 1 Inventory and coding: survey every piece of equipment, give it a unique code (plant-area-type-number) and a legible nameplate. List of equipment with nameplate data and location. 2 Criticality: classify each item by what happens if it fails. Classes A, B and C. 3 Tasks and frequencies: define what is done on each item and how often, starting from the manufacturer’s manual. Plan for each item. 4 Resources: staff by specialty, spare parts in stock, tools and calibrated instruments. Organised tool crib and estimated labour hours. 5 Scheduling: spread the tasks across the year, coordinating shutdowns with production. Annual calendar an"},{"t":"The work order and follow-up","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#ot","x":"02 The work order and follow-up The work order (WO) is the document that turns a need into a task with an owner, a date, resources and a record. It is born from an inspection, from an operator’s call, from a condition alarm or from the plan’s calendar; and it always travels the same path. A useful WO is short but complete. This is a model, with a preventive order already filled in and closed: Field Filled-in example No. and issue date WO 0147 · issued on 04/03 Equipment P1-BOM-03 · discharge pump motor (15 kW, 380 V) · class A Origin and type Annual plan · preventive · normal priority Tasks Re-tighten terminals with a torque wrench, measure winding insulation, measure current per phase, grease the bearings, thermography under load. Safety Lock out breaker Q3 with padlock and tag; work permit No. 52; insulating gloves and face shield for the thermography. Planned resources 2 technicians · 3 h · torque wrench, insulation tester, clamp meter, thermal camera · 1 grease cartridge. Planned date and execution Planned for 12/03 · carried out on 12/03, from 8:00 to 10:40. Measured values Currents 28.1 – 28.4 – 28.2 A · insulation 640 MΩ · maximum ΔT at terminals 6 °C. Actual hours and spares 2 technicians × 2.7 h = 5.4 h · 0.3 grease cartridge. Remarks and closing L3 terminal with ΔT of 6 °C: check on the next round. Signed by the person who did the work and by the supervisor. Tracking sheet. The orders are entered in a sheet (today almost always a spreadsheet or a management program)"},{"t":"Management indicators: MTBF, MTTR and availability","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#indicadores","x":"03 Management indicators: MTBF, MTTR and availability Three numbers sum up how a machine behaves over a period. All of them come from the corrective orders in the sheet: how many times it failed ( n ), how long it ran ( T op ) and how long it stood idle for repairs ( T rep ). MTBF (mean time between failures) measures reliability; MTTR (mean time to repair) measures how quickly service is restored; availability A is the fraction of time in which the machine is in a condition to work. Example · A compressor before and after the preventive plan A compressor is scheduled for 2,000 h of service in the half-year. It suffers 4 failures and the repairs last 3, 5, 2 and 6 h. We calculate: T rep = 3 + 5 + 2 + 6 = 16 h · T op = 2,000 − 16 = 1,984 h Check by another route:. The compressor was available 99.2 % of the time. After a year with the plan (re-tightening, insulation tests, oil analysis, filter changes), the next half-year brings only 2 failures of 4 h each: T rep = 8 h, MTBF = (2,000 − 8) / 2 = 996 h, MTTR = 4 h and A = 996 / 1,000 = 99.6 %. The MTBF almost doubled and the MTTR did not change: the plan improved reliability, not the speed of repair. Lowering the MTTR takes other measures: spares in stock, written procedures, trained staff. Availability alone is misleading 99.2 % looks excellent, but it depends on when the 16 lost hours fall. If a single 16 h failure hits during harvest or over a full shift, the production damage is far greater than that of four failures spread o"},{"t":"Costs, depreciation and service life","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#costos","x":"04 Costs, depreciation and service life Maintaining costs money, and not maintaining costs more, but you have to be able to prove it with numbers. Maintenance costs are labour, spares and materials, contracted services and, the most underestimated, the cost of downtime: lost production, penalties, rework. Hourly cost. To make decisions about a machine, what it costs for each hour it works is expressed: Hourly cost = depreciation per hour + maintenance per hour + energy and consumables per hour. Depreciation. This is the way of spreading the value of a machine over its service life, that is, the period in which it performs at a reasonable cost. With the straight-line method: V 0: purchase value · V r: residual value at the end of the service life · N: years of service life · h yr: service hours per year. Example · Hourly cost of the compressor The compressor cost 18,000 USD, a residual value of 2,000 USD is estimated after 10 years and it works 2,000 h/year. Its maintenance (labour and spares) runs about 1,200 USD/year. M h = 1,200 / 2,000 = 0.60 USD/h, so, not counting energy, the machine costs 0.80 + 0.60 = 1.40 USD per hour worked. At 4 years its book value is 18,000 − 4 × 1,600 = 11,600 USD. Check: 16,000 / 20,000 h of life = 0.80 USD/h. Replace or repair. When an important machine fails, the decision is not made by intuition or by looking only at the repair invoice. Many workshops use as a rule of thumb not to repair if the cost exceeds half the price of a new unit, but t"},{"t":"From preventive to predictive: periodic checks and control charts","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#predictivo","x":"05 From preventive to predictive: periodic checks and control charts Preventive maintenance changes a part “just in case”; predictive maintenance measures a variable that follows the deterioration and intervenes only when the trend justifies it. For that you need to take the same measurement, at the same point and under comparable conditions, at regular intervals. This is called a periodic check. Technique What it detects Measured with Vibration Unbalance (vibration concentrated at 1× the rotation speed), misalignment (often shows up at 2×), looseness and bearing wear, in motors, pumps and fans. Accelerometer with a data collector; vibration velocity is recorded in mm/s RMS. Severity is assessed with the ISO 20816 series. Thermography Contacts and terminals with abnormal resistance, overloads, imbalance. See the criterion based on temperature difference. Infrared camera, with the installation under load. Oil analysis In transformers: moisture, acidity, dielectric strength and dissolved gases that reveal overheating or internal discharges. In gearboxes: wear particles, viscosity, contamination. Sample taken following a procedure and sent to a laboratory; compared with the previous analysis. Insulation Degradation of the winding insulation from moisture, dirt or temperature. The trend of the values matters more than a single number. Insulation tester; always measured at a similar temperature, which is recorded. Current and power quality Imbalance, growing overload, harmonics, f"},{"t":"Safety in maintenance tasks","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#seguridad","x":"06 Safety in maintenance tasks Most serious electrical accidents happen during maintenance tasks, not in normal use. The basic procedure — disconnect, lock out, verify absence of voltage, ground and short-circuit, and delimit the area — is the five golden rules, explained in Occupational health and safety. What follows is what management adds so that those rules are always followed and do not depend on each technician’s memory. Lockout and tagout (LOTO) Lockout prevents someone from re-energising the equipment while another person is working on it. An orderly system requires: a written procedure for each machine listing all its energy sources —electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, capacitors or loaded springs— and where each one is isolated; a personal padlock and tag with each worker’s name, date and phone number; if three people work, there are three padlocks, and a group lockout box is used for the keys of several people; checking that the lockout works: trying to start the machine from the local control, after verifying that there is no voltage; that the padlock is removed only by the person who placed it, and that before re-energising it is confirmed that everyone has left the area. Work permit It is a document that authorises a high-risk task and sets its conditions. It does not replace the work order: it complements it. It is required when the risk goes beyond the routine: work on medium voltage, live work, work at height, in confined spaces or with fire. It contains: desc"},{"t":"In the workshop","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#taller","x":"07 In the workshop Practice 1 · Inventory and criticality of the workshop Materials: survey sheet, tape or labels for coding, flashlight. Steps: survey ten pieces of equipment in the workshop or school (switchboards, motors, test benches), assign each a code and copy the nameplate data; rate each one by the consequences of its failure and classify it as A, B or C; for class A, propose tasks and frequencies. Acceptance criterion: all equipment with a unique code and a class justified in writing; each class A item with at least four tasks and a reasoned frequency. Practice 2 · Issue, carry out and close a work order Materials: a decommissioned three-phase motor with its control switchboard, torque wrench, insulation tester, clamp meter, padlocks and tags, WO and work-permit templates. Steps: (1) issue the WO with tasks, resources and risks; (2) disconnect, lock out with padlock and tag, verify absence of voltage testing the instrument before and after; (3) re-tighten terminals to the specified torque and measure the winding insulation; (4) remove the lockout and measure current per phase under load; (5) close the WO with measured values, hours and remarks, and enter it in the sheet. Acceptance criterion: WO complete and signed; insulation recorded with the approximate temperature; current imbalance between phases below 5 %; no empty field. Practice 3 · Control chart of one variable Materials: a laboratory motor running, infrared thermometer or contact sensor, spreadsheet. Steps"},{"t":"Frequent mistakes","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#errores","x":"08 Frequent mistakes Mistake Consequence Applying the same plan to all equipment Effort is spent on equipment that does not need it and the critical items are neglected. Criticality exists to allocate resources. Issuing orders without closing them with data There is no history, no indicators can be calculated and the plan does not improve. Calculating the MTBF by dividing the whole period by the failures Downtime is included as if it were running time. The repair time must be subtracted. Believing the MTBF is a guaranteed time It is an average: with random failures, only 36.8 % of machines reach the MTBF without failing. Deciding to repair by looking only at the cost of the repair Energy and years of life are ignored: an old machine that is cheap to repair can cost more per year. Taking predictive measurements under different conditions each time The measurements cannot be compared and there is no trend. Same point, same load, same instrument. Waiting to leave the limit before acting A rising trend inside the limits is already a warning; waiting turns predictive into corrective. Doing the lockout “by word of mouth” or with one padlock for the whole machine If someone re-energises while another is working, the accident happens. One padlock per person, always."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Maintenance management","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/gestion-mantenimiento#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the point of classifying equipment by criticality? To allocate maintenance resources: class A equipment, whose failure stops the plant or endangers people, gets strict preventive and predictive maintenance; class C can wait for corrective. In one month 24 preventive orders were scheduled and 21 were closed. What is the compliance? 21 / 24 = 0.875, that is 87.5 %. The remaining 3 are recorded as overdue, with the reason. A machine is scheduled for 3,000 h in the period and has 5 failures adding up to 30 h of repair. Calculate MTBF, MTTR and availability. If 3,000 h is the scheduled period, T op = 3,000 − 30 = 2,970 h. MTBF = 2,970 / 5 = 594 h; MTTR = 30 / 5 = 6 h; A = 594 / (594 + 6) = 0.99, or 99 %. Check: (3,000 − 30) / 3,000 = 0.99. A machine cost 50,000 USD, is worth 5,000 USD after 15 years and works 3,000 h/year. What is the depreciation per hour? D = (50,000 − 5,000) / 15 = 3,000 USD/year; D h = 3,000 / 3,000 = 1.00 USD/h. Check: 45,000 / (15 × 3,000) = 1.00. A control chart has an average of 2.20 mm/s and σ = 0.15 mm/s. What are the limits and what should be done if a reading of 2.8 appears? 3σ = 0.45; UCL = 2.65 mm/s and LCL = 1.75 mm/s. A reading of 2.8 is outside the UCL: a scheduled corrective order is issued to look for the cause (for example a bearing or misalignment), first confirming that the measurement was taken under the same conditions. What is the difference between a work order and a work permit? The work order organises the tas"},{"t":"Hand tools","d":"Tool handling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/herramientas#manuales","x":"01 Hand tools Tool What it is for and what it is not for Combination pliers Gripping, bending and cutting thick conductor. It is not a wrench: tightening nuts with it rounds off the flats. Needle-nose pliers Fine work, forming eyelets, reaching narrow spaces. It cannot take force: it spreads open and becomes useless. Diagonal cutters Cutting conductor flush. Never cut steel or hardened wire: it permanently nicks the edge. Wire stripper Removing the insulation without biting into the copper. Set to the correct cross-section, it leaves the conductor intact. Screwdrivers Slotted, Phillips, Pozidriv and Torx. The tip must fill the slot: a small tip ruins the screw and your hand. Wrenches and sockets For terminals and bolts. The open-end wrench only when the socket or box-end wrench does not fit. Crimping tool Pre-insulated terminals, ferrules and connectors. Each type has its own die: crimping with combination pliers makes a connection that will fail. Voltage tester and detector They indicate the presence of voltage. They do not replace the multimeter: they are never to be trusted to declare the absence of voltage. 1000 V insulated tools They carry the double-triangle mark and the test voltage. They are used in any job where voltage could be present, even if it is believed not to be. They are inspected before each use: if the insulation is cut, cracked, burned or peeling away from the metal, the tool is discarded. It is not repaired with tape. And even with an insulated tool, the"},{"t":"Power tools","d":"Tool handling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/herramientas#electricas","x":"02 Power tools Machine Use and criteria Drill / driver Drilling and driving screws. The adjustable clutch prevents overtightening and ruining the screw. Rotary hammer Masonry and concrete, with a carbide-tipped bit and hammering action. In metal or wood it is used without hammering. Angle grinder Cutting and grinding metal. The tool that causes the most serious accidents: it demands a face shield and the right disc. Jigsaw Cutouts in sheet metal and plastic for enclosures. Blade chosen by material and low speed in metal. Hole punch Clean round holes in metal enclosures, much better than the jigsaw for cable glands. Conduit bender and threader Metal conduit: bends without crushing the cross-section, and threads for the fittings. n in revolutions per minute, V c the cutting speed in meters per minute and D the drill bit diameter in millimeters. Worked example · Drilling steel with a 10 mm bit For ordinary steel with a high-speed steel bit,.. In practice, the nearest step on the drill: about 800 rpm, with coolant and firm, constant pressure. With a 4 mm bit the same calculation gives 1990 rpm, and with a 20 mm one, 400 rpm. The rule of thumb: big bit, slow spin. A bit does not burn out because of poor quality It burns out from turning too fast, from lack of pressure —the bit rubs instead of cutting— or from lack of coolant. A bluish edge is a sign of overheating: it lost its temper and will no longer cut again even if sharpened. Constant sparks and squealing are the two signs th"},{"t":"Safe use","d":"Tool handling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/herramientas#seguridad","x":"03 Safe use Before use Check the power cord, plug and housing. A cord repaired with insulating tape is a reason to retire the tool, not to fix it. Verify that the guards and shields are in place. Removing the guard from a grinder “because it gets in the way” is what causes the most serious injuries in the workshop. Use the appropriate personal protective equipment: safety glasses always, face shield with the grinder, gloves when there are no rotating parts, safety footwear. Connect to a circuit with an RCD. It is the protection that saves you with a portable tool. During and after The workpiece clamped, never held in the hand: the vise or clamp is not optional. No gloves or loose sleeves near a rotating tool: they catch. Wait for a complete stop before setting the machine down. Disconnect before changing a bit, disc or blade. Clean, inspect and put away in its place. A tool put away dirty is a tool that fails the next time. The general framework of prevention, personal protective equipment and risk analysis is developed in health and safety."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Tool handling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/herramientas#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Lab 1 · Connections compared Prepare three connections of the same conductor to a terminal: one with the bare wire twisted, another with a ferrule crimped with combination pliers and another with a ferrule crimped with the correct die. Measure the voltage drop across each one at 10 A and compare the temperature after ten minutes. Lab 2 · Tightening torque Tighten identical terminals with an ordinary screwdriver “by feel” and with a torque screwdriver set to the manufacturer’s value. Measure the torque actually applied in the first case: the spread among students is usually enormous, and that is exactly the problem. Lab 3 · Cutting speed Drill steel plate with 4, 8 and 12 mm bits, calculating the rpm for each. Repeat on purpose at the drill’s maximum speed and compare the finish of the hole and the condition of the cutting edge. Document with photos. Lab 4 · Preparing an enclosure Mark, drill and mount a complete enclosure: hole punch for cable glands, DIN rail, wire ducts, and orderly routing of conductors. Verify at the end that the IP rating is maintained: every hole with its cable gland or its plug."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Tool handling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/herramientas#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Stripping the cable with pliers or a knife Strands of the conductor are cut: the real cross-section drops and that point heats up. Crimping with combination pliers The compression is uneven: the terminal loosens over time or pulls out. Screwdriver with a small tip The screw slot is rounded off and the screw can no longer be tightened or loosened. Hammering action in metal or wood The bit is ruined and the hole comes out irregular. Large bit at maximum speed The edge burns, loses its temper and stops cutting. Angle grinder without guard or face shield It is the source of the most serious injuries in the workshop. Holding the workpiece by hand If the tool jams, the workpiece spins with it. Trusting the voltage tester to declare “no voltage” It can read wrong. The absence of voltage is verified with an instrument, checking it before and after."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Tool handling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/herramientas#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How do you identify a 1000 V insulated tool? By the double-triangle mark and the indication of the test voltage. If the insulation is damaged, the tool is discarded: it is not repaired. Is an insulated tool enough to work safely? No. The procedure remains disconnect, lock out, tag and verify the absence of voltage with an instrument. The insulated tool is the last barrier, not the first. Why does the tightening torque of a terminal matter? Too loose, and the contact resistance heats the terminal and the problem worsens by itself; overtightened, the conductor is deformed or the thread strips. That is why a value is specified and a torque screwdriver is used. Calculate the rpm for a 6 mm bit in steel, with.. Why must a large drill bit turn more slowly? Because the speed at the cutting edge is proportional to the diameter times the revolutions. To maintain the right cutting speed, as the diameter grows the revolutions must come down. What does a bluish bit edge indicate? That it overheated and lost its temper. It no longer cuts well even if sharpened: it must be discarded and the speed, pressure and coolant reviewed. Why are gloves not worn near a rotating tool? Because the glove catches and drags the hand in. Around rotating parts you work without gloves, with tight sleeves and nothing dangling. When is hammering action used on a drill? Only in masonry and concrete, with a carbide-tipped bit. In metal, wood or plastic you work without hammering action. What e"},{"t":"What current does to the body","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#riesgo","x":"01 What current does to the body What does the damage is not the voltage but the current, how long it flows and where. Voltage matters because it determines that current against the body's resistance, which ranges from about 1000 Ω with dry skin to less than 500 Ω with wet hands. Current (50 Hz) Effect 0.5 to 1 mA Threshold of perception: barely a tingle. 5 mA Unpleasant sensation. Sudden reaction: the danger becomes the blow or the fall. 10 to 15 mA Tetanization: the muscles contract and the person cannot let go of the conductor. 25 to 30 mA Diaphragm contraction: breathing becomes difficult. Dangerous if it lasts. 50 mA and above Risk of ventricular fibrillation: the heart stops pumping. It is the usual cause of death by electric shock. Amperes Cardiac arrest and deep internal burns that look like little injury on the skin. Example · Why 220 V is dangerous and 24 V much less so With dry hands (≈ 1000 Ω) and 220 V:. Far above the fibrillation threshold. With damp hands (≈ 500 Ω):. At 24 V dry skin resists considerably more, because the body's resistance only drops as the voltage rises: about 3000 Ω.. It is felt and is unpleasant, but it stays below the let-go threshold and is rarely lethal in dry conditions. That is why industrial control circuits work at 24 V, and why the limits of safety extra-low voltage are 50 V AC and 120 V DC, and considerably lower in damp locations. The path matters as much as the current Right hand to left foot passes through the heart; hand to hand"},{"t":"Direct and indirect contact","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#contactos","x":"02 Direct and indirect contact Direct Touching a part that is normally live: a bare conductor, a terminal, a contactor contact. It is prevented with insulation, enclosures, barriers, clearances and signage. The RCD is the last line of defense, not the first. Indirect Touching a metal part that should not be live but is because of an insulation fault: a motor's enclosure, a machine's casing. It is prevented with grounding plus an RCD, or with double insulation. It is the most treacherous case, because nothing looks different. The measures, in order of effectiveness Eliminate the hazard: work de-energized. It is the only measure that never fails. Substitute: use safety extra-low voltage wherever possible. Collective protection: enclosures, barriers, interlocks, grounding, RCDs. Signage and procedures: lockout, tagout, work permits. Personal protection: gloves, footwear, face shield. It is the last resort, not the first: it protects one person and only if it is used correctly."},{"t":"The five golden rules","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#reglas","x":"03 The five golden rules Step What you do Why 1 Disconnect all sources of voltage Including the non-obvious ones: another supply, a generator set, a battery, a charged capacitor, energy stored in a spring or in compressed air. 2 Lock out and tag the disconnecting device With your own lock and a tag with your name. This is LOTO (lockout/tagout): only the person who put the lock on removes it. If three people are working, there are three locks. 3 Verify the absence of voltage With a suitable instrument, on all conductors, and testing the instrument before and after on a known source. 4 Ground and short-circuit Mandatory in medium and high voltage: it protects against accidental reconnection or an induced voltage. 5 Delimit and mark off the area So that no outsider enters or reconnects, and so that it is clear where the safe zone ends. Why step 3 gets skipped and why it must not be Because “we already disconnected it, it must be dead.” The real cases that prove otherwise are always the same: the wrong switch was opened, the panel was mislabeled, another line feeds the same terminal, someone reconnected, or the instrument itself was faulty. Verifying takes twenty seconds. And the instrument must be verified too: a voltage tester with a dead battery shows nothing, exactly like a circuit with no voltage."},{"t":"The electric arc","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#arco","x":"04 The electric arc It is the least known hazard and one of the worst. A short circuit in a panel can form an arc of thousands of degrees that vaporizes the copper, expands the air suddenly and throws molten metal. You do not need to touch anything: being nearby is enough. What causes it A tool that falls and bridges two busbars. An instrument on the wrong range —ammeter across a voltage— is the classic cause. A probe tip that slips. Operating a switch with a bolted fault downstream. How to reduce it Work de-energized: it eliminates the hazard completely. Insulated, full-handle tools with no exposed metal parts. An instrument of the correct category with original fuses. Cotton or flame-resistant clothing —never synthetic, which melts onto the skin— and a face shield when appropriate. Do not rest tools or instruments on the open panel."},{"t":"Other electronics workshop hazards","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#taller","x":"05 Other electronics workshop hazards Hazard Where it appears Measure Thermal Soldering iron at 350 °C, heat sinks, power resistors Keep the iron in its stand at all times; warn others before leaving something hot on the bench. Soldering fumes The rosin flux in solder irritates the airways Fume extractor or at least ventilation. It is not lead fume, but it still does harm. Lead Traditional tin-lead solder No eating or drinking at the workstation; wash your hands when done. Chemical Ferric chloride, isopropyl alcohol, aerosols Gloves, safety goggles, ventilation and disposal as special waste: it does not go down the sink. Projectiles A reversed electrolytic capacitor explodes; solder spatters Safety goggles. It is the most common accident and the most avoidable. Mechanical Drill, PCB drilling machine, shear cutter Clamp the workpiece, never hold it by hand; safety goggles; no loose clothing or dangling sleeves. Noise Compressors, motors, blowers Hearing protection above 85 dB sustained. Ergonomics and eyesight Hours of fine soldering and screen work Good lighting, magnifier, bench height, breaks."},{"t":"Argentine regulatory framework","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#normativa","x":"06 Argentine regulatory framework Regulation What it establishes Ley 19,587 (Occupational Health and Safety Law) The framework law: obligations of the employer and the worker. Decreto 351/79 Its implementing regulation: conditions of workplaces, lighting, noise, electrical hazard, personal protection. Ley 24,557 (Occupational Risks Law) System of ART (occupational risk insurers), prevention and compensation. AEA 90364 (Argentine Electrotechnical Association) Regulation for the execution of electrical installations in buildings: the one the technician uses every day. IRAM 10005 (Argentine Standards Institute) Safety colors and signs: red for prohibition and fire, yellow for warning, green for rescue, blue for mandatory action. What falls to each party The employer must provide safe conditions, protective equipment, training and written procedures. The worker must use the equipment, follow the procedures and report any unsafe condition detected. No one is obliged to perform a task under unsafe conditions. Refusing to work without lockout, without a verified instrument or without protection is not misconduct: it is complying with the regulations."},{"t":"If someone receives an electric shock","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#primeros","x":"07 If someone receives an electric shock In order, no exceptions Do not touch them. If they are still in contact, whoever touches them gets caught too. Cut the power: switch, circuit breaker, panel. If that is not possible, separate them with an insulating, dry object —a wooden stick, a plastic chair— while standing on insulating material. Call for help: 107, medical emergencies. Say that it is an electrical accident. Assess consciousness and breathing. If they are not breathing, start CPR and use the defibrillator if one is available. Always transport them to hospital, even if the person seems fine: a shock can cause heart rhythm disturbances hours later, and internal burns cannot be seen."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Lab 1 · Applying the five rules On a training panel, carry out the complete procedure with lock and tag, verifying with an instrument and testing the instrument before and after. Time it: it takes less than two minutes. Repeat it until you can do it without consulting the list. Lab 2 · Workshop audit Walk through the workshop with a checklist and note: extension cords in poor condition, plugs without ground pin, unlabeled panels, expired fire extinguishers, blocked exits, uninsulated tools, lack of safety goggles. Prioritize by risk and propose corrections. Audit again after two weeks. Lab 3 · Testing the RCD Check the workshop's RCD with its test button and record the date on a log sheet. Discuss how often it should be done and who would be responsible in a workplace. Lab 4 · Analysis of an accident With a documented real case, reconstruct the sequence and identify at what point one of the five rules —or a collective protection— would have broken the chain. There is almost always more than one point where the accident could have been avoided."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Unsafe practice Why it is serious “It’s only a minute, I won’t disconnect” Most serious accidents happen during short, routine tasks. Disconnecting without locking out Anyone can reconnect without knowing someone is working. Not verifying the absence of voltage The panel may be mislabeled or fed from somewhere else. Using the multimeter on its current range to measure voltage A dead short and an electric arc. It is one of the most common causes of serious burns. Instrument with an insufficient category rating It cannot withstand the panel's transients: it explodes in your hand. Working alone on energized installations If something goes wrong, there is nobody to cut the power or to assist. Rings, watches or metal bracelets They concentrate current and heat: they cause very severe burns. Synthetic clothing In an arc, it melts onto the skin and greatly worsens the injury. Bypassing a protective device “because it keeps tripping” The protection trips for a reason. Bypassing it turns a fault into a fire."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Which is more dangerous: voltage or current? The current flowing through the body, together with the time and the path. Voltage matters because, against the body's resistance, it determines that current. At what current can a person no longer let go of the conductor? Around 10 to 15 mA AC: this is muscular tetanization. That is why the protection threshold of household RCDs is 30 mA and they act within milliseconds. With 220 V and a body resistance of 1000 Ω, what current flows?: far above the threshold of ventricular fibrillation. What is the difference between direct and indirect contact? Direct contact is touching a part that is normally live; indirect contact is touching an exposed metal part that became live through an insulation fault. The second is prevented with grounding plus an RCD. List the five golden rules in order. 1) Disconnect all sources. 2) Lock out and tag. 3) Verify the absence of voltage. 4) Ground and short-circuit. 5) Delimit and mark off the area. Why must you test the instrument before and after verifying? Because a faulty instrument reads “no voltage” exactly like a circuit that is really disconnected. By testing it on a known source before and after, that confusion is ruled out. What are lockout and tagout, and why does each person put on their own lock? It means physically securing the disconnecting device with a lock and an identified tag. Each person puts on their own so that nobody can reconnect while anyone is still working: "},{"t":"Mounting in the panel","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#montaje","x":"01 Mounting in the panel The structure of the modules, the memory and the addressing are explained in Structure of a programmable controller; here the focus is what it takes to install them. The layout of the panel determines the working temperature, the noise the signals pick up and how easy maintenance is, and it is the one thing that cannot be fixed later with software. Fixing 35 mm DIN rail (IEC/EN 60715), screwed to the mounting plate every few centimetres: a rail that vibrates loosens everything on it. Metal mounting plate bonded to PE; the rail makes electrical contact with it through screws and washers that bite through paint or zinc plating. The PLC modules snap onto the rail and connect to each other through the backplane bus or connectors: they are assembled from left to right in the order of the configuration, with no gaps. Normal position: horizontal, with the ventilation slots at the top and bottom. Mounted vertically, the permitted temperature range is usually reduced: the manual says so. Air, heat and spare room Leave free air above and below the PLC (in the order of 25 to 50 mm, or whatever the manual says) and keep the distance to ducts and other devices. The temperature limit (usually up to about 55 °C) refers to the air around the PLC inside the panel, not to the air in the room. Anything that produces heat —supply, drive, braking resistors, transformers— goes apart and never below the PLC, because hot air rises. Reserve space and free terminals (20 to 30 "},{"t":"Power supply and earthing","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#alimentacion","x":"02 Power supply and earthing A panel with a PLC contains three circuits with different needs: the electronics (CPU and modules), the sensors and inputs, and the outputs with their loads. They are fed from a 24 V DC supply, and it pays to protect them separately so that a short circuit in a solenoid valve does not leave the CPU without power. The 24 V supply Switch-mode and regulated, with safe isolation between the mains and the secondary (safety extra-low voltage, called MBTS/MBTP in AEA 90364 terminology). With a margin over the calculation, and with the voltage set to 24 V on the supply’s potentiometer. If the supply delivers less current at high temperature, the derating curve of the data sheet is used, not the nameplate value. Mounted apart from the PLC (it produces heat) and with its own short, thick wiring to the power terminals. Protection Primary: a miniature circuit breaker of the rating the supply manufacturer specifies, generally curve C because of the supply’s inrush current (see Electrical protection ). Secondary: a fuse or breaker per branch (CPU, sensors, each group of outputs), rated below what the conductor can carry. This way, a fault in one branch trips its own protection and the rest stays energised: the PLC logs it and raises an alarm instead of shutting down. How to size the supply Add up the currents of everything fed from the 24 V terminals in the worst case, that is, with everything running at once, and add a margin for expansion, ageing and temperat"},{"t":"Wiring the inputs","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#entradas","x":"03 Wiring the inputs The characteristics of each input type (optical isolation, filter, thresholds, resolution) are in Structure of a programmable controller. Here we deal with the wiring, which is where mistakes are made. Digital inputs: PNP and NPN sensors A pushbutton, a limit switch or an auxiliary contact is a dry contact: it works with any module, and the “common” is connected to whichever terminal applies. Three-wire proximity sensors (inductive, capacitive, photoelectric) have an output transistor that can be of two types, and which one it is determines how the module’s common has to be wired. Sensor On detection, the output… Module common Name of the module PNP (switched positive) delivers +24 V to the input to 0 V sinking input: drains the current to the common NPN (switched negative) takes it to 0 V to +24 V sourcing input: supplies the current to the input “Sinking” and “sourcing”, depending on who is speaking In North American literature these terms describe the module’s input, as in the table; in other manuals they are applied to the sensor, and then a PNP sensor is “sourcing” (it supplies current). The confusion is common. Do not settle it by name but by polarity: measure with the multimeter between the sensor output and 0 V, with the sensor powered and detecting. If it reads almost 24 V it is PNP; if it reads almost 0 V (and 24 V when not detecting) it is NPN. Many European modules come with the common at 0 V and expect PNP sensors; NPN is frequent in equipmen"},{"t":"Wiring the outputs","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#salidas","x":"04 Wiring the outputs Outputs are one of the parts of the PLC that suffer most: they switch current, and inductive loads (contactor, relay and solenoid-valve coils) give it back with interest when they open. The output types are compared in Structure of a programmable controller; here, what to check and what to add when connecting them. Before connecting a load, check Why Voltage and type of the output (DC or AC) against the load A transistor output only switches DC; a triac output, only AC; a relay output works for both. Current per channel and per group The total current of a group is lower than the sum of the individual maxima: it is in the data sheet. Inductive or capacitive load A relay contact’s capacity for coils is lower than for a resistor (utilisation categories AC-15 and DC-13 of IEC 60947-5-1). Incandescent lamps and switch-mode supplies have an inrush current far above the rated one. Protection per group One fuse per output group keeps a short circuit in the field from taking out the whole module. Safe state What the load does when the output opens or the PLC stops. The safe thing is for the machine to stop: energise to run, de-energise to stop. Inductive loads: suppression A coil stores magnetic energy while current flows. When the circuit opens, that current cannot stop instantly: the coil generates whatever voltage it takes —hundreds of volts— to keep it going, and this voltage appears across the output terminals. On a transistor it causes immediate failure or"},{"t":"Commissioning step by step","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#puesta","x":"05 Commissioning step by step Order matters: you go from what has no voltage to what has, and from what moves nothing to what moves. Each step has an acceptance criterion; if it is not met, you do not move on. Step What is done Acceptance criterion 1 Review and documentation. With the drawing, the I/O list and the program at hand: visual inspection of terminals, labels, ferrules and fuses. Every conductor labelled as in the drawing; no loose wire or empty terminal without explanation. 2 Checks with no voltage. PE continuity, every field conductor end to end, no short circuit between +24 V and 0 V (with the ohmmeter, supply isolated from the circuit). Insulation of the power circuits is measured with a megohmmeter only with the modules and electronics disconnected. PE continuous; no short circuit between +24 V and 0 V (the reading does not go to zero); no leakage to earth. 3 Energise the supply alone, with the output and sensor fuses open. Measure the voltage at the PLC terminals, not at the supply’s. 24 V ± 5 % (22.8 to 25.2 V) and stable as each branch is connected. 4 Energise the PLC with the CPU in STOP and outputs disabled, if the device allows it. Power and STOP LEDs on; no fault LED. 5 Input test. Operate every sensor, pushbutton and limit switch by hand; simulate the analog inputs with a loop calibrator at 4, 12 and 20 mA. The module LED, the bit in the software and the drawing agree. Analog: 4 mA reads 0 %, 12 mA 50 %, 20 mA 100 %. 6 Forced-output test, with the actua"},{"t":"Program backup and maintenance","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#respaldo","x":"06 Program backup and maintenance Where the program lives and how it is saved The CPU keeps the program in a non-volatile load memory (flash or memory card); what it executes, together with process data, is in working memory. Retentive variables and the clock depend on a capacitor or on a backup battery ( memory and retentive area ). The program in the device is not a backup: if the CPU breaks, it is lost with it. What is saved What it is for The complete project (with symbols and comments), not just the binary A program without symbolic names or comments is almost impossible to maintain. Hardware configuration and network addresses To replace the CPU or a module and have the system start up the same way again. Parameters of drives, panels (HMI) and instruments A drive without its parameters is a new device to configure. Retentive values and recipes actually used in the process What was tuned on site is not in the original project. Software and firmware versions, and access levels A project from a newer version may not open with the older one. Copy, version and documentation Copy before touching anything and after every change, in at least two places (one outside the plant computer). Version in the file name, with the date: Packer_v1.4_2026-10-30, and a change log saying what was modified, by whom and why. Comparison online between the device and the file before downloading: “downloading” a program overwrites the one in the device. Documentation: I/O list, updated diagrams an"},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#laboratorio","x":"07 In the laboratory Practice 1 · Wiring inputs with PNP and NPN sensors Materials: training PLC with a 24 V module, 24 V supply, one PNP and one NPN inductive sensor, multimeter. Steps: wire the PNP sensor first, with the module common at 0 V. With the sensor powered and the input disconnected, measure the voltage between the sensor output and 0 V with and without an object. Connect the input and check the LED and the bit. Repeat with the NPN sensor, moving the common to +24 V. Criterion: the PNP reads close to 24 V when detecting and close to 0 V with no object; the NPN, the opposite; in both cases the input LED lights only on detection. Try and explain what happens if the NPN is connected with the common at 0 V. Practice 2 · The opening spike of a coil Materials: a 24 V relay or solenoid-valve coil, a transistor output or a switch, oscilloscope, 1N4007 diodes. Steps: at 24 V, open the output and capture the voltage across the coil without a diode (×10 probe and the scope ground on the circuit’s 0 V); repeat with the diode in anti-parallel and then with the diode reversed, only with a current-limited laboratory supply. Criterion: without the diode, a reverse voltage spike of tens or hundreds of volts; with the diode correctly fitted, the voltage at the output terminal does not go beyond about −0.7 V relative to 0 V; with the diode reversed, a short circuit shows. Also measure how long the contact takes to release with and without the diode. Practice 3 · The 4-20 mA loop and"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Mixing PNP and NPN sensors on the same common Inputs always on or never on. Solved by wiring each group with the type that applies. Connecting a 4-20 mA signal to an input configured for 0-10 V (or the other way round) Out-of-range reading, absurd values and sometimes a damaged input. Leaving out the flyback diode on a DC coil, or fitting it reversed Without it: spikes that damage the output, with intermittent faults and resets. Reversed: a short circuit. A supply with no margin Voltage dips and resets when several outputs switch on together. Faults that do not repeat when you look at them. Shield grounded at both ends, or at neither Currents in the braid or undrained noise: unstable analog readings. Power and analog signal cables in the same duct Coupled noise that no program can filter out later. Energising everything at once, with no de-energised checks A short circuit on the first connection or an actuator that moves when nobody expects it. Leaving forced values on at the end of commissioning An output that does not follow the program, or an input that hides a broken sensor. Changing the program without copying the previous one first If the change goes wrong, there is no way back. Replacing the battery with the CPU off and no backup Retentive variables, the clock and, in RAM-based devices, the program are lost."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"PLC installation and commissioning","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalacion-plc#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the module common tied to with PNP sensors and with NPN sensors, and why? With PNP sensors the common goes to 0 V: the sensor delivers +24 V to the input and the current leaves through the common. With NPN sensors the common goes to +24 V: the sensor takes the input to 0 V, and the current enters through the common and leaves toward the sensor. It is decided by the polarity of the sensor output, not by the name “sinking” or “sourcing”. Why does a DC coil get a diode, and how is it connected? Because on opening, the coil keeps its current flowing and generates a very high voltage that destroys the output. The diode, in anti-parallel with the coil and with the cathode on the positive side, gives it a recirculation path and limits the voltage to about 0.7 V. In AC an RC snubber or a varistor is used. A panel has a 0.30 A CPU, 10 sensors of 15 mA and 4 solenoid valves of 24 V and 3 W. What 24 V supply is needed? Total current in the worst case, with the valves at 3 W / 24 V = 0.125 A, and a 25 % margin: At least 1.19 A is needed: the next commercial value up is chosen. At which end is the shield of a 4-20 mA cable grounded, and why? At one end only, the panel end, to the shield bar bonded to earth. With both ends grounded, the potential difference between the two earths drives current through the braid and turns it into a noise source. At the transmitter end the braid is cut back and insulated. The cable of a 4-20 mA transmitter is cut. What does the mo"},{"t":"What protects what","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#que-protege","x":"01 What protects what This is the idea that organizes the whole topic, and it is worth fixing in mind before any calculation: Device Protects Against Thermal-magnetic breaker The conductor Overload and short circuit. Keeps the cable from heating beyond what its insulation can withstand. Fuse The conductor and the equipment Mainly short circuits. It is fast and has a high breaking capacity. Residual-current device (RCD) The person Direct or indirect contact: current that flows to ground. Grounding (earthing) The person Metal enclosures ending up live after an insulation fault. Thermal overload relay The motor Prolonged overload, phase loss, locked rotor. Motor protection circuit breaker The motor and its supply line Both: adjustable thermal + magnetic trip, in a single device. Surge arrester The equipment Transients caused by lightning or switching. The sentence to remember The thermal-magnetic breaker looks after the cable; the RCD looks after the person. They are independent and neither replaces the other: an installation can have perfect circuit breakers and still electrocute someone, and it can have an RCD and still catch fire because of an undersized cable. That is why the standard requires both."},{"t":"Selecting conductors","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#conductores","x":"02 Selecting conductors Choosing the cross-section of a cable means answering three questions, and all three must be satisfied: the largest of the resulting cross-sections is the one you use. 1 · Current-carrying capacity The cable must not exceed the maximum temperature of its insulation (70 °C for PVC, 90 °C for XLPE). The value depends on the installation method: it is not the same in free air as inside a conduit with other circuits. 2 · Voltage drop The voltage at the farthest point must remain acceptable. The AEA (Argentine Electrotechnical Association) sets 3 % for final circuits and 5 % from the meter. On long runs, this condition takes precedence over the previous one. 3 · Minimum regulatory cross-section Regardless of the calculation: 1.5 mm² for lighting, 2.5 mm² for outlets and 4 mm² for special circuits. Never less, even if the calculation allows it. L in meters, I in amperes, S in mm² and for copper. The 2 in the single-phase formula is there because the current goes and returns through two conductors. Cross-section Approximate current-carrying capacity Typical protection Common use 1.5 mm² 15 A 10 A Lighting circuit (IUG) 2,5 mm² 21 A 16 A General-purpose outlets (TUG) 4 mm² 28 A 20 to 25 A Special circuits, electric water heater, air conditioner 6 mm² 36 A 32 A Supply to sub-distribution boards 10 mm² 50 A 40 A Main line of a large dwelling Indicative values for PVC-insulated copper in conduit. The table that counts is always the one in the AEA 90364 standard f"},{"t":"Overcurrent protection","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#sobrecorriente","x":"03 Overcurrent protection A thermal-magnetic breaker has two mechanisms inside, each for a different problem: Thermal · bimetallic Acts on overloads: currents somewhat above the rated value, sustained. It is slow on purpose—seconds to minutes—to tolerate motor starting. It depends on the ambient temperature: in summer it trips sooner. Magnetic · coil Acts on short circuits: currents tens of times the rated value. It cuts in milliseconds, before the energy of the arc can damage the installation. Curve Trips between For what load B 3 to 5 Resistive loads: lighting, heating, outlets without motors. C 5 to 10 General use. Tolerates the starting current of small motors and switch-mode power supplies. D 10 to 20 Large motors, transformers, welders: loads with huge starting peaks. Breaking capacity and selectivity The breaking capacity (3 kA, 6 kA, 10 kA) is the maximum short-circuit current the device can interrupt without being destroyed. It depends on how close the utility’s transformer is: the closer, the more fault current available. Selectivity means that on a fault only the protection closest to the problem acts, not the main one. It is achieved by grading currents and times: the main one always larger and slower than the branch ones. Fuses and thermal-magnetic breakers are not interchangeable The fuse is faster on large short circuits and has a higher breaking capacity, but it has to be replaced and someone always ends up fitting one of higher rating—or a piece of wire. The "},{"t":"The residual-current device and grounding","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#diferencial","x":"04 The residual-current device and grounding It is the only device designed to protect the person, and it works on a very simple idea: all the current that enters through one conductor has to return through the other. If it does not return, it is going somewhere else, and that somewhere can be a person. Sensitivity What it protects Where it is used 30 mA People: direct and indirect contact Mandatory in every residential installation, on all circuits. 10 mA People, with an extra margin Bathrooms, laboratories, operating rooms, children’s circuits. 300 to 500 mA Property: prevents fires caused by leakage current Head of industrial installations, selective with the 30 mA ones. Why 30 mA Because it is the threshold below which a 50 Hz current passing through the body does not cause ventricular fibrillation in the time it takes the protection to act. For a sense of scale: 1 mA can be felt, 10 mA already prevents letting go of the conductor (tetanization), 30 mA affects breathing and from 50 mA there is a risk of fibrillation. The RCD does not prevent the shock: it prevents it from lasting long enough to kill. Grounding It connects all exposed metal parts—a motor housing, a refrigerator, a distribution board enclosure—to a buried ground rod. If a line conductor touches the casing, the current finds that path instead of waiting for a person, and the RCD detects the leakage and trips. Without grounding, the RCD still protects —it acts when the person touches—but the first line of def"},{"t":"Switching and motor control","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#maniobra","x":"05 Switching and motor control A motor is not operated with an ordinary switch: you must be able to start and stop it from several places, protect it from overload and guarantee that it does not restart on its own when power returns. The contactor and the thermal overload relay take care of that, governed by a control circuit separate from the power circuit. Element Function Practical detail Contactor Switch operated by an electromagnet Its coil (220 V, 24 V) is controlled with low-current pushbuttons. When the voltage is lost, it opens: hence the protection against restart. Auxiliary contacts Signaling and holding NO (normally open) and NC (normally closed). The NO in parallel with the start pushbutton is the holding contact. Thermal overload relay Protecting the motor from overload It is set to the motor’s nameplate current. Its NC contact is in series with the contactor coil. It also detects the loss of a phase. Motor protection circuit breaker Thermal + magnetic in one device Replaces the fuse + thermal relay combination and is adjusted with a dial. Pushbuttons Start and stop commands Start NO (green) and stop NC (red). Stop is always normally closed: if the pushbutton wire is cut, the motor stops, which is the safe outcome. Interlock Preventing impossible operations In a reversing circuit, each contactor opens the other’s circuit with an NC contact: the two can never close together and cause a short circuit between phases. Power diagram and control diagram They are drawn"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Measured voltage drop Set up a long run of thin cable (for example 30 m of 1 mm² on a reel) feeding a known load. Measure the voltage at the source and at the far end with the load connected and disconnected. Compare the difference with the calculated value. It is the check that the formula is not a paper formality. Lab 2 · Testing the RCD On a teaching board with a 30 mA RCD, press the test button and verify the trip. Then, with a calibrated high-value resistor between line and ground (for example 22 kΩ, which gives about 10 mA), check that it does not trip, and with 4.7 kΩ (47 mA) that it does trip. Measure the approximate time with a stopwatch. The whole lab is done on a protected teaching board and under supervision. Lab 3 · Start-stop with holding contact Wire the control circuit of Figure 2 with one contactor and two pushbuttons, without connecting the motor. Verify the holding contact, then remove the supply and restore it: the contactor must not pull in again on its own. Finally, add the NC contact of the thermal overload relay in series and simulate its trip. Lab 4 · Reversing with interlock With two contactors, wire the reversing circuit by swapping two phases on the second one. Add the electrical interlock—the NC of each in series with the other’s coil—and check that it is impossible to energize both at once. Only then connect the motor and test. The five golden rules Disconnect all voltage sources. Lock out the disconnecting device and tag it"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause “It kept tripping, so I fitted a bigger breaker” The cable was left unprotected. If the breaker keeps tripping, the circuit is overloaded or there is a fault: split the load or increase the cross-section, never enlarge the breaker. The RCD trips for no apparent reason Real leakage accumulated from several devices (each switch-mode power supply contributes some), moisture in a junction box, or a neutral bonded to ground downstream. The RCD does not trip when the test button is pressed It is faulty. Replace it: it cannot be repaired. You get a shock touching the enclosure of an appliance plugged into an outlet Insulation fault without grounding. The appliance has voltage on its casing and waits for someone to touch it. The lamp at the far end of the shed is dimmer Voltage drop from an insufficient cross-section on a long run. A motor’s breaker trips on starting Wrong curve: a C or D curve is needed to tolerate the starting peak. The motor does not start and the thermal relay looks “fine” Thermal overload relay tripped and not reset, or its NC contact open in the control circuit. When power comes back, the machines start by themselves The control has no contactor holding circuit, just an ordinary switch. It is a dangerous situation, which is why control is always done with a holding contact. Short circuit when reversing the rotation The interlock between the two contactors is missing."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electrical installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does a thermal-magnetic breaker protect and what does an RCD protect? The thermal-magnetic breaker protects the conductor against overload and short circuit. The RCD protects the person against currents diverted to ground. They are different functions and both are needed. A 25 m, 20 A, single-phase 220 V circuit with 2.5 mm² cable: does it meet the voltage-drop limit?, which is 3.18 %. It exceeds the 3 % allowed: you have to move up to 4 mm², which brings the drop down to 4.4 V (2.0 %). Why can’t you put a 25 A breaker on a 1.5 mm² cable? Because that cable carries about 15 A: current above its limit could flow for hours without the protection acting, degrading the insulation until it causes a fire. The condition is. What is the difference between curves B, C and D? The magnetic trip threshold: B between 3 and 5 times the rated current, C between 5 and 10, D between 10 and 20. The larger the starting peak of the load, the higher the curve must be. How does an RCD detect a leakage? It adds up magnetically, in a toroid, the current going in and the current coming back. If they are equal, the resulting field is zero. If return current is missing, a field appears that induces a voltage in a sensing winding and trips the mechanism. Does an RCD protect if there is no grounding? Yes, when the current goes to ground through the person: it detects the imbalance and trips. What is lost without grounding is early detection of the fault: with grounding, the leakag"},{"t":"What makes a place “special”","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#especial","x":"01 What makes a place “special” The general rules of electrical installations and of electrical protection assume an adult user with dry skin who knows the place and can walk out of it. A location is special when one of those assumptions stops holding: there are many people who do not know the building, the body is wet or connected to equipment, the environment attacks the material or a single spark is enough to start a fire. The Argentine regulation for installations in buildings ( AEA 90364 ) devotes its Part 7 to the particular rules for those places: they do not replace the general ones, they tighten them. Place What makes it special What changes in the installation Places of public assembly (cinemas, theatres, shopping centres, stadiums, auditoriums) Many people who do not know the building, panicked evacuation, smoke. Emergency and escape lighting, safety circuits with their own supply, cables that do not spread flame and emit little smoke, switchboards with restricted access. Medical rooms The patient may have broken skin or a catheter: currents far below those felt on intact skin are already dangerous. And the power cannot be cut mid-operation. IT system with isolating transformer and monitor, strict equipotential bonding, backup supply. Damp and wet locations (bathrooms, industrial kitchens, laundries, cold rooms) Humidity lowers body resistance and degrades insulation. Higher IP rating, 30 mA RCD, zones with restrictions, corrosion-resistant conduit and fittings. Sw"},{"t":"Safety circuits and their own supply","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#seguridad","x":"02 Safety circuits and their own supply A special building has two kinds of circuits. Normal circuits supply what makes the place run: general lighting, sockets, air conditioning. Safety circuits supply what protects people when something goes wrong: emergency and escape lighting, fire pumps, evacuation lifts, stairwell pressurization, alarms and detection. The latter must keep working when the mains fails, and for that they have an own supply. No interruption UPS or batteries with an inverter. For whatever cannot tolerate a single instant without power: alarm panels, intensive-care equipment, IT systems. Short interruption Central battery or self-contained luminaires. They cover emergency lighting during the evacuation, with an autonomy set by the applicable regulation. Interruption of seconds Generating set with automatic changeover (see Power generation and generating sets ). It sustains the pumps, the lifts and the safety circuits for a long time. Three rules repeat in every building with safety circuits: Independence. The safety supply has its own line from the main switchboard, with its own protection, so that a fault in a normal circuit does not leave it without power. Fire resistance. The cables of safety circuits are chosen of the type that keeps service during a fire (circuit integrity test, IEC 60331 series) and are run along protected routes. On escape routes, the other cables should preferably be low-smoke zero-halogen. Supervision. A safety circuit that is not k"},{"t":"Medical rooms","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#medico","x":"03 Medical rooms Dry human skin has a resistance of thousands of ohms; skin pierced by a cannula, an electrode or a catheter no longer protects it. When current has a direct path to the heart, microamperes are enough —currents that on intact skin are not even felt— to disturb the heart rhythm. On top of that, in an operation or in intensive care the power cannot be cut at the first fault. Rooms where the patient is connected to equipment that acts on the heart or sustains vital functions (operating theatres, intensive care, haemodynamics) are solved with a system different from that of a house. This is the IT system from the table in Electrical protection applied to a medical room: A single-phase isolating transformer of limited power galvanically separates the room from the mains: no point of the secondary is connected to earth. An insulation monitoring device ( IMD ) continuously measures the insulation resistance between each live conductor and earth. When it falls below a threshold —of the order of 50 kΩ in common devices— it raises a visual and audible alarm. The alarm is never silenced or disconnected: the faulty equipment is located and removed as soon as the procedure allows. The first fault to an exposed conductive part does not cut the power: the current can only return through the insulation of the other conductor, which is very high, so it is small. If a second fault appears on the other conductor before the first is repaired, the two join through the exposed cond"},{"t":"Low-current systems and extra-low voltage","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#debiles","x":"04 Low-current systems and extra-low voltage Low-current systems ( corrientes débiles ) are the signal and communication installations that do not deliver power to a load: alarms, data, telephony, video, door entry. They work at extra-low voltage and with small currents. Being “weak” does not make them secondary: in a special building they are the ones that give warning, release doors and record what happens, and they must keep working like the power does in an emergency. System What it does Installation particulars Fire detection and alarm Smoke and heat detectors, call points, sounders, controlled by a panel. Supervised loops; fire-resistant and generally shielded cables; own battery supply. International references in common use: NFPA 72 and the EN 54 series. Intruder alarm Sensors on doors, windows and rooms; sirens; communicator. Each zone is closed with an end-of-line resistor: that way the panel tells a cut or short circuit of the cable from a normal opening. Battery backup. Data and telephony The building's computer network and telephony (structured cabling). Twisted-pair cable with a permanent link of up to 90 m plus 10 m of patch cords; optical fibre in backbones between floors and buildings; earthed racks. CCTV Cameras and recorders. IP cameras powered over the same data cable (PoE, IEEE 802.3af/at/bt) or at extra-low voltage from a central supply; voltage-drop calculation matters. Door entry and access control Intercom, electric locks, readers. An electromagnetic "},{"t":"Switchboards, discrimination, wiring routes and earthing","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#tableros","x":"05 Switchboards, discrimination, wiring routes and earthing Switchboards of a large building A large building does not have “a switchboard”: it has a main low-voltage switchboard (TGBT) that receives the service connection or the transformer, and from it sub-boards by floor, sector or service. Safety circuits leave from a board of their own, also supplied by the set. Each board is built to the standard for low-voltage assemblies (IEC 61439 series), with its rated short-circuit withstand stated, and is placed in an accessible location, with restricted access and ventilated. For large ones the form of internal separation (1 to 4) is specified: how far the busbars, functional units and terminals are separated from each other, so that one can be worked on without exposure to the others. Switching is also designed for the emergency: an accessible main isolator, local and remote control of the safety circuits, emergency stop of machines and isolators that allow one part to be worked on without cutting the building. For protection and short-circuit calculations see Electrical protection and AC power. Discrimination in a cascade of boards In a building with many levels, a fault in a ground-floor socket must not leave the whole building without power. Discrimination (selectivity) is achieved with steps: protections are chosen so that, on a fault, the nearest one acts and those upstream do not. With circuit-breakers, rated current and curve are stepped; with RCDs, a selective (time-del"},{"t":"Worked example: emergency lighting of a corridor","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#ejemplo","x":"06 Worked example: emergency lighting of a corridor Example · A 24 V SELV circuit for a 40 m corridor An evacuation corridor in an office building has 8 emergency luminaires of 5 W at 24 V d.c., supplied from a 24 V central battery (two 12 V batteries in series) that must sustain them for 1 h. The lighting design has already fixed how many luminaires go and where. The cable cross-section and the battery capacity are required. Design data: at the end of discharge the battery delivers 21 V; the luminaires work down to 24 V − 20 % = 19.2 V; a voltage drop of 5 % is allowed; the farthest point is 40 m from the board (all the load is taken as concentrated there: a calculation on the safe side); copper at ambient temperature has. 1) Current in the worst case. The luminaires draw almost constant power: as the battery falls, the current rises. It is calculated with the final voltage: 2) Allowable drop and minimum cross-section. 5 % of 24 V is the maximum drop. The circuit is out and back, so the current path is: The next commercial cross-section up is 2.5 mm². 3) Check. With 2.5 mm² the actual drop is: By another route: the resistance of one conductor is, the out-and-back loop is, and. They agree. The voltage at the farthest luminaire, at the end of discharge, is, above the 19.2 V minimum: it complies. With 1.5 mm² the drop would be: That is 7.6 %, above the 5 % design limit: it does not comply. So the 1.5 mm² cable, which would be plenty for current, is insufficient in this case. 4)"},{"t":"In the workshop","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#laboratorio","x":"07 In the workshop Practice 1 · IT system mock-up Materials: a 220/220 V isolating transformer of 100 to 300 VA, two 47 kΩ resistors (2 W or more, insulated for 500 V), an a.c. milliammeter, a multimeter, a 40 W lamp with its holder, a board with a circuit-breaker and a 30 mA RCD upstream, and cables with safety plugs. Steps: (1) Feed the primary through the RCD. (2) With the multimeter, measure between each secondary terminal and earth: record it. (3) Connect the lamp between one secondary terminal and earth: it must not light, because there is no return path. (4) Connect a 47 kΩ resistor between each secondary terminal and earth, with the milliammeter in series in one of them, and measure the current. Safety: the secondary is at 220 V even though it “floats”: the whole set-up is built with the transformer unplugged and measured with hands away from the terminals. What is measured and acceptance criterion: the current must agree, within ±10 %, with. Explain why the lamp does not light and why, with the simulated fault, the upstream RCD does not trip. Practice 2 · Surveying the safety of a building Materials: plans of the school or of the assigned building, torch, camera, survey form, stopwatch. Steps: walk the evacuation routes and note: number and position of emergency luminaires and exit signs; IP rating marked on cabinets in damp or outdoor areas; low-current routes relative to power routes; state of the board (access, labelling, protections, free of obstructions). Test a"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Wiring a special location with the criteria of a house The requirement that justifies the location is left out. It is found at inspection or, worse, in the accident. Silencing or disconnecting the insulation monitor of a medical room The next fault is already the second: short circuit and loss of power mid-operation. Taking an equipotential connection “from the pipe” and not checking continuity A loose or painted joint leaves a potential difference exactly where there should be none. Using emergency luminaires without testing autonomy The battery ages and after two years lasts minutes. It is discovered when the power fails. Sharing the safety wiring route with normal circuits A fire or fault in the normal circuit drags down the safety one. Running data cables tight against and parallel to power cables Noise, network drop-outs, intermittent errors that are very hard to find. Sizing a 24 V circuit by current rating only Excessive voltage drop: luminaires that do not light or equipment that resets at the end of discharge. Using a 300 mA RCD “because it works” without measuring the earth The earthing does not meet the maximum value for that RCD: exposed conductive parts can sit at a dangerous voltage. An electromagnetic lock that does not open on loss of power or on a fire alarm People trapped during evacuation. Opening or connecting live equipment in an area classified as explosive A spark is enough to start the explosion."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Installations in special buildings","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-especiales#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the difference between a normal circuit and a safety circuit? The normal one supplies the building's everyday operation; the safety one protects people when something fails (emergency lighting, fire pumps, evacuation lifts, detection). The second has its own supply (battery, UPS, set), an independent line, fire-resistant cables and is tested periodically. What does it mean for a cabinet to be IP65? The first digit, 6, indicates it is completely dust-tight; the second, 5, that it resists water jets from an ordinary hose. It suits a place washed with a hose, though it does not withstand immersion. Why does an operating theatre use the IT system and what does the insulation monitor do? How much current does a first fault let through at 220 V and 50 kΩ? The IT system leaves the circuit floating with respect to earth, so the first fault does not cut the power and no dangerous current flows:. The monitor measures insulation all the time and warns before there is a second fault, which would be a short circuit. Why are power and low-current routes kept separate? For noise (power induces voltage in signal cables and degrades the data) and for safety (a power fault must not reach a signal circuit). Separate routes or trays with a continuous divider are used, and crossings are made at 90°. What is SELV and what are its limits? It is safety extra-low voltage: up to 50 V a.c. or 120 V d.c. ripple-free, obtained from a safety source, with no earthing of the circu"},{"t":"Electrification grades","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#grados","x":"01 Electrification grades The first step is to classify the dwelling. The AEA (Argentine Electrotechnical Association) regulations set four grades according to floor area and expected demand, and from that grade comes the minimum number of circuits. Grade Floor area Demand Minimum circuits Minimum up to 60 m² up to 3.7 kVA 2 Medium up to 130 m² up to 7 kVA 3 High up to 200 m² up to 11 kVA 5 Superior over 200 m² over 11 kVA per design The grade is a minimum, not a target Meeting the minimum grade is what the regulations require, but a sensible design anticipates: a spare circuit in the distribution board, an empty conduit up to the ceiling, a cross-section one step larger in the feeder. All of that costs little during construction and a great deal afterwards."},{"t":"Circuit types","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#circuitos","x":"02 Circuit types Code What it supplies Max. points Cross-section Protection IUG General-purpose lighting. 15 1.5 mm² 10 A TUG General-purpose outlets. 15 2.5 mm² 16 or 20 A IUE Special-purpose lighting: larger loads or outdoor lighting. 12 2.5 mm² 16 A TUE Special-purpose outlets: water heater, oven, washing machine. 12 2.5 mm² or more 20 A ACU One air-conditioning unit per circuit. 1 per unit per unit ATE Feeder to a sub-distribution board. — per calculation per calculation APM Feeder to small motors: pump, gate. — per motor per motor Rules that organize everything Lighting and outlets never share a circuit: if the protection trips because of an appliance, the house is not left in the dark. Every outlet circuit carries a 30 mA RCD. Every circuit carries its protective conductor, green and yellow, to every point. Large loads go on their own circuit: they are not hung from a TUG. Standard colors Line (live): brown, black or red. Neutral: light blue. Protective (PE): green and yellow, and only for that use. It is not a decorative convention: it is what allows another technician to work on the installation years later without guessing. Using green-yellow as a live conductor is a serious violation."},{"t":"Distribution boards and raceways","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#tableros","x":"03 Distribution boards and raceways Element Design criteria Main distribution board Main switch, RCD, one thermal-magnetic breaker per circuit, and separate neutral and ground terminal blocks. With spare room, and at an accessible height: between 1.4 and 1.8 m. Sub-distribution board When there is a remote area or a differentiated use —outdoor barbecue area, workshop, upper floor—. It is fed by an ATE circuit. Raceways Semi-heavy steel conduit or rigid PVC conduit embedded in the wall, cable tray or surface trunking in view. They are sized so that the conductors fill at most one third of the cross-section. Boxes Rectangular for switches and outlets, octagonal for ceiling points. Every splice goes inside an accessible box, never buried in the wall. Identification Each protection device labeled with the circuit it supplies, and the single-line diagram attached to the door of the board. What must never be done Splices without a box, twisted together and taped, inside the wall or the ceiling. Using the protective conductor as a neutral, or connecting neutral and ground downstream of the distribution board. Hanging the water heater or the air conditioner from a general-purpose outlet circuit. Connecting the grounding to a water pipe: today pipes are plastic and do not conduct, and even if they did conduct, it is not a valid ground electrode. Working without verifying the absence of voltage, even when “the breaker is already off.”"},{"t":"Low-current and extra-low-voltage systems","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#debiles","x":"04 Low-current and extra-low-voltage systems The design does not end with power. The same building also houses the data network, the intercom, the alarm, the doorbell, TV and home automation, all at extra-low voltage. Separation Low-current systems run in their own conduits. Sharing a conduit with power induces noise in the data and, above all, no longer guarantees insulation between systems. Provision An empty conduit with a pull wire to every room, and a space in the communications board. It is cheap during construction and impossible afterwards. SELV Safety extra-low voltage —up to 50 V AC— supplies doorbells, intercoms and control: it is not dangerous to touch, but it still demands neat work."},{"t":"Project documentation","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#documentacion","x":"05 Project documentation Document What it shows Floor plan The actual location of points, switches, outlets, boards and raceways on the architectural drawing, with standard symbols. Single-line diagram The complete board on a single line: main switch, RCD, each thermal-magnetic breaker with its circuit, cross-section and protection. Functional diagram How one particular circuit works: a staircase point, an automation, a two-way switching arrangement. Load schedule Circuit by circuit: number of points, expected power, simultaneity factor, current and voltage drop. Design calculations How each cross-section and each protection device was arrived at. It is what allows the design to be reviewed without redoing it. The drawing is made with the tools and symbols covered in technical drawing and CAD and component symbols."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Design your own home On each student’s own floor plan: count rooms and areas, determine the electrification grade, define the circuits with their points and put together the load schedule. Calculate the maximum simultaneous demand and check that the chosen grade covers it. Lab 2 · The single-line diagram Draw the complete single-line diagram of the designed board, with each protection device labeled, its rated current, the conductor cross-section and the circuit it supplies. Review it with peers looking for mistakes: it is the fastest way to learn to read them. Lab 3 · Building the board Physically assemble the designed board on a panel: DIN rail, main switch, RCD, thermal-magnetic breakers, neutral and ground terminal blocks, wire dressing and labeling. Verify continuity and the absence of crossed wires before energizing. Lab 4 · Verification On the practice installation: measure insulation resistance, verify the continuity of the protective conductor at every point, test the RCD with the test button and with an instrument, and measure the grounding resistance. Document everything in a test report."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence A single circuit for the whole house Any fault leaves everything without power, and the conductor works at its limit. Lighting and outlets mixed When the protection trips because of an appliance, you are left in the dark at the worst moment. More points than allowed per circuit The conductor is overloaded and the protection does not warn in time. Water heater or air conditioner on a TUG Permanent overload of the circuit and of the plug, which ends up heating and melting. No protective conductor to the point The RCD cannot act as expected and the casing of an appliance can become live. Unlabeled board Every later intervention is done blind, by trial: it is dangerous and slow. Data and power in the same conduit Noise in the data network and loss of separation between systems. No spare room in the board The first expansion forces replacing the entire board."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Residential electrical design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What defines the electrification grade of a dwelling? The floor area and the expected demand. The grade gives the minimum number of circuits: 2 for minimum, 3 for medium, 5 for high, and per design for superior. Why are lighting and outlets not mixed? Because a fault in an appliance would trip the protection and leave the dwelling in the dark, just when you need to see to solve the problem. What is the minimum cross-section of a lighting circuit and of an outlet circuit? 1.5 mm² for general-purpose lighting and 2.5 mm² for general-purpose outlets. What is the maximum simultaneous power demand? The sum of the expected loads multiplied by their simultaneity factors, because not all loads run at the same time. It is what is used to size the feeder and choose the grade. Which circuit corresponds to an air-conditioning unit? Its own ACU circuit, one per unit, with its protection sized according to the unit. It is never hung from a general-purpose outlet circuit. What color corresponds to the protective conductor, and what other use can it have? Green and yellow, and no other use. That color is reserved exclusively for the protective conductor. Where are splices made? Always inside an accessible box. A splice embedded in the wall or hidden in the ceiling is a fault waiting to happen, and impossible to find besides. What documents make up the design? Floor plan, single-line diagram, functional diagrams, load schedule and design calculations. Without them there is "},{"t":"The law and its body of rules","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#ley","x":"01 The law and its body of rules Provincial Law 10.281 on Electrical Safety requires every user electrical installation, public or private, inside a building or on the public road, to hold a Certificate of Suitable Electrical Installation in order to obtain service. It applies to new installations and also to those that predate the law when service is resumed. It is issued by a Licensed Electrical Installer and submitted to the distribution utility, which may not provide service without it. The law has been in force since 1 December 2017, the date set by ERSeP General Resolution (RG) 46/2017. ERSeP, the enforcement authority Defines the rules that apply to the installations covered. Creates and keeps the Register of Licensed Electrical Installers. Defines the conditions of the licences. Sets the offences and penalties regime. Sets the requirements of the Certificate of Suitable Electrical Installation. Administers the certificates that are issued. Law, decree, resolution, standard The law imposes the obligation; Regulatory Decree 1022/2015 implements it (categories, scope, form of the certificate); ERSeP resolutions specify how to comply; and the AEA 90364 regulation and IRAM standards say how it is built and what each material must meet. For a single-family dwelling the reference is AEA 90364-7-770 (up to 63 A, classifications BA2 and BD1). The hierarchy is detailed in regulations for electrical installations. The regulations have changed several times. Before 2023 the resol"},{"t":"Categories and the register","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#categorias","x":"02 Categories and the register The decree distinguishes three categories of Licensed Electrical Installer according to training. Category I and II installers are registered with ERSeP by their professional Boards; Category III installers, by ERSeP itself. Category Who How they register Scope I Professionals with a university degree Enrolled with their Board; the Board registers them. ERSeP updates the list every month. By default, the types of installation within their competence in the electrical specialty. II Technicians with a qualifying title Enrolled with their Board; the Board registers them. Same as Category I. III Skilled persons in electrical work, with accredited related training Directly with ERSeP (second part of the Register), after passing the assessment. Home installations and small commercial or industrial installations, in low voltage and up to 10 kW. The category is not a hierarchy of voltage or power RG 50/2017 (art. 4) clarifies that Categories I and II correspond exclusively to the level of the qualification: they do not indicate authorization or rank for designing or building works of a given voltage or power, nor do they alter the competences the Ministry of Education set for each title. A Category I installer does not \"do more\" for being I: they may do what their title allows. For Category III, RG 17/2021 (Annex I, second part) sets the path to registration: Topic What it establishes Who may not register (art. 2) Minors, persons disqualified, incapacit"},{"t":"What a Category III installer may certify","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#alcance","x":"03 What a Category III installer may certify To certify, an installation must pass three checks at the same time: be low voltage, not exceed 10 kW and be of a permitted type, with no special conditions. If one fails, it cannot be certified, even if the other two are fine. Power. RG 50/2017 (art. 5) limits certification to single-family dwellings and small commercial or industrial installations at low voltage with a maximum power of 10 kW, in line with the “AEA Guide – Electrical Installations in Buildings up to 10 kW” and excepting its annex on buildings that are not dwellings (premises of other characteristics). To calculate that power, the design power factor may not be lower than 0.85. Since project calculations give apparent power S in VA, it must be converted to active power P in kW; conversely, the largest apparent power that fits in 10 kW is: Type of premises and conditions. Installations with special treatment may not be worked on or certified by a Category III installer, even if within low voltage and 10 kW: those in explosive atmospheres, medical locations, places of public assembly (AEA 90364-7-718), and those with special conditions of use: capacity of persons BA3, evacuation conditions BD2, BD3 or BD4, nature of materials BE2, BE3 or BE4. Nor may those subject to extreme external influences (ambient temperature, atmospheric humidity, vibration, solar radiation). ERSeP's competence guide lists what is permitted and what is prohibited. What is permitted: Residentia"},{"t":"Administrative liability","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#administrativa","x":"04 Administrative liability Art. 5 of the law says that, through the certificate, the installer becomes responsible for compliance with the law, without prejudice to other responsibilities that may apply; ERSeP's responsibility is limited to setting the rules and their scope. Since the installer is considered a skilled person, prepared to build an optimal installation, a higher diligence is required than of an unskilled person: working without the care their technique demands brings greater responsibility. Offences and penalties are in RG 5/2016 with its amendments RG 97/2018 and RG 42/2025, in force throughout the Province. ERSeP applies them through its Misdemeanour Court. Penalties may be imposed alone or together, graded by seriousness: Warning: any offence without a more serious treatment. It is recorded in the file. Fine, in Fine Units (UM). Paid into the account ERSeP specifies within 5 working days of notification. Suspension from the Register for up to 6 months per offence: nothing covered by the law may be practised. Disqualification: permanent removal from the Register. The Fine Unit is worth 100 times the unit price of the kWh of the highest residential tariff in the tariff schedule of the utility where the offence was committed (if that cannot be determined, EPEC's). That is why fines update themselves with the tariff. Item Installer offence (art. 5) Before (RG 97/2018) In force (RG 42/2025) a.1 Repeating an offence already penalized with a warning. 40 to 60 UM 1"},{"t":"Civil and criminal liability","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#civil-penal","x":"05 Civil and criminal liability The administrative layer is only one of three. The same conduct may lead to an ERSeP penalty, an obligation to pay damages and criminal proceedings, each independent of the others. The civil and criminal ones are determined by the ordinary courts according to the case. Civil Art. 1757 of the Civil and Commercial Code establishes that whoever causes damage through the risk or defect of things, or through activities that are risky or dangerous by their nature, by the means used or by the circumstances, is liable, and that the liability is strict. Neither administrative authorization to carry out the activity nor compliance with prevention techniques exempts. Applied to the trade: being licensed by ERSeP and following the standard does not free you from answering for the damage; only proving the user's fault, that of a third party for whom one is not responsible, an unforeseeable event or force majeure cuts off the liability. Arts. 1724 and 1725 add the professional's yardstick: the skill and diligence corresponding to their title or licence are required. Hence the importance of acting with care, knowing Law 10.281 and its framework, and documenting everything. Criminal The Criminal Code has no “electrician's crimes”, but several offences can reach badly done work: Offence Article When it applies Example from the trade Negligent injury 94 Harm to body or health through recklessness, negligence, lack of skill or breach of regulations. Imprisonment "},{"t":"The trade as a profession","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#profesion","x":"06 The trade as a profession Before the law, the electrician was a person with an unregulated trade, working by the rules of good practice. Since 1 December 2017 the skilled-person trade has become a regulated profession. The law raises its standing and also exposes it: the best defence is to comply with legislation and the technical rules in force and to document every intervention. The rules of good practice AEA 90364 is the national reference standard for installations in buildings and is updated (for example, with a 2025 edition of part 6, on verification). It gives a common language —symbols and terminology— so that ERSeP, an insurer or an expert witness speak the same language as the installer, and it represents the state of the art: if it is followed, maximum diligence is presumed, which is the best defence against a malpractice claim. Consumer Protection Law (24.240) The installer is a professional supplier and the user a consumer. Hence: Obligation of result: finishing the work with diligence and probity. Duty to inform: clearly informing about the installation's risks and the improvements needed to comply with the standard, and checking that the service presents no danger (arts. 4, 5 and 6). It is also the installer's shield. Burden of proof: the supplier must produce the evidence it holds —the certificate—; if it does not, the judge may take the consumer's version as true. Certified materials, mandatory, protect the consumer from dangerous products. Defects and def"},{"t":"In the professional practice","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#practica","x":"07 In the professional practice Activity 1 · Classify twelve cases Material: the guide of permitted and prohibited installations (section 03) and the note on assimilation. Steps: classify each case as permitted or prohibited, noting the guide letter or the trade it is assimilated to. (1) Hot-dog stand on the pavement. (2) Ceramics factory. (3) Pub with live music. (4) Hardware store with paint. (5) Bakery with electric oven. (6) Neighbourhood primary school. (7) Hair salon. (8) Spa. (9) Print shop. (10) Dental office. (11) Temporary site supply for a house. (12) Temporary site supply for an apartment building. Acceptance: twelve answers with their justification; for 5, also the remark that the power remains to be verified. See the key (1) Permitted, assimilated to a kiosk. (2) Permitted, assimilated to a clay flowerpot factory. (3) Prohibited (c, entertainment). (4) Prohibited (d, flammables). (5) Permitted by trade (industrial), verify the 10 kW. (6) Prohibited (q). (7) Permitted (commercial). (8) Prohibited (k). (9) Permitted (industrial). (10) Prohibited (s). (11) Permitted (residential). (12) Prohibited (x). Activity 2 · Calculate the power and decide Steps: with pf = 0.85, convert the apparent power of four premises to kW and decide, for each, whether a Category III installer may certify it: hair salon of 5,800 VA; print shop of 12,600 VA; grocery store of 11,700 VA; spa of 6,000 VA. Acceptance: four calculations with units, the 11,765 VA ceiling taken into account and t"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Believing Category I “can do higher voltage” than II Categories reflect the title, not a hierarchy of voltage or power (RG 50/2017, art. 4). Checking only whether the trade is on the permitted list The low voltage, the 10 kW and the absence of public assembly or special conditions are still to be checked. Calculating kW with a power factor below 0.85, or confusing VA with W The power is underestimated and the certificate is issued outside competence. Declaring less power or a more benign trade to fit False certificate: offence a.3 and possible ideological forgery (art. 293). Signing a certificate someone else made, or without verifying Offence a.4: 15 to 150 UM, suspension or disqualification, and civil and criminal liability. Believing the licence and following the standard remove civil liability Liability for risky activity is strict and those are not exemptions (art. 1757). Not checking the CIDI mailbox or not reporting a data change within 2 months You are notified from the moment the notice is sent, and not updating data is an offence (a.2). Accepting materials without the IRAM mark or QR code The installation is not standardized and the good-practice defence is lost. Letting the licence lapse or renewing with pending penalties After 3 years you may not practise; with pending penalties it is not renewed."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The Licensed Electrical Installer: law, categories and liability","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalador-habilitado#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What does Law 10.281 require and who issues the certificate? It requires a Certificate of Suitable Electrical Installation to provide service, for new installations and for earlier ones when service is resumed. It is issued by a Licensed Electrical Installer and submitted to the utility. ERSeP is the enforcement authority. What do categories I and II indicate, and what does III? I and II correspond to the level of the title (degree and technician), registered by their Boards; they do not indicate a hierarchy of voltage or power (RG 50/2017, art. 4). III is for skilled persons with accredited training, registered by ERSeP, and covers single-family dwellings and small commercial or industrial installations at low voltage, up to 10 kW. A premises of a permitted trade has 11,000 VA. May a Category III installer certify it? With pf = 0.85: 9.35 kW ≤ 10 kW: yes on power (the ceiling is 11,765 VA). It remains to verify low voltage and that it is not a public-assembly venue and has no special conditions. A tyre shop and a car wash have the same power. Why can they not be treated alike? The tyre shop is permitted; the car wash is a wet premises (prohibited e). The trade and the conditions of the premises rule, not only the kW. With an assumed price of $120 per kWh, what is the UM worth and what is the minimum of offence a.3 if it is a repeat? The minimum of a.3 is 20 UM; with a repeat within 2 years it doubles to 40 UM, that is $480,000. A fire caused by a badly mad"},{"t":"Why study them when everything is digital today","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#por-que","x":"01 Why study them when everything is digital today Digital multimeters are more accurate, cheaper, and do not break if you connect them backwards. And yet the analog instrument remains in the curricula and in the workshop, for four specific reasons: The pointer shows trends. To adjust a trimmer to its maximum or minimum, or to see whether a voltage is fluctuating, a pointer beats a number that jumps around. The panel of industrial equipment is still analog: moving-iron ammeters and voltmeters in electrical switchboards, VU meters, process indicators. The concept of sensitivity and loading of an instrument is only truly understood once you see why an analog instrument disturbs the circuit. It is the basis of every pointer transducer: the moving-coil mechanism is the same one found in a speaker and in the motor of a hard drive."},{"t":"The moving-coil galvanometer","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#bobina-movil","x":"02 The moving-coil galvanometer Also called the D'Arsonval movement. It is the heart of every analog direct-current instrument. It works through three effects: Driving torque. The current flowing through the coil, immersed in the magnet's field, produces a force (Laplace's law, ) that tends to make it rotate. This torque is proportional to the current. Restoring torque. Two spiral springs oppose the rotation with a torque proportional to the angle. The pointer stops where the two are equal, that is, at an angle proportional to I. That is why the scale is linear. The springs also serve a second purpose: carrying the current to the coil. Damping. Without it the pointer would oscillate around the final value. It is provided by the coil's own aluminum frame: as it moves through the field, eddy currents are induced in it that brake the motion. The ideal setting is critical damping: the pointer arrives quickly and without bouncing. What defines a galvanometer Two figures, and everything else is calculated from them: I fsd ( full scale deflection ): the current that drives the pointer to full scale. Typical values: 50 µA, 100 µA, 1 mA. R m: winding resistance. Typically 1 kΩ to 2 kΩ in 50 µA movements. Sensitivity A 50 µA galvanometer has; a 1 mA one, barely 1000 Ω/V. The higher the sensitivity, the less it loads the circuit. Sensitivity is the most important specification of an analog instrument. The resistance a voltmeter presents on a given range is: a 20,000 Ω/V instrument on th"},{"t":"From galvanometer to ammeter: the shunt","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#amperimetro","x":"03 From galvanometer to ammeter: the shunt A 50 µA galvanometer burns out with any real current. To measure amperes, a very small resistor, the shunt, is placed in parallel with it, diverting almost all of the current. R m and I m are the galvanometer's figures; I is the current you want to read at full scale. Worked example · A 1 A ammeter A galvanometer with and. You want a 1 A ammeter at full scale. Voltage across the galvanometer at full scale:. Current the shunt must divert:.. Note two things: the shunt has an extremely low value (it is made from a piece of calibrated wire or from manganin) and the total drop across the ammeter is only 0.1 V, which is why it hardly disturbs the circuit. This value is called the burden voltage and appears in multimeter datasheets. Golden rule of the shunt In a multi-range ammeter, the shunt must never be opened while the instrument is connected: all of the current would flow through the coil and burn it out instantly. That is why multirange instruments use the Ayrton shunt, in which the selector switch distributes a chain of resistors without ever leaving the galvanometer on its own."},{"t":"From galvanometer to voltmeter: the multiplier resistor","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#voltimetro","x":"04 From galvanometer to voltmeter: the multiplier resistor To measure voltage, a large resistor is placed in series with the galvanometer, so that the voltage to be measured produces exactly I fsd. The total resistance of the voltmeter is, which is exactly. Worked example · A three-range voltmeter Same galvanometer (50 µA, 2 kΩ, ): Range 10 V 200 kΩ 198 kΩ 50 V 1 MΩ 998 kΩ 250 V 5 MΩ 4.998 MΩ With a 1 mA galvanometer ( ), the 10 V range would present barely 10 kΩ. Measuring with that instrument the base of a transistor biased with 47 kΩ resistors would completely ruin the measurement."},{"t":"The ohmmeter","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#ohmetro","x":"05 The ohmmeter It is the only one of the three that needs its own source: an internal battery. It measures indirectly, by injecting current and observing the deflection. The current is not proportional to R x but inversely proportional to a sum: hence the scale is non-linear. With the probes shorted: I is at its maximum, the pointer goes to full scale → 0 Ω on the right. With the probes open:, the pointer does not move → ∞ on the left. The midpoint of the scale corresponds to, and it is where the reading is most accurate. So it pays to choose the range (× 1, × 10, × 100, × 1 k) that leaves the pointer near the center. The zero adjustment Before each measurement, and every time you change range, you must touch the probes together and turn the “zero adjust” trimmer until the pointer reads exactly 0 Ω. This compensates for battery wear. If it does not reach zero with a new battery, the instrument has a fault; if the pointer never moves, the battery is dead."},{"t":"Moving-iron and electrodynamic instruments","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#otros-mecanismos","x":"06 Moving-iron and electrodynamic instruments The moving coil works only for direct current: with alternating current, the torque reverses direction 100 times per second (twice per cycle) and the pointer does not move (or just vibrates). Measuring AC directly requires other mechanisms. Mechanism Principle Works on Scale Typical use Moving coil Coil in the field of a permanent magnet DC Linear Analog multimeters, VU meters Moving iron Two pieces of iron inside a fixed coil repel each other, magnetized with the same sign DC and AC Square-law (cramped at the start) Electrical switchboards. Measures true RMS value Electrodynamic One fixed coil and one moving coil; the torque depends on the product of the two currents DC and AC Square-law or linear depending on use Wattmeter: measures real power, including the power factor Thermal A wire expands as it heats from the Joule effect DC and AC Square-law Radio frequency, RMS value at any frequency Why moving iron does measure AC Because the two pieces of iron are magnetized always with the same polarity, regardless of the direction of the current: they always repel. The torque turns out proportional to I 2, and since the average of I 2 is what defines the RMS value, the instrument directly indicates the true RMS value, even if the waveform is distorted. That is what a cheap digital multimeter does not do: those that do not say “True RMS” assume the wave is a sine wave and calculate the RMS value from the average value. How a moving-coi"},{"t":"Analog versus digital","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#comparacion","x":"07 Analog versus digital Analog Shows trends: ideal for adjusting a maximum or a minimum. Needs no battery to measure voltage and current. Limited sensitivity: it loads the circuit, and differently on each range. Error referred to full scale: poor in the first third. Damaged by reversed polarity or overload. You have to read by interpolating between marks, with a risk of parallax error. Digital High and constant input impedance (10 MΩ). Error referred to the reading: good across the whole range. Unambiguous reading, with automatic polarity. Many extra functions: capacitance, frequency, diode test, h FE, temperature. The digits “dance” if the signal is noisy: trends are hard to see. Needs a battery for everything. In the workshop the sensible thing is to have both, and know which to use: the digital for values, the analog for adjustments."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Lab 1 · Determining I fsd and R m of a galvanometer Using a 1.5 V battery, a 100 kΩ trimmer in series (starting at maximum ) and a reference microammeter: reduce the trimmer until you reach full scale and note the current. For R m, place a resistance decade box in parallel with the galvanometer and adjust it until the pointer falls to half scale: at that point the decade box equals exactly R m. Lab 2 · Building a voltmeter With the galvanometer characterized, calculate the multiplier resistor for a 10 V range and build it from series resistors (combining E12 values until you get close). Compare the instrument against a digital multimeter at several points: 2, 5, 8 and 10 V. Calculate the relative error at each point and check that it grows toward the start of the scale. Lab 3 · The effect of sensitivity Build a divider with two 470 kΩ resistors powered from 10 V (theoretical voltage at the midpoint: 5 V). Measure with the analog instrument you built and with the digital multimeter. The difference between the two readings is the loading effect, and it can be calculated in advance. Repeat with 4.7 kΩ resistors: now the two readings agree. Lab 4 · Ohmmeter and form factor (a) Check the ohmmeter's zero adjustment on each range and measure five known resistors, noting where on the scale the pointer falls and the resulting error. (b) If a function generator is available: measure with an analog multimeter (or a digital one without True RMS) a sine wave and a square wav"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The pointer pegs against the left stop Reversed polarity. The pointer can bend: disconnect immediately. The pointer pegs against the right stop Range too low. Always start with the highest. The reading changes depending on who reads it Parallax error. Good instruments have a mirror on the scale: you read when the pointer hides its own reflection. When measuring voltage, the circuit stops working Loading effect: the instrument has too little sensitivity for that high-impedance circuit. The ohmmeter does not reach zero with the probes together Dead battery, or the zero adjustment for that range is needed. Measuring 12 V reads 11 V on the 250 V range Class error referred to full scale. On a 250 V range of class 1.5 the error is ± 3.75 V. The pointer oscillates and does not settle Damping fault (damaged aluminum frame), or the measured signal really is varying."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Analog measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Why is the scale of a moving-coil instrument linear? Because the driving torque is proportional to the current and the restoring torque of the spring is proportional to the angle. When they balance, the angle turns out proportional to the current. The soft-iron core keeps the field radial, which is the condition for this to hold over the whole travel. A galvanometer of 100 µA and 1 kΩ. What shunt is needed to measure 500 mA? Voltage at full scale:. Current through the shunt:.. What is the sensitivity of that same galvanometer, and what resistance does it present on the 20 V range?. On the 20 V range it presents. Why is the ohmmeter zero on the right side of the scale? Because with the probes shorted (0 Ω) the maximum current flows and the pointer reaches full scale. The higher the resistance, the lower the current and the smaller the deflection: the scale ends up inverted and is also non-linear. A class 2.5 instrument with a 300 V full scale. What is the error when measuring 30 V? Absolute error:. On a 30 V reading that is a relative error of 25 %. You need to switch to a lower range. Why does a moving-iron instrument work on AC and a moving-coil one does not? Because in the moving-iron instrument the two pieces are always magnetized with the same polarity and repel each other regardless of the direction of the current: the torque does not reverse. In the moving coil, the torque changes direction with the current and its average on AC is zero. What does a m"},{"t":"What changed compared with the pointer instrument","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#analogico-digital","x":"01 What changed compared with the pointer instrument In analog instruments the measurement was a physical phenomenon: a current produced a torque on a coil and the pointer stayed where that torque balanced against the spring. Here the measurement is a number, and the whole instrument exists to produce it. Analog Digital Reading Interpreted by the operator on a scale Numeric, no interpretation Reading error Parallax, interpolation between marks None: the number is what it is Input impedance 20 kΩ/V typical: loads the circuit 10 MΩ constant on all ranges Varying signals The trend is visible on the pointer The digits dance and you cannot make sense of anything Zero adjustment Mechanical, and for the ohmmeter, every time Automatic, internal Range Always manual Autoranging Robustness The movement is damaged by a knock Electronics with no moving parts The one thing the analog does better Seeing how a quantity changes. Adjusting a trimmer to the maximum, looking for a peak, following the charging of a capacitor: the pointer shows it at a glance and the digits do not. That is why many good-quality digital multimeters have an analog bargraph below the number: it is exactly that function that was being lost."},{"t":"Architecture of a digital instrument","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#arquitectura","x":"02 Architecture of a digital instrument All digital instruments —multimeter, frequency counter, capacitance meter, scale— share the same skeleton. The first block changes, the one that translates the quantity into a voltage. Autoranging The control block looks at the result of each conversion. If the number exceeds full scale ( overflow: the screen shows a lone “1” or “OL”), it goes up a range; if it falls below 10%, it goes down, and that way it makes use of all the digits. It is convenient, but it has two costs: it takes time while it searches, and on a signal that changes a lot it can end up hopping between two ranges. That is why every decent multimeter has a RANGE key to lock it by hand, which is what you should use when you are adjusting something."},{"t":"The dual-slope converter","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#doble-rampa","x":"03 The dual-slope converter It is the heart of almost all multimeters. It was already mentioned in A/D and D/A converters; here it is seen in detail, because it explains where its three virtues come from: accuracy, immunity to mains noise and low cost. Neither R nor C appears in the result: they cancel between the two ramps. That is why the converter does not depend on the capacitor’s tolerance or its thermal drift. Why it is so accurate R and C cancel: only V ref and the clock matter. The clock cancels too, because both times are measured with the same one. A single critical standard remains: the voltage reference. Why it ignores 50 Hz The first ramp averages the input over t 1. If t 1 is an exact multiple of 20 ms, any mains interference contributes the same above and below zero: it cancels itself. This is what datasheets call NMR (normal mode rejection) and it gives 60 dB or more at 50 Hz. The downside: it is slow Between the two ramps and the auto-zero, a complete measurement takes from 10 to 400 ms. A typical multimeter refreshes two or three times per second, and that is its physical limit, not a limitation of the display. It is no use for fast-changing signals: for those you go to the oscilloscope."},{"t":"The digital multimeter: how to read its specification","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#multimetro","x":"04 The digital multimeter: how to read its specification Counts and digits A “3½-digit” multimeter has three full digits (0 to 9) and one that can only be 0 or 1: it goes up to 1999, which is 2000 counts. The “count” is the unit of resolution, and it defines what the last digit is worth on each range. Digits Counts Maximum reading Resolution on 20 V Instrument category 3½ 2000 1999 10 mV Pocket multimeter 3¾ 4000 3999 10 mV Workshop, mid-range 4½ 20,000 19,999 1 mV Bench, laboratory 6½ 2,000,000 1,999,999 10 µV Calibration standard Accuracy: not the same as resolution Resolution is how many numbers it can display; accuracy is how close they are to the true value. It is always specified with two terms: The first term grows with what is measured; the second is fixed and dominates on small readings. Example · What the display is really worth A 3½-digit multimeter, DC specification ±(0.5% + 3 digits). It measures on the 20 V range (resolution 10 mV) and the screen shows 12.45 V. 0.5% of 12.45 V 3 digits Total error: The true value lies between 12.36 V and 12.54 V. The screen shows hundredths, but the last reliable digit is the tenths one. Reporting “12.45 V” without the error is reporting too much. Now the same measurement on the 200 V range (resolution 100 mV): 0.5% of 12.4, plus, total ±0.36 V. Four times worse just for leaving the range high. Always the lowest range that does not overflow. The four traps of the digital multimeter Average-responding, RMS-calibrated On AC, almos"},{"t":"The digital frequency counter","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#frecuencimetro","x":"05 The digital frequency counter It is the purest case of a digital instrument: it converts nothing, it counts. And what it counts is exactly what you want to measure. Two errors add up: the ±1 count one, which matters at low frequencies, and the one from the timebase crystal, which matters at high ones. Example · The two errors, each in its own territory Frequency counter with a 1 s timebase and a 10 ppm crystal: Measuring 50 Hz:, the counting error is, and the crystal’s is 0.001%. Counting dominates. It is better to measure the period. Measuring 10 MHz:, the counting error is 0.00001%, and the crystal’s is, that is ±100 Hz. The crystal dominates. That is why laboratory frequency counters have a temperature-compensated oscillator (TCXO) or an oven-controlled one (OCXO), and why the good ones have an input for an external 10 MHz standard. Measuring period The roles are reversed: the signal opens the gate and pulses of the standard clock are counted. For 50 Hz with a 10 MHz clock, 200,000 counts get in: the ±1 count error drops to 0.0005%. Prescaler Above the speed of the counter, the signal is divided by 10, 64 or 256 before being counted and the result is compensated. It is what makes it possible to reach UHF. Reciprocal Modern instruments always measure the period of a whole number of cycles and calculate the frequency. It gives constant resolution in digits, regardless of the frequency. The input conditioner Before the counter there is a Schmitt trigger that converts any w"},{"t":"Other digital instruments in the shop","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#otros","x":"06 Other digital instruments in the shop Instrument What it measures and how What to look at Capacitance meter It charges the capacitor with a constant current and measures the time, or measures the frequency of an oscillator that includes it. Electrolytics have ±20% tolerance: an “odd” value can be normal. It does not measure series resistance (ESR), which is the most common fault. ESR meter It injects a signal of ~100 kHz and measures the resistive part. It is the instrument that really finds the dried-out electrolytics in a switching power supply, with the capacitor still on the circuit board. LCR meter It measures the magnitude and phase of the impedance at a chosen frequency. The test frequency matters: an inductor is not worth the same at 100 Hz as at 100 kHz. It is covered in impedance measurements. Digital oscilloscope It samples and stores; then it draws. Sampling rate, memory depth and real bandwidth. And aliasing, which on a digital instrument is a concrete danger. Clamp meter It measures the magnetic field around the conductor with a split core or a Hall sensor. Transformer-type ones only measure AC; Hall-effect ones also measure DC. You have to clamp around only one conductor. Data logger A digital instrument with memory and a clock. It turns a measurement into a time series: it is what lets you find the fault that shows up at three in the morning. The digital oscilloscope deserves a separate warning Its two specifications are confused all the time. The bandwidth"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Lab 1 · Resolution versus accuracy Measure the same battery with all the multimeters in the shop and note the readings. They will differ in the last digit or in two. Look up the specification in each manual and calculate the error interval of each one: you will see that all the intervals overlap and that none contradicts the others. It is the best way to understand that one more digit is not a truer value. Lab 2 · Burden voltage, measured Build an LED with its current-limiting resistor at 5 V. Measure the current on the mA range and note it. Then measure the voltage across the LED with and without the ammeter inserted. The difference is the instrument’s burden voltage. Repeat on the 10 A range and compare: the shunt is different. Lab 3 · 50 Hz rejection Measure a battery with the multimeter and note it. Then bring the test lead wire close to a mains cable (without touching it) and measure again: the reading barely changes, because the dual slope averages. Repeat the same with the oscilloscope on DC: there you do see the superimposed hum. Two instruments, two different responses to the same noise. Lab 4 · Frequency versus period Measure a 50 Hz signal from the generator with the frequency counter in frequency mode, 1 s gate, and note how much the last digit fluctuates. Switch to period mode and compare the stability. Then measure 1 MHz in both modes: there the advantage is reversed. Build a table with the four cases. Before measuring on a panel Check the category"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause Two multimeters give different values Normal if the difference falls within each one’s error. It is only a problem if the intervals do not overlap. The AC reading is 0 with a signal that exists Frequency outside the bandwidth, or input DC-coupled on a small signal riding on a large value. It measures voltage where nothing is connected Ghost voltage from capacitive coupling in long cables, and the 10 MΩ input does not discharge it. Use LoZ mode. The circuit stops working when the ammeter is inserted Burden voltage of the shunt. Change range or measure the drop across a known resistor. The measured resistance is higher than the resistor’s You are measuring with the circuit powered or with another component in parallel. And at low values, the resistance of the test leads: use REL. The display shows a lone “1” or “OL” Out of range, not a fault. Go up a range. The frequency counter jumps or reads double Noisy signal crossing the Schmitt threshold twice, or insufficient amplitude. The reading drifts over the months Drift of the voltage reference. The instrument needs calibration: it is a service with a traceable standard, not a trimmer you adjust by eye. With worn-out batteries the instrument measures wrong before warning Several models lose accuracy before showing the low-battery symbol. Replace them at any suspicious reading."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Digital measuring instruments","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment How many counts does a 4½-digit multimeter have and what is its maximum reading? 20,000 counts, and it shows up to 19,999. The “half digit” is the one on the left, which can only be 0 or 1. A multimeter of ±(0.8% + 2 dig) on the 200 V range shows 118.5 V. What is the error? The resolution on 200 V with 3½ digits is 0.1 V. So: 0.8% of 118.5, plus. Total error ±1.15 V: the true value lies between 117.4 and 119.7 V. Why does the capacitor’s tolerance not matter in a dual slope? Because the same capacitor is used to charge and to discharge. In the quotient the RC product cancels, and the result depends only on the voltage reference. What integration time is appropriate in a country with a 50 Hz mains, and why? A multiple of 20 ms (the mains period): 20, 40, 100 ms. That way the mains interference averages to zero during the first ramp. In a 60 Hz country the multiple is 16.67 ms; some instruments let you choose it. Why does measuring 50 Hz with a frequency counter in frequency mode give a poor result? Because with a 1 s gate 50 pulses are counted and the ±1 count uncertainty is 2%. Measuring the period with a 10 MHz standard clock, 200,000 pulses are counted and the error drops to 0.0005%. A 10 ppm crystal measuring 10 MHz: what is the error in hertz?, which on gives ±100 Hz. It is the accuracy floor of the instrument: no counting trick improves it. What does it mean for a multimeter to be CAT III 600 V? That it is designed for measuring in fixed installations "},{"t":"Oils and greases for machines","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#lubricantes","x":"01 Oils and greases for machines A lubricant keeps two moving surfaces apart with a film that shears easily, so that friction and wear are minimal. It also cools, protects against corrosion and, in greases, seals out dust. An electrical workshop deals with two families: oil (a liquid, for gearboxes, sleeve bearings and hydraulic circuits) and grease (an oil held in a thickener, for rolling bearings, that does not run off). Oils: ISO VG viscosity The property that decides the oil is viscosity, the resistance to flow. It is measured as kinematic viscosity, in mm²/s (equal to cSt), and it drops when the oil heats up. The ISO VG grade (ISO 3448) is the midpoint of the viscosity at 40 °C, with a ±10 % tolerance: an ISO VG 68 has between 61.2 and 74.8 mm²/s at 40 °C. The higher the number, the thicker the oil. An oil that is too thin cannot hold the film under load; one that is too thick wastes power, heats up and takes too long to reach the surfaces on a cold start. The viscosity index tells how much the viscosity changes with temperature: the higher, the more stable. ISO VG grade mm²/s at 40 °C Typical uses 32 – 46 28.8 – 50.6 Hydraulic circuits, compressors, sleeve bearings of fast motors and generators. 68 61.2 – 74.8 Sleeve bearings of medium machines, pumps, light gearboxes. 100 – 150 90 – 165 Gearboxes under moderate load, slow bearings. 220 – 320 198 – 352 Industrial gearboxes under heavy load. The table gives an order of magnitude: the equipment manual always rules, giving"},{"t":"Dielectric oil and fuels in the workshop","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#dielectrico","x":"02 Dielectric oil and fuels in the workshop Transformer oil An oil-immersed transformer ( Transformers ) uses the oil for two things: to insulate (it fills the gaps between coils and core, where air would break down at a much lower voltage) and to cool (hot oil rises, gives up heat to the radiator and sinks again). The usual oil is mineral, refined from petroleum and specified by IEC 60296. Its properties are monitored because oil degrades: it oxidizes, takes up moisture and particles, and all of that lowers its insulation. The most important property is dielectric strength: the voltage at which the oil breaks down, measured between two electrodes a fixed distance apart. And the main enemy is water: a few tens of parts per million (ppm) dissolved already reduce it a lot, because droplets and particles form conducting bridges between the electrodes. That is why transformers have a silica gel breather, which dries the air drawn in when the oil cools and changes colour when saturated: that is the signal to replace it. What is tested and how the sample is taken Test What it says about the oil Breakdown voltage (IEC 60156) Dielectric strength: how clean and dry the oil is. Done in the workshop with a portable tester. Water content (Karl Fischer) Moisture in ppm. The laboratory test that explains a low strength. Acidity number Degree of oxidation: acids attack the paper insulation and metal parts. Dielectric dissipation factor (tan δ) Contamination and ageing: how much is dissipate"},{"t":"Solders and joints","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#soldaduras","x":"03 Solders and joints An electrical joint must conduct like the cable, withstand the mechanical load and not work loose with heating cycles. Depending on how it is made, the joint can be soldered (a filler alloy flows between the pieces), exothermic (copper is melted in place) or mechanical (a terminal is deformed onto the conductor). The boundary between soft and hard soldering is the melting temperature of the filler alloy: 450 °C. Soft solder: tin Alloy Melting approx. Where it is used Tin-lead (Sn63Pb37, Sn60Pb40) 183 – 190 °C Electronics and low-power splices. Easy to work; lead is toxic and restricted in many products. Tin-silver-copper (SAC305, 96.5 % Sn) 217 – 220 °C Lead-free electronics (RoHS). Requires a hotter iron. Tin-silver (Sn96.5Ag3.5) 221 °C Power joints that run warm and silver-plated parts: resists thermal fatigue better. With 2 % silver and lead (Sn62Pb36Ag2, 179 °C), silver-plated contacts are soldered without the silver dissolving. Tin-antimony (Sn95Sb5) 235 – 240 °C Joints that run hotter: power copper conductors, windings, refrigeration installations. Tin only wets clean metal: the flux (the rosin in the core of the solder, or a paste) dissolves the oxide and lets the molten metal through. In electrical and electronic work rosin flux is used; acid fluxes («soldering water», zinc chloride) are corrosive and are left to plumbing: they leave a residue that keeps eating the copper. A good solder joint is bright and concave, with the metal «wetting» both s"},{"t":"Plastics, insulation, varnishes, tapes and adhesives","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#aislantes","x":"04 Plastics, insulation, varnishes, tapes and adhesives The basic concepts of insulators (dielectric strength, insulation resistance, thermoplastics and thermosets) are in Electrical materials. Here we look at the actual materials that are bought and their temperature limits. Cable plastics and insulation Material Conductor temperature Characteristics and use PVC (polyvinyl chloride) 70 °C continuous · 160 °C in short circuit The most common insulation of installation cables. Cheap and flexible; it softens with heat and, when burning, releases corrosive gases. PE (polyethylene) 70 °C continuous Excellent insulator with low water absorption; used in coaxial and communication cables. XLPE (cross-linked polyethylene) 90 °C continuous · 250 °C in short circuit The chains are linked by cross-linking: more temperature and more current than PVC with the same section. Power cables. Halogen-free (LSZH) depends on the compound Low smoke emission and no corrosive gases when burning. For places where smoke is critical: tunnels, places of public assembly. Silicone up to about 180 °C Flexible from −60 °C to 180 °C. Oven and heating cables, sleeving and tapes. Beware of silicone vapours near contacts. PTFE (Teflon) up to about 260 °C Very good insulation and chemical resistance; high-temperature cables. Resins are two-component liquids that harden by chemical reaction: epoxy (adhesion and rigidity, encapsulation of dry-type transformers and coils), unsaturated polyester (winding impregnatio"},{"t":"Winding materials and new materials","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#bobinado","x":"05 Winding materials and new materials The calculation and technique of winding are in Motor winding. Here we describe the materials bought to do it. Magnet wire The conductor of the winding is enamelled copper wire (magnet wire): electrolytic copper coated with a thin layer of insulating enamel, which provides the insulation between turns. It is chosen by three data: the copper diameter (in mm), the enamel type, which sets the thermal class (polyester, 155 °C; polyesterimide, 180 °C; polyamide-imide, 200 °C) and the grade, the thickness of the coat (grade 1, 2 or 3: the higher, the more enamel). There are also wires with an adhesive overcoat that bonds the turns when heated. If the motor will run on a variable-frequency drive ( Variable-speed drives ), a wire with partial-discharge-resistant enamel is advisable, because the drive's pulses punish the insulation more than a sine voltage does. The section needed by the conductor comes from the current and the admissible current density J, which in ventilated machines is of the order of 4 to 7 A/mm²: S: copper cross-section in mm²; I: current in A; J: density in A/mm²; d: diameter in mm. Diameter (mm) Section (mm²) Current at 5 A/mm² 0.50 0.196 0.98 A 0.80 0.503 2.51 A 1.00 0.785 3.93 A 1.25 1.227 6.14 A 1.60 2.011 10.05 A Example · Choosing the wire for a current of 4.2 A With J = 5 A/mm², the section is S = 4.2 / 5 = 0.84 mm² and the diameter, d = √(4 · 0.84 / π) = 1.03 mm. A larger commercial diameter is taken, for example 1."},{"t":"Safety: data sheets, storage and waste","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#seguridad","x":"06 Safety: data sheets, storage and waste The safety data sheet Every chemical product comes with its safety data sheet (SDS; in Spanish HDS), which the manufacturer or supplier provides and which must be at hand where the product is used. In Argentina products are classified and labelled under the Globally Harmonized System (GHS), adopted for the workplace by Resolution SRT 801/2015, with red-diamond pictograms (flame, skull, corrosion, exclamation mark, health hazard, environment). The sheet has 16 sections; the ones that matter in the workshop are: Section What is found out 2 Hazards of the product: pictograms, hazard statements. 4 and 5 First aid and what to do in case of fire (with which extinguisher). 7 Safe handling and storage. 8 Personal protection needed: gloves (of what material), goggles, mask, ventilation. 9 Properties: flash point, state, odour. 10 What it is incompatible with and what not to mix. 13 How to dispose of the product and the container. Law 19.587 and Decree 351/79 require the employer to inform workers about the risks of what they handle and to provide personal protective equipment; the general framework is in Occupational health and safety. Storage Original container, labelled and closed; if decanted, the new container carries the same label. Segregate by incompatibility: flammables (solvents, petrol) away from oxidizers and any heat source; acids away from bases; epoxy hardeners (amines), which are corrosive, away from the rest. With a containment"},{"t":"In the workshop","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#taller","x":"07 In the workshop Practice 1 · Calibrate the grease gun and grease a bearing Materials: three-phase motor stopped and locked out, grease gun, the grease specified for the motor, 0.1 g scale, clean rag, infrared thermometer, worksheet. Steps: read the plate and the bearing designation (for example, 6309); calculate G = 0.005 · D · B; calibrate the gun (grams per stroke, average of ten strokes); wipe the nipple; apply the amount in slow strokes; start the motor and measure the end-cap temperature every 15 minutes for two hours, with the drain open for the first of them. Acceptance criterion: quantity within ±20 % of the calculated value, no excess grease expelled at the end, and an end-cap temperature that levels off (rising no more than 10 °C from the initial one). Practice 2 · Dielectric strength of an oil sample Materials: oil tester with a 2.5 mm cup (with the teacher), sample in a clean bottle, lint-free cloth, worksheet, gloves. Steps: rinse the cup with the same sample; fill it carefully without bubbles and wait a few minutes; with the safety lid closed, make six breakdowns with the rest the equipment calls for; record each value; calculate the average; classify the oil with the criterion in section 02; repeat with a sample to which a few drops of water have been added. Acceptance criterion: six values recorded, average calculated to one decimal, a written decision (in service, monitor or treat) and a check that water lowers the breakdown voltage. Practice 3 · Three joi"},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Mistake Consequence Greasing «until grease comes out» or with far too much The grease is churned and the bearing overheats; the grease degrades and the bearing life is shortened. Mixing greases with different thickeners Incompatibility: the grease softens or hardens and the bearing fails. Using a «similar» oil instead of the one the manual calls for Insufficient film or power loss from excessive viscosity. Leaving the transformer's silica gel saturated The oil absorbs moisture and the dielectric strength drops; the risk is an internal breakdown. Taking the oil sample in a plastic or damp bottle The result does not represent the oil, and decisions are made on false data. Cleaning contacts or coils with petrol Fire risk, and the solvent damages varnishes and enamels. Tinning the ends of a flexible cable to put it in a screw terminal The solder flows under pressure and the terminal works loose: hot spot. Using acid flux in an electrical installation The residue corrodes the copper of the joint over time. Acid-cure silicone in a switchboard Acetic acid corrodes copper and contacts. Baking a rotor with rare-earth magnets Loss of magnetization above the maximum temperature of the grade. Using a varnish or paper of a lower thermal class than the winding The insulation ages sooner and the repair lasts half as long as planned. Storing everything together, with no data sheets or containment Reactions between incompatible products, spills that reach the drain, and nob"},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electrical workshop supplies","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/insumos-electricos#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What does «ISO VG 68» mean and at what temperature is it measured? It is the viscosity grade of an oil according to ISO 3448: its midpoint kinematic viscosity is 68 mm²/s at 40 °C, within ±10 % (between 61.2 and 74.8). The higher the number, the thicker the oil. Calculate the grease relubrication quantity for a 6312 bearing (60 × 130 × 31 mm). G = 0.005 · D · B = 0.005 · 130 · 31 = 20.2 g. It is applied in slow strokes, with the calibrated gun, and the temperature is checked afterwards. If the motor has a lubrication plate, the plate rules. Six oil breakdowns give 38, 41, 36, 40, 39 and 38 kV. What is reported and what is decided? The average is (38 + 41 + 36 + 40 + 39 + 38) / 6 = 232 / 6 = 38.7 kV. It is between 30 and 40 kV: the oil is still usable, but it must be monitored and the test repeated more often, without waiting for it to fall below 30 kV. What is the difference between soft and hard soldering, and which would you use to join copper to copper without flux? Soft solder melts below 450 °C (tin alloys) and hard solder above. For copper to copper without flux, the copper-phosphorus alloy is used, because phosphorus reduces copper oxide. For brass, bronze or steel, silver and flux are needed. Why is exothermic welding preferred in a buried earthing system? Because it forms a continuous copper joint that neither loosens nor corrodes, with the conductor's section and the ability to carry the fault current. In a buried installation it cannot be retight"},{"t":"What exactly happens","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#mecanismo","x":"01 What exactly happens Step What happens 1 The peripheral detects the event and sets its request flag. 2 If that interrupt is enabled and the global interrupts are too, the core finishes the current instruction. 3 It saves the return address and the processor state on the stack: this is the context. 4 It looks up the address of the interrupt service routine in the vector table and jumps there. 5 The routine runs. In many families you have to clear the flag by hand. 6 The return instruction restores the context and the program carries on as if nothing had happened. Latency It is the time between the event and the first instruction of the routine. It is made up of what remains to finish the current instruction, saving the context and the jump: typically between 4 and 20 cycles. At 16 MHz that is on the order of a microsecond. But the real latency can be vastly longer if at that moment another interrupt service routine is running, or a critical section with interrupts disabled. That is the reason why routines must be short."},{"t":"Where they come from","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#fuentes","x":"02 Where they come from Source What it is used for External, on a pin A pushbutton, a limit switch, a sensor. Configurable for rising edge, falling edge, both, or level. Pin change on a port A single vector for a whole group of pins: the routine has to find out which one changed. Timer Overflow or compare: it is the basis of every periodic task and of the system timebases. Serial communication Byte received or transmit register empty. It avoids losing data without having to keep watching. A/D converter Conversion complete: the program does not wait, it gets notified. Input capture Stores the exact value of the timer when an edge occurs. It is the precise way to measure periods and pulse widths. Watchdog If the program hangs and does not refresh it, it resets the device."},{"t":"Priorities and nesting","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#prioridades","x":"03 Priorities and nesting How ties are resolved In simple 8-bit microcontrollers, priority is given by the position in the vector table: it is not configurable, and there is no nesting unless you enable it by hand. On ARM Cortex-M, the interrupt controller lets you assign a numeric priority to each source, and nesting is automatic: lower number, higher priority. Nesting gives a better response to urgent events, but it uses more stack and makes it much harder to reason about the program. How they are assigned Highest: safety. Emergency stop, overcurrent, power failure. High: whatever is lost if it arrives late. Edge capture, high-speed serial reception. Medium: timebases and periodic control. Low: user interface, logging, non-critical communication."},{"t":"The problem with shared variables","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#compartidas","x":"04 The problem with shared variables An interrupt can occur between any two instructions of the main program. Everything that both of them touch is a potential source of errors that show up once in a thousand times, and only with the equipment in production. /* BAD: the compiler may keep 'listo' in a register and never reread it */ uint8_t listo = 0; ISR (INT0_vect) { listo = 1; } while (!listo) { } /* may hang forever */ /* GOOD: volatile forces it to be read from memory on every pass */ volatile uint8_t listo = 0; /* BAD: 'pulsos' is 32 bits; on an 8-bit micro it is read in four steps */ volatile uint32_t pulsos; total = pulsos; /* if the interrupt falls in the middle, an impossible value comes out */ /* GOOD: critical section, as short as possible */ cli (); /* disables interrupts */ total = pulsos; sei (); /* enables them again */ The two rules that prevent 90% of the problems Every variable shared between an interrupt routine and the main program is declared volatile. Every access to a variable wider than the processor’s word size is done inside a critical section, as short as possible. And a third one, more important than the other two: the interrupt service routine does the bare minimum. It sets a flag, stores a piece of data, and the heavy work is done by the main program. What never goes inside an interrupt service routine Waits and delays. They block the whole system. Writing to the serial port or to a display. These are slow operations. Floating-point calculations "},{"t":"The pattern worth using every time","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#patron","x":"05 The pattern worth using every time /* Pushbutton debouncing by interrupt + timer */ volatile uint8_t evento = 0; volatile uint16_t t_ultimo = 0; ISR (INT0_vect) /* falling edge on the pushbutton */ { uint16_t ahora = ms_actual (); if (( uint16_t )(ahora - t_ultimo) > 30 ) { /* 30 ms of bounce */ evento = 1; t_ultimo = ahora; } } /* the routine finishes in microseconds */ int main ( void ) { inicializar (); while ( 1 ) { if (evento) { cli (); evento = 0; sei (); /* the event is consumed without losing another one */ atender_pulsador (); /* here it is fine to take a while */ } otras_tareas (); } } Why this pattern works The routine is short and deterministic, the debouncing does not burn waiting time, and the heavy work happens in the main loop, where it can take as long as it needs without affecting anyone. The detail of the subtraction with an explicit cast keeps the count correct when the millisecond counter wraps around, which is the bug that shows up after 65 seconds of operation and baffles everybody."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Lab 1 · Polling versus interrupt Detect a pushbutton in two ways: by polling in the main loop and by interrupt, with the program also doing a long task. Use the oscilloscope to measure the time between the edge and the response in each case, and compare the worst case of the two. Lab 2 · Measuring the latency Set a pin high as the first instruction of the interrupt service routine and observe on the oscilloscope the distance to the edge that triggered it. Repeat with another interrupt active and with a long critical section in the main program: you can see the latency grow. Lab 3 · Reproducing the volatile bug Write the wait loop without volatile and compile with optimization enabled. Verify that it hangs. Look at the assembly listing to see that the variable is not even reread. Add volatile and check the difference. It is the best way to make sure you never forget it again. Lab 4 · Measuring frequency by capture Use the input capture interrupt to measure the period of a signal, and compare the accuracy with measuring it by polling. With capture, the timer value is taken by hardware at the instant of the edge: the difference in accuracy is remarkable."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Symptom Usual cause The interrupt fires nonstop The request flag is not cleared inside the routine. The interrupt never fires It has not been enabled individually, or the global interrupts have not been enabled, or the vector name is misspelled. The program hangs in a wait loop The shared variable is not declared volatile. A multi-byte counter gives absurd values It is read without a critical section and the interrupt lands in the middle of the read. A pushbutton generates several events Mechanical bounce. Filter it by time, not by adding waits inside the routine. The system becomes erratic when interrupts are added Stack overflow caused by nesting, or routines that are too long. It fails at exactly 65 seconds A 16-bit millisecond counter overflows and is compared incorrectly. Everything stops for moments There is a wait, a serial write or a heavy calculation inside an interrupt service routine."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Interrupts","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does the processor save when it services an interrupt? The return address and the processor state —the context— on the stack, so it can continue exactly where it was when the routine finishes. What is the vector table? A table in memory where each interrupt source has the address of its interrupt service routine. The core consults it to know where to jump. What is latency and what does it depend on? The time between the event and the first instruction of the routine. It depends on the current instruction, on saving the context and, above all, on whether another routine is running or a critical section is open. Why must a shared variable be declared volatile? Because without that keyword the compiler may keep it in a register and not read it from memory again. Since the interrupt modifies it “from outside,” the main program never finds out about the change. What is a critical section and when is it needed? A stretch of code with interrupts disabled. It is needed when reading or writing variables wider than the processor’s word size, so that the interrupt does not land in the middle of the operation. Name three things that must not go inside an interrupt service routine. Waits or delays, slow writes such as the serial port or a display, and heavy calculations or dynamic memory allocation. How is pushbutton bounce handled with interrupts? By comparing the current instant with that of the last valid event and discarding the ones that arrive within about 20"},{"t":"Why C in an electronics technician program","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#por-que-c","x":"01 Why C in an electronics technician program There are more comfortable languages, but none combines these four things at once: Direct access to the hardware. It lets you write a value into a microcontroller register or read an input port exactly as described in the datasheet. Efficiency. The generated code is almost as compact and fast as code written in assembly, and that matters when you have 2 KB of memory. Portability. The same program, with minimal adjustments, compiles for a PIC, an AVR, an ARM or a PC. Universality. Every microcontroller manufacturer supplies its own C compiler. It is the common denominator of the industry. C is a compiled language The source program (.c ) goes through the preprocessor, then through the compiler, which translates it to object code (.o ), and finally through the linker ( link editor ), which joins the objects and the libraries and produces the executable. On a microcontroller that result is a.hex file that is written to flash memory. Understanding this chain is what makes it possible to interpret error messages."},{"t":"Structure of a program","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#estructura","x":"02 Structure of a program #include <stdio.h> // preprocessor directives #define PI 3.14159 // symbolic constant float area ( float radio); // function prototype int main ( void ) // EVERY program has a main { float r = 2.5; // declaration with initialization printf ( \"Area = %.2f\\n\", area (r)); return 0; // 0 = finished without error } float area ( float radio) // function definition { return PI * radio * radio; } Element What it is for #include Brings in a header file with ready-made declarations. With <> it searches the system libraries; with \"\", the project directory. #define Defines a constant or macro that the preprocessor replaces textually before compiling. Prototype Tells the compiler a function’s name, what it receives and what it returns, before its code appears. main() Entry point. On a microcontroller it never ends: its body is usually a while(1). { } Delimit blocks. Everything declared inside a block exists only there.; Ends each statement. Forgetting it is the number one compilation error. C is case-sensitive contador, Contador and CONTADOR are three different variables. By convention, lowercase is used for variables and functions, and UPPERCASE for constants defined with #define."},{"t":"Variables, types and constants","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#tipos","x":"03 Variables, types and constants Every variable must be declared before it is used, stating its type. That type determines how much memory it takes up and what values it can hold. Type Typical size (8-bit microcontroller) Range Use char 1 byte −128 to 127 A character, or a small signed integer. unsigned char 1 byte 0 to 255 The most widely used type in microcontrollers: one port, one byte. int 2 bytes −32,768 to 32,767 General-purpose integers. Careful: on a PC they are 4 bytes. unsigned int 2 bytes 0 to 65,535 Counters, values from a 10-bit ADC. long 4 bytes ± 2,147,483,648 Elapsed milliseconds, frequencies. float 4 bytes ± 3.4 × 10 38, 7 digits Calculations with decimals. Expensive on a micro with no floating-point unit. double 8 bytes 15 digits Double precision. Rarely justifiable in embedded systems. void — — “Nothing.” A function that returns no value or takes no parameters. The size of int depends on the machine The C standard only guarantees minimums, not exact sizes. That is why in embedded programming the types from <stdint.h> are used, which are unambiguous: uint8_t, int16_t, uint32_t. The number is the count of bits and the u means unsigned. It is the recommended professional practice and avoids errors when porting code. Overflow unsigned char c = 250; c = c + 10; // NOT 260: it gives 4, because 260 does not fit in 8 bits unsigned char i; for (i = 0; i < 300; i++) {... } // INFINITE LOOP: i never reaches 300 Overflow does not generate any error: the value simply “"},{"t":"Operators","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#operadores","x":"04 Operators Arithmetic and assignment + − * / % · ++ -- · = += -= *= /= The % is the remainder of integer division. / between integers truncates: 7/2 gives 3, not 3.5. Relational and logical == != < > <= >= · && || ! In C, 0 is false and any other value is true. Bitwise operators: the essential ones These are what make C suitable for electronics. They let you manipulate individual bits of a register without touching the others. Operator Function Typical use & Bitwise AND Setting bits to 0 (masking) and testing a bit. | Bitwise OR Setting bits to 1. ^ Bitwise XOR Inverting bits (toggle). ~ Complement Inverting all the bits. << Shift left Building masks. Equivalent to multiplying by 2. >> Shift right Extracting fields. Equivalent to dividing by 2. // The four operations used all the time with registers: PORTB |= ( 1 << 3 ); // set bit 3 to 1, without touching the others PORTB &= ~( 1 << 3 ); // set bit 3 to 0 PORTB ^= ( 1 << 3 ); // toggle bit 3 if (PINB & ( 1 << 3 )) { // is bit 3 set to 1? // yes: the pushbutton is pressed } Why you do not write PORTB = 8; Because that would set bit 3 to 1 and all the others to 0, switching off the rest of the port’s outputs. The forms with |= and &= ~ modify only the bit you care about. It is the difference between a program that works and one that shuts off the motor every time it turns on the LED. The most common error of all: = versus == if (x = 5 ) // ASSIGNS 5 to x, and since 5 is not 0, it is ALWAYS true if (x == 5 ) // COMPARES x wit"},{"t":"Flow control","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#control","x":"05 Flow control Decision if (temp > 30 ) { encender_ventilador (); } else if (temp < 18 ) { encender_estufa (); } else { apagar_todo (); } switch (tecla) { case 1: subir (); break; case 2: bajar (); break; default: parar (); } Repetition for (i = 0; i < 8; i++) { encender_led (i); } while (! dato_listo ()) { // wait } do { v = leer_adc (); } while (v < umbral); while ( 1 ) {... } // main loop Forgetting the break in a switch Without break, execution keeps falling through into the next case. Sometimes this is done on purpose (to group cases), but it is almost always an oversight and produces baffling behavior. The main loop of an embedded system int main ( void ) { configurar_puertos (); // runs ONCE configurar_adc (); configurar_timers (); while ( 1 ) { // repeats FOREVER leer_sensores (); procesar (); actualizar_salidas (); } return 0; // never reached } This structure — configuration + infinite loop — is that of every microcontroller program, and it is literally what the setup() and loop() functions of Arduino do, which is C underneath."},{"t":"Arrays and strings","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#arreglos","x":"06 Arrays and strings An array is a set of elements of the same type, contiguous in memory, accessed by an index that starts at 0. int muestras[ 10 ]; // valid indices: 0 to 9 int tabla[ 5 ] = { 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 }; muestras[ 0 ] = 512; // first element muestras[ 9 ] = 128; // LAST element muestras[ 10 ] = 0; // OUT OF RANGE! Overwrites memory that is not yours // Average 10 samples from the ADC long suma = 0; for ( int i = 0; i < 10; i++) { muestras[i] = leer_adc (); suma += muestras[i]; } int promedio = suma / 10; C does not check the bounds of an array Writing muestras[10] in an array of 10 elements gives no error: it writes to the next memory location, which may belong to another variable, to the stack or to a register. The program keeps running and fails later in an inexplicable way. On a PC this is a classic security hole ( buffer overflow ); on a microcontroller, a random hang. Checking the indices is the programmer’s responsibility. Character strings In C a string is simply an array of char terminated by the null character '\\0'. It is not a special type. char nombre[ 20 ] = \"Electronics\"; // takes 11 letters + 1 for the '\\0' = 12 #include <string.h> strlen (nombre); // 11 — does not count the '\\0' strcpy (destino, origen); // copy strcmp (a, b); // compare: returns 0 if they are equal strcat (destino, origen); // concatenate You must reserve room for the '\\0' An array of 20 char holds at most a string of 19 characters. And strcmp(a, b) returns 0 when they are equal, whic"},{"t":"Functions","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#funciones","x":"07 Functions Splitting the program into functions is not cosmetic: it is what lets you test each part separately, reuse code and understand a thousand-line program. // return_type name(type param1, type param2) float celsius_a_fahrenheit ( float c) { return c * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0; } void parpadear ( unsigned char veces) // void: returns nothing { for ( unsigned char i = 0; i < veces; i++) { PORTB |= ( 1 << 0 ); retardo_ms ( 200 ); PORTB &= ~( 1 << 0 ); retardo_ms ( 200 ); } } In C, parameters are passed by value The function receives a copy of the argument. If it modifies it, the original does not change. For a function to be able to modify a variable of its caller you have to pass its address, with pointers — which is the central subject of the next topic. Scope of variables Kind Where it lives When to use it Local Declared inside a function. Born on entry and dies on exit. Whenever possible. It is the safest and saves memory. Global Outside every function. Exists during the whole program. Only for data shared by several parts, typically between the main loop and an interrupt. Static static inside a function: keeps its value between calls. Internal counters that must not be visible from outside. Volatile volatile: tells the compiler that it can change outside the program flow. Mandatory for variables modified by an interrupt or mapped to a hardware register. Without volatile, the optimizer breaks the program If a global variable is modified inside an interrupt and the main loo"},{"t":"The preprocessor and the standard library","d":"Basic elements of the C language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c#preprocesador","x":"08 The preprocessor and the standard library The preprocessor acts before the compiler and performs text replacements. It knows nothing about types or C syntax. #include <stdio.h> // paste the contents of that file here #define LED_ON PORTB |= 1 #define MAX(a,b) ((a) > (b) ? (a): (b)) // macro with parameters #ifdef DEBUG // conditional compilation printf ( \"value = %d\\n\", x); #endif #ifndef CONFIG_H // include guard: avoids including #define CONFIG_H // the same file twice... #endif Parentheses in macros are not optional With #define DOBLE(x) x*2, the expression DOBLE(3+1) expands to 3+1*2 = 5, not 8. That is why every macro is written wrapping each parameter and the whole expression in parentheses: #define DOBLE(x) ((x)*2). When possible, an inline function is preferable, since it does respect types. The libraries in use Header Contains Note for embedded systems <stdio.h> printf, scanf, files printf takes up several KB: on a small micro it is replaced by a custom function that sends over serial. <stdlib.h> atoi, rand, malloc Avoid malloc in embedded systems: it fragments memory and can fail without warning. <string.h> String and memory handling memset and memcpy are very useful with buffers. <math.h> sin, sqrt, pow Very heavy. If it is enough, use integer arithmetic or precomputed tables. <stdint.h> uint8_t, int16_t, … Always use in microcontroller programming. <stdbool.h> bool, true, false Improves readability at no cost."}]);
